window.SCIENCE_CURRICULUM_9 = [{"id":"exploration9","index":0,"name":"Entering Secondary Science","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 1","emoji":"🔭","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"ex1","title":"Building Models of Reality","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The gates of Secondary Science swing open, and the world beyond looks impossibly tangled -- every star, cell and falling stone at once. A wise explorer hands you a magnifying glass: 'Do not study everything. Study what matters, and ignore the rest on purpose.'","teach":[{"title":"Why science uses models","body":"The natural world is far too complex to study in full detail, so science builds models: simplified versions of real systems that focus only on what matters for a given question. A moving car might be treated as a single point, atoms as spheres, or the Earth as a smooth layered sphere.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Ignoring on purpose","body":"Building a model means deliberately choosing what to leave out. When studying a falling object, air resistance may be ignored to see gravity's basic effect clearly. These choices are not mistakes -- they keep the model simple enough to still answer the question.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Example: the cricket six","body":"To model whether a hit cricket ball clears the boundary, the mass of the ball and the speed and direction it was hit matter a great deal. The bat's brand, the ball's colour, and the grass on the field make no real difference and can be safely ignored.","visual":"🏏"},{"title":"Meghnad Saha and the stars","body":"Physicist Meghnad Saha did not model every atom inside a star. He simplified the star's matter as a hot gas, focusing only on temperature, pressure, and how atoms formed ions -- and this simplification explained why a star's colour is linked to its temperature.","visual":"⭐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does science build models of real systems instead of studying every detail at once?","options":["Models simplify a complex system by focusing only on what matters for the question being asked","Models are always exact copies of reality with nothing left out","Models are only used when an experiment is impossible","Models replace the need for any observation of the real world"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter explains that models are simplified ways of looking at real systems, focusing on what is most important for a given question."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the cricket shot example, which details actually matter for predicting whether the ball clears the boundary?","options":["The mass of the ball and the speed and direction it was hit","The brand of the bat and the colour of the ball","The amount of grass on the field","All of the above matter equally"],"answer":0,"explain":"Example 1.1 states that mass, speed, and direction matter greatly, while bat brand, ball colour, and grass are safe to ignore."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When Meghnad Saha studied the light from stars, how did he simplify his model?","options":["He treated the matter in a star as a hot gas and focused on temperature, pressure, and ion formation","He modelled every individual atom and reaction inside the star","He ignored temperature completely","He used only the star's brightness as seen from Earth"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 'Meet a Scientist' feature explains Saha simplified stellar matter as a hot gas, ignoring many complex processes, to connect star colour with temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When studying how the heart pumps blood as a functioning system, what do scientists typically ignore?","options":["The behaviour of many individual cells","The fact that the heart is a muscle","That blood moves through the body at all","Nothing is ever ignored in a biological model"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes that individual cells are ignored so the heart can be understood as a functioning organ."}]},{"id":"ex2","title":"The Language of Precision","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"Deeper in the Atlas, you find a vast library where every word is locked to one exact meaning, and every quantity has its own secret symbol. One wrong unit here, the old librarian warns, and even an aeroplane can fall out of the sky.","teach":[{"title":"Everyday words, precise meanings","body":"Words like force, work, cell, or reaction are used loosely in everyday life, but in science they carry very specific meanings. This precision lets scientists across the world describe observations and compare results without confusion.","visual":"📖"},{"title":"Symbols and units","body":"Quantities such as mass, velocity, force, and electric current are represented by symbols like m, v, F, and I, each tied to a defined unit, so relationships between quantities can be expressed and tested using mathematics.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Why standard units matter","body":"A kilogram is expected to mean the same amount everywhere, based on agreed international standards rather than local objects or opinions. This allows scientific results to be compared and keeps trade fair.","visual":"⚖"},{"title":"The airplane fuel mix-up","body":"A well-known incident saw a passenger aircraft run out of fuel mid-flight because ground crew calculated fuel using pounds per litre instead of kilograms per litre. The plane needed 22,300 kg of fuel but ended up about 15,000 litres short, and had to glide to an emergency landing.","visual":"✈"},{"title":"Why the speed of light is 'c'","body":"Scientific symbols often come from history and international agreement, not convenience. The speed of light is denoted 'c' from the Latin word celeritas, meaning speed, and is now a defined physical constant of exactly 299,792,458 m/s.","visual":"💡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does science give precise, specific meanings to everyday words like 'force' or 'work'?","options":["So scientific ideas can be communicated clearly and unambiguously across the world","So that only scientists can ever understand them","Because everyday meanings and scientific meanings are always identical","To make science sound more complicated than it is"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter says specific meanings let scientists describe observations, compare results, and build ideas together clearly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What actually caused the famous airplane fuel mid-flight shortage described in the chapter?","options":["The ground crew calculated fuel using pounds per litre instead of kilograms per litre","The pilot forgot to check the fuel gauge","The airport ran out of fuel to sell","The aircraft's engines used more fuel than designed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mix-up happened because fuel density was calculated in pounds (lb) per litre rather than kilograms (kg) per litre."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the chapter, how many kilograms of fuel did the flight need in total?","answer":22300,"explain":"The chapter states the flight needed 22,300 kg of fuel in total."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the speed of light given the symbol 'c'?","options":["It comes from the Latin word celeritas, meaning speed","It is the first letter of the scientist who discovered light","It stands for 'constant' in English","It was chosen at random by early scientists"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 'Threads of Curiosity' box explains that 'c' comes from the Latin word celeritas, meaning speed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the exact defined speed of light in metres per second (m/s)?","answer":299792458,"explain":"The chapter states the speed of light is defined to be exactly 299,792,458 m/s."}]},{"id":"ex3","title":"Laws, Theories and Educated Estimates","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"A hooded figure challenges you at a crossroads: 'Tell me a law, a theory, and a guess -- and know the difference, or you shall not pass.' Beyond her, a storm gathers, and somewhere a phone buzzes with a rumour about eclipses and food.","teach":[{"title":"Laws, theories and principles","body":"A law describes a regular pattern in nature, often in words or equations -- like Newton's laws of motion explaining the jerk felt when a bus stops suddenly. A theory goes further, explaining why the pattern occurs, based on evidence, such as atomic theory explaining how molecules form. A principle is a broad idea applied to a situation, like conservation of energy while climbing stairs.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"A theory is not a guess","body":"In science, a theory is an explanation based on careful testing and critical examination, not an untested idea. Theories are always open to improvement and often change as new evidence becomes available -- this openness is what makes science reliable.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Predictions and their limits","body":"Well-established laws, theories, and models let scientists predict what will happen under new conditions. When predictions fail to match observations, scientists re-examine their assumptions rather than reject evidence. Weather forecasts, for instance, stay reliable only for a few hours or days because tiny differences in conditions can grow into very different outcomes over time.","visual":"🌦"},{"title":"Estimation over exactness","body":"Science often values a reasonable estimate over an exact number, especially early in reasoning. Estimating how many breaths you take and their volume shows a person breathes roughly 10,000 litres of air a day -- the goal is checking if an answer makes sense, not finding a precise figure.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between a scientific law and a scientific theory?","options":["A law describes a regular pattern in nature, while a theory explains why that pattern occurs","A law is always wrong, but a theory is always right","A theory is just a guess, while a law is proven mathematically","There is no difference between them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Laws describe patterns (like Newton's laws of motion), while theories explain why those patterns happen (like atomic theory)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The claim 'food becomes harmful during an eclipse' is debunked in the chapter because:","options":["No physical, chemical, or biological mechanism supports the claim once you ask what actually changes","Eclipses have never been observed carefully","Scientists have not tested the claim yet","It is impossible to disprove any viral claim"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter shows that asking simple scientific questions about temperature and shadows reveals no mechanism supports the claim."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do weather forecasts become less reliable further into the future?","options":["Tiny differences in conditions like temperature and pressure can grow over time into very different outcomes","Weather forecasters intentionally guess randomly after a few days","Weather has no patterns that can be measured","Forecasts are always equally reliable no matter how far ahead"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter explains tiny differences in changing factors can grow over time, making forecasts less certain further into the future."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Roughly how many litres of air does the chapter estimate a person breathes in one day?","answer":10000,"explain":"Example 1.3 estimates about 20,000 breaths a day at roughly 0.5 litre each, giving about 10,000 litres of air a day."}]},{"id":"ex-boss","title":"The Boundary Breaker","icon":"🌐","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Boundary Breaker","taunt":"Physics, chemistry, biology, earth science -- pick just ONE box to live in, explorer, or you are not worthy of the compass!"},"story":"At the edge of the Atlas stands a shape-shifting guardian who insists every idea must stay locked in one single box. Only by showing how science truly connects -- models, precise language, laws, predictions, and every branch working together -- can you prove the guardian wrong and complete your first journey.","teach":[{"title":"Science has no real walls","body":"Physics, chemistry, biology, and earth science are divisions made by us to help organise knowledge -- the natural world itself has no such boundaries. Understanding how a mask works, for example, needs physics (particle motion), chemistry (polymer fibres), biology (viruses), and mathematics (filtration modelling) all together.","visual":"😷"},{"title":"Science is a human activity","body":"Science is not just facts, equations, or experiments -- it is shaped by curiosity, creativity, collaboration, and careful questioning, built over time by people across many cultures and generations who ask questions, test ideas, and learn from mistakes.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The chapter's mask example (physics, chemistry, biology, and mathematics all needed to explain how a mask works) shows that:","options":["Real-world problems often require ideas from multiple branches of science working together","Only physics is needed to understand any real-world object","Chemistry and biology never overlap with physics","Masks are purely a mathematical invention"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter uses the mask example specifically to show how solving real problems requires knowledge from several branches of science."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When building a scientific model, what do scientists do with details that don't matter much for the question at hand?","options":["Deliberately ignore them, to keep the model simple enough while still answering the question","Include every single detail no matter what","Randomly guess which details to keep","Refuse to build a model at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Models work by focusing only on what matters for a given question and deliberately leaving out less important details."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main reason science relies on standard (SI) units instead of local or informal measures?","options":["So measurements mean the same thing everywhere, letting results be compared and trade stay fair","Because SI units are easier to pronounce","Because local units are always inaccurate","SI units are only used in physics, not other sciences"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter explains standard units are based on agreed international standards, allowing fair comparison everywhere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What truly separates a scientific theory from a mere guess?","options":["A theory is an explanation based on careful testing and evidence, always open to revision as new evidence appears","A theory can never be questioned once proposed","A theory is simply an opinion shared by many people","A theory and a guess mean exactly the same thing in science"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter stresses a theory is not a guess -- it is a well-tested explanation that remains open to being revised by evidence."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the airplane fuel mix-up story, about how many litres short of fuel was the aircraft?","answer":15000,"explain":"The chapter states the aircraft was about 15,000 litres short of fuel due to the unit mix-up."}]}]},{"id":"cell9","index":1,"name":"The Cell","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 2","emoji":"🔬","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"ce1","title":"First Sightings","icon":"🔭","kind":"lesson","story":"3.5 billion years ago, in the bubbling hot springs of early Earth, the very first cell sealed itself inside a membrane. You've shrunk down to investigate — but first you'll need to see something no naked eye ever could.","teach":[{"title":"Life's Watery Cradle","body":"Most scientists agree life began in water, possibly in small hot-spring pools rather than oceans. The hot springs of Puga Valley, Ladakh, stay near boiling even in cold climates, much like early Earth conditions. They host thermophiles — unicellular, heat-loving bacteria — and calcium carbonate deposits there may have helped form the first protective membrane, the barrier that defines a cell.","visual":"🌋"},{"title":"The Limit of the Naked Eye","body":"Viewed from the eye's near point of 25 cm, two dots closer than about 0.1 mm blur into one point. This is the limit of resolution of the human eye. Since cells are almost always smaller than this, biologists needed instruments that could magnify and resolve much finer detail.","visual":"👁️"},{"title":"Robert Hooke's Cork","body":"In 1665, Robert Hooke used a self-designed microscope (about 200-300X magnification) to examine a thin slice of cork. He saw small box-like compartments and named them 'cells' — the first time anyone had ever seen one.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Seeing Smaller and Smaller","body":"A light microscope magnifies using an objective lens and an eyepiece together — for example, two 10X lenses give 100X total magnification. Electron microscopes go further, using a beam of electrons instead of light to reveal cell structures down to the nanometre scale (one nanometre is one-billionth of a metre).","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first observed and named 'cells' in 1665, after examining a slice of cork?","options":["Robert Hooke","Rudolf Virchow","Matthias Schleiden","Camillo Golgi"],"answer":0,"explain":"Robert Hooke used a self-made microscope to see cork's box-like compartments and named them cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the limit of resolution of the unaided human eye?","options":["0.1 mm","1 mm","0.1 µm","1 nm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two points closer than about 0.1 mm, viewed from 25 cm, appear as a single point to the naked eye."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If both the eyepiece and objective lens of a light microscope have a magnifying power of 10X, what is the total magnification?","answer":100,"explain":"Total magnification is the product of eyepiece and objective magnifying powers: 10 x 10 = 100X."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of organisms are the thermophiles found in the hot springs of Puga Valley, Ladakh?","options":["Unicellular, heat-loving bacteria","Multicellular fungi","Unicellular plants","Multicellular animals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thermophiles are unicellular bacteria that thrive in the near-boiling conditions of hot springs."}]},{"id":"ce2","title":"The Gatekeeper","icon":"🧫","kind":"lesson","story":"Every cell needs a border guard — something that decides what gets in and what stays out. Dive into the thin, fluid boundary that gives every cell its individuality.","teach":[{"title":"The Plasma Membrane","body":"The cell membrane (or plasma membrane) is a thin boundary, only about 7 to 10 nanometres thick, that surrounds and protects every cell's contents. It is selectively permeable — allowing some substances through while blocking others — and defines the individuality of the cell.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"The Fluid-Mosaic Model","body":"The membrane is a lipid bilayer: two layers of fat molecules with water-attracting heads facing outward and water-repelling tails facing inward, with proteins embedded throughout. Because the molecules can move sideways, flip, and rotate, the membrane is fluid; because the parts are arranged like tiles, it's called a mosaic. The embedded proteins act like gatekeepers, helping substances pass through.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Diffusion and Osmosis","body":"Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a higher to a lower concentration, and it can happen even without a membrane. Osmosis is specifically the diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane, from a dilute (more water) region to a concentrated (less water) region. This is how water enters plant root cells from the soil.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"The Cell Wall","body":"Plant, fungal, and bacterial cells have an extra rigid layer outside the cell membrane called the cell wall, made mainly of cellulose in plants. It is permeable to water and dissolved minerals and keeps the cell's shape even when it loses water — the membrane pulls away from the wall instead of the whole cell collapsing, unlike an animal cell, which has no wall and simply shrinks.","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is another name for the cell membrane?","options":["Plasma membrane","Cell wall","Nuclear membrane","Golgi membrane"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cell membrane is also called the plasma membrane."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is osmosis?","options":["Diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane","Movement of solute particles across any surface","Active pumping of proteins out of a cell","Splitting of a cell into two daughter cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Osmosis is the diffusion of water specifically, moving across a selectively permeable membrane."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the fluid-mosaic model, which part of the lipid bilayer faces outward toward water?","options":["The water-attracting heads","The water-repelling tails","The embedded proteins only","The cellulose fibres"],"answer":0,"explain":"The water-attracting (hydrophilic) heads face outward while the water-repelling tails point inward, away from water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A potato piece placed in a strong salt solution loses weight and shrinks. Why?","options":["Water moved out of the potato cells by osmosis","Salt moved into the potato cells","The cell wall dissolved in salt water","Diffusion pulled the cell membrane apart"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water moves from the region with more water (inside the cell) to the region with less water and more solute (the concentrated salt solution), so the potato loses water and shrinks."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The plant cell wall is primarily made of which substance?","options":["Cellulose","Chlorophyll","Chromatin","Haemoglobin"],"answer":0,"explain":"The plant cell wall is made mainly of cellulose, a carbohydrate formed of many linked glucose units."}]},{"id":"ce3","title":"Two Kinds of Cities","icon":"🏙️","kind":"lesson","story":"Not all cells are built the same. Some are simple one-room huts, others are sprawling cities with dedicated districts for every job. Time to compare blueprints.","teach":[{"title":"Three Basic Parts","body":"Most cells have three basic parts: a selectively permeable plasma membrane, a semi-fluid jelly-like cytoplasm, and a prominent nucleus. Within the cytoplasm sit sub-cellular components called organelles, many of which are visible only under an electron microscope.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Prokaryotic Cells","body":"A bacterial cell lacks a well-defined nucleus and membrane-bound organelles — 'pro' means primitive and 'karyon' means nucleus. Such prokaryotic cells carry out most cellular activities directly in the cytoplasm, their DNA sits in a region called the nucleoid, and they are usually unicellular with a diameter of just 1 to 10 µm.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"Eukaryotic Cells","body":"Plant and animal cells have a well-defined nucleus and several membrane-bound organelles — 'eu' means true. Eukaryotic cells can be unicellular or multicellular and are larger, typically 10 to 100 µm in diameter.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"The Cytoskeleton","body":"In eukaryotic cells, a network of fine fibres called the cytoskeleton provides structural support, maintains cell shape, and enables cell movement and internal transport. It is visible only under an electron microscope. The cytoplasm may also hold cell inclusions such as starch or crystals of calcium oxalate or silica.","visual":"🕸️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What do prokaryotic cells lack, compared to eukaryotic cells?","options":["A well-defined nucleus and membrane-bound organelles","A cytoplasm","A cell membrane","Any genetic material"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prokaryotic cells have no well-defined nucleus or membrane-bound organelles; their DNA lies in the nucleoid region instead."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the region containing genetic material in a bacterial (prokaryotic) cell called?","options":["Nucleoid","Nucleolus","Nuclear membrane","Chromatin"],"answer":0,"explain":"In prokaryotic cells, the circular DNA lies in a region called the nucleoid, without a surrounding membrane."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the text, a typical eukaryotic cell's diameter can be as large as how many micrometres (µm)?","answer":100,"explain":"Eukaryotic cells typically range from 10 to 100 µm in diameter, compared to 1 to 10 µm for prokaryotic cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the cytoskeleton do in a eukaryotic cell?","options":["Provides structural support, maintains shape, and enables movement and transport","Produces energy through cellular respiration","Synthesises chlorophyll for photosynthesis","Stores water and maintains turgor pressure"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cytoskeleton is a network of fine fibres giving structural support, shape, and enabling movement and internal transport."}]},{"id":"ce4","title":"The Factory Floor","icon":"🏭","kind":"lesson","story":"A cell is a tiny living factory: instructions in one room, assembly lines in another, a shipping department, and a clean-up crew. Walk the factory floor and meet the workers.","teach":[{"title":"Nucleus — House of Coded Instructions","body":"The nucleus has a double-layered nuclear membrane with pores that let material pass to and from the cytoplasm. Inside, the nucleolus makes ribosomal subunits, which travel to the cytoplasm to assemble into ribosomes. The nucleus holds chromosomes — rod-shaped structures of DNA and protein, visible only when a cell is about to divide; in a non-dividing cell this DNA exists as tangled chromatin. The functional segments of DNA are called genes.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Ribosomes — Protein Factories","body":"Ribosomes are tiny structures found either free in the cytoplasm or attached to the endoplasmic reticulum. They are the sites of protein synthesis.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"The Endoplasmic Reticulum","body":"The ER is a large, network-like organelle continuous with the nuclear membrane. Rough ER (RER) has ribosomes attached, giving it a rough look, and mainly handles protein synthesis and secretion (as in pancreatic cells). Smooth ER (SER) lacks ribosomes and is involved in synthesising and storing fats and hormones.","visual":"🕸️"},{"title":"Golgi Apparatus — Packaging & Shipping","body":"The Golgi apparatus is a stack of flattened, sac-like structures, functionally linked to the ER and cell membrane. It modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids into vesicles for transport, secretion, or lysosome formation — acting like the cell's post office. It was first observed in 1898 by Camillo Golgi.","visual":"📦"},{"title":"Lysosomes — The Clean-Up System","body":"Lysosomes are single membrane-bound sacs filled with enzymes that break down unwanted proteins, carbohydrates, fats, and even damaged parts of the cell, keeping it clean and healthy. The breakdown products are released into the cytoplasm to be reused.","visual":"🧹"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens in the nucleolus, the dense round body inside the nucleus?","options":["Synthesis of ribosomal subunits","Synthesis of ATP","Breakdown of damaged organelles","Storage of starch and cell sap"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nucleolus produces ribosomal subunits, which later exit to the cytoplasm and assemble into ribosomes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key structural difference between Rough ER and Smooth ER?","options":["RER has ribosomes attached to its surface, SER does not","SER is found only in animal cells","RER stores fats and hormones, SER makes proteins","SER is continuous with the nuclear membrane, RER is not"],"answer":0,"explain":"RER looks rough because ribosomes are attached to it and it mainly makes/secretes proteins; SER has no ribosomes and looks smooth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Golgi apparatus mainly do?","options":["Modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids into vesicles","Breaks down worn-out organelles using enzymes","Produces ATP through cellular respiration","Synthesises ribosomal subunits"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Golgi apparatus acts like the cell's post office, packaging proteins and lipids into vesicles for transport, secretion, or lysosome formation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Lysosomes are best described as sacs filled with what?","options":["Enzymes that break down waste and damaged cell parts","Chlorophyll for photosynthesis","DNA and ribosomes","Cell sap for water storage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lysosomes contain enzymes capable of breaking down unwanted proteins, carbohydrates, fats, and damaged organelles."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did Camillo Golgi first observe the structure now named after him?","answer":1898,"explain":"Camillo Golgi observed the Golgi apparatus in 1898 in the nerve cells of a barn owl."}]},{"id":"ce5","title":"Power and Pigment","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"Every factory needs a power plant — and plant cells need a kitchen too. Explore the organelles that generate energy, cook up food from sunlight, and store the surplus.","teach":[{"title":"Mitochondria — The Powerhouse","body":"Mitochondria supply the energy needed for most cellular activities and are often called the 'powerhouses of the cell.' Each mitochondrion has an outer membrane that is smooth and porous, and an inner membrane folded into finger-like cristae that increase surface area for chemical reactions. During cellular respiration, glucose and other molecules are broken down to release energy, stored in a molecule called ATP (Adenosine Triphosphate), the cell's energy currency.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Chloroplasts — Food Synthesis","body":"Plants use plastids for food synthesis and storage. Chloroplasts are double-membrane-bound organelles containing the green pigment chlorophyll, which absorbs sunlight for photosynthesis. Inside is a semi-fluid stroma holding disc-shaped, chlorophyll-containing membranes; the sugars made are stored in the stroma along with starch granules.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Chromoplasts and Leucoplasts","body":"Chromoplasts contain pigments other than chlorophyll — yellow, orange, or red — giving bright colours to flowers and fruits that attract pollinators and seed-dispersing animals. Leucoplasts lack pigment and are colourless; they store food such as starch, oils, or proteins, as in potato and taro (Colocasia) cells.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"Vacuoles — Storage and Support","body":"A mature plant cell usually has one large central vacuole, bounded by a selectively permeable membrane and filled with cell sap, storing water, minerals, sugars, and waste. By holding water, it keeps the cell firm; when water is scarce the vacuole shrinks and the plant wilts. Animal cells may have smaller vacuoles for temporary storage.","visual":"💦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are mitochondria often called the 'powerhouses of the cell'?","options":["They supply the energy needed for most cellular activities","They store water and maintain cell firmness","They package and ship proteins in vesicles","They contain chlorophyll for photosynthesis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mitochondria carry out cellular respiration, releasing energy stored in ATP for the cell's activities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the finger-like folds of a mitochondrion's inner membrane called?","options":["Cristae","Stroma","Cisternae","Nucleoid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cristae are folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane that increase surface area for chemical reactions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the 'energy currency' molecule produced during cellular respiration?","options":["ATP (Adenosine Triphosphate)","DNA","Chlorophyll","Cellulose"],"answer":0,"explain":"The energy released during cellular respiration is stored in ATP, which the cell then uses for its activities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What gives flowers and fruits their bright yellow, orange, or red colours?","options":["Pigments in chromoplasts","Chlorophyll in chloroplasts","Starch in leucoplasts","Cell sap in vacuoles"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chromoplasts contain non-green pigments that produce the bright colours seen in many flowers and fruits."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to a plant when its vacuoles lose too much water?","options":["The plant wilts as cells become less firm","The cell wall dissolves","The chloroplasts stop making chlorophyll","The nucleus shrinks and divides"],"answer":0,"explain":"The central vacuole normally keeps a plant cell firm by storing water; without enough water, cells lose firmness and the plant wilts."}]},{"id":"ce6","title":"Cells Multiply, Cells Retire","icon":"🔁","kind":"lesson","story":"A cut on your skin heals. A single fertilised egg becomes a whole body. Somewhere, cells are dividing — and, just as importantly, some are knowing when to stop.","teach":[{"title":"Cell Theory","body":"In 1838, Matthias Schleiden showed all plants are made of cells; in 1839, Theodor Schwann showed the same for animals. In 1855, Rudolf Virchow added that new cells form only from pre-existing cells. Together these give the Cell Theory: all living organisms are made of one or more cells, the cell is the basic unit of structure and function, and all cells arise from pre-existing cells.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Mitosis","body":"Mitosis is the most common type of cell division. It produces two genetically identical daughter cells, each getting the same DNA and chromosome number as the parent cell. Mitosis drives normal growth, repair, maintenance, and asexual reproduction.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Meiosis","body":"Meiosis occurs only in cells of reproductive organs (testes and ovaries in animals; anthers and ovaries in plants) and produces gametes. The parent cell divides twice in succession to form four daughter cells, each with half the chromosome number of the parent. When gametes fuse at fertilisation, the full chromosome number is restored, and genetic diversity is created.","visual":"🌸"},{"title":"Growth, Control, and Cancer","body":"Cells grow and divide in a controlled way, then eventually die when no longer needed, being replaced by new cells. In many animal cells, division stops once cells touch their neighbours — a process called contact inhibition. Cancer cells lose this control and keep dividing uncontrollably, forming tumours. Plant cells, protected by rigid walls, do not show contact inhibition and grow differently.","visual":"🚦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who, in 1855, expanded the Cell Theory by stating that new cells arise only from pre-existing cells?","options":["Rudolf Virchow","Matthias Schleiden","Theodor Schwann","Robert Hooke"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rudolf Virchow, a German scientist, added this principle in 1855, completing the classical Cell Theory."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does mitosis produce?","options":["Two genetically identical daughter cells","Four daughter cells with half the chromosomes","One cell with double the DNA","A tumour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mitosis produces two daughter cells identical to the parent, each with the same DNA and chromosome number."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many daughter cells does meiosis produce from one parent cell?","answer":4,"explain":"Meiosis involves two successive divisions, producing four daughter cells, each with half the chromosome number."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 'contact inhibition'?","options":["Cell division stopping when cells touch their neighbours","A cell losing its nucleus during division","Water leaving a cell during osmosis","Enzymes breaking down damaged organelles"],"answer":0,"explain":"Contact inhibition is when animal cells stop dividing upon contact with neighbouring cells; cancer cells lose this control."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In animals, where does meiosis take place?","options":["Testes (producing sperm) and ovaries (producing eggs)","Only in skin cells","Only in the nucleus of red blood cells","In mitochondria of muscle cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Meiosis occurs only in the cells of reproductive organs — testes in males and ovaries in females."}]},{"id":"ce-boss","title":"The Rogue Divider","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Cancerion, the Endless Divider","taunt":"Contact inhibition? Never heard of it — I don't stop dividing for anyone!"},"story":"Deep in the tissue, a cell has stopped listening to its neighbours and started dividing without control. Everything you've learned about cells — their structure, their organelles, their division — is the only thing that can explain, and stop, this rogue.","teach":[{"title":"The Chapter, In Brief","body":"The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. Prokaryotic cells have no true nucleus (just a nucleoid) and no membrane-bound organelles; eukaryotic cells have both. All cells have a membrane; plants, fungi, and bacteria add a cell wall. Organelles like the nucleus, ER, Golgi apparatus, mitochondria, and lysosomes each do a specific job, and cells divide by mitosis or meiosis — until, sometimes, that control breaks down.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first observed and named 'cells' in 1665, using a self-made microscope to examine cork?","options":["Robert Hooke","Camillo Golgi","Rudolf Virchow","Theodor Schwann"],"answer":0,"explain":"Robert Hooke observed cork's box-like compartments in 1665 and named them cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What do prokaryotic cells lack that eukaryotic cells have?","options":["A well-defined nucleus and membrane-bound organelles","A cell membrane","Cytoplasm","DNA"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prokaryotic cells have no true nucleus or membrane-bound organelles; their DNA sits in the nucleoid region."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organelle is known as the 'powerhouse of the cell'?","options":["Mitochondria","Golgi apparatus","Lysosome","Nucleolus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mitochondria carry out cellular respiration, releasing energy stored as ATP."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Golgi apparatus do?","options":["Modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids into vesicles","Breaks down waste using enzymes","Synthesises chlorophyll","Stores cell sap"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Golgi apparatus acts like the cell's post office, packaging materials for transport or secretion."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many daughter cells are produced by one round of meiosis?","answer":4,"explain":"Meiosis involves two successive divisions and produces four daughter cells, each with half the chromosome number."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which scientist added to the Cell Theory in 1855 that all new cells arise only from pre-existing cells?","options":["Rudolf Virchow","Matthias Schleiden","Robert Hooke","Camillo Golgi"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rudolf Virchow's 1855 contribution completed the classical Cell Theory."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is contact inhibition, and what happens when a cell loses it?","options":["Cell division normally stops on contact with neighbours; losing it lets cells divide uncontrollably, forming tumours","Cells stop absorbing water; losing it causes the cell to swell and burst","Ribosomes stop making protein; losing it halts all growth","The nucleus stops dividing; losing it prevents mitosis entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Contact inhibition normally stops division when cells touch neighbours; cancer cells lose this control and divide uncontrollably, forming tumours."}]}]},{"id":"tissues9","index":2,"name":"Tissues in Action","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 3","emoji":"🧫","palette":{"from":"#f472b6","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"ti1","title":"Cells Unite","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"You shrink down to the size of a cell and land inside a growing seedling. Alone, one cell can barely do anything -- but all around you, cells are linking arms with their neighbours, forming teams built for a single job. Welcome to the world of tissues.","teach":[{"title":"What is a tissue?","body":"A tissue is a group of cells that are similar in structure and work together to perform a specific function. Instead of one cell doing everything, groups of cells specialise -- this division of labour increases the efficiency of the body and allows it to carry out complex life processes.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"One cell vs many cells","body":"In a unicellular organism like Amoeba, a single cell performs all functions of life. In multicellular organisms such as plants and animals, different groups of cells perform different functions, giving rise to a hierarchy: cells form tissues, tissues form organs, organs form organ systems, and organ systems form an organism.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Why plant and animal tissues differ","body":"Most plants stay fixed in one place and need support to stay upright, so plant cells have a rigid cell wall for strength. Animals generally move about, and without a rigid cell wall, animal cells can change shape easily -- this flexibility suits their bodies for locomotion.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Different jobs, different tissues","body":"Plants and animals also differ in nutrition and transport. Plants use tissues to capture solar energy for photosynthesis and have xylem and phloem to transport water and food. Animals have tissues to digest food from external sources and transport materials through tissues like blood.","visual":"🥦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a tissue?","options":["A group of similar cells working together to perform a specific function","A single cell that performs every function of life","A type of organ found only in animals","The outermost layer of a plant's stem"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tissue is a group of cells similar in structure that work together to perform a specific function."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can animal cells change shape more easily than plant cells?","options":["They lack a rigid cell wall","They have a thicker cell wall than plant cells","They do not have a cell membrane","They contain chloroplasts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without a rigid cell wall, animal cells can change shape easily, which helps make their bodies suitable for locomotion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which organism does a single cell perform all functions of life?","options":["Amoeba","Sunflower","Onion","Human"],"answer":0,"explain":"In unicellular organisms such as Amoeba, one single cell carries out every life function."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What benefit does forming different tissues give to a multicellular body?","options":["Division of labour, which increases efficiency","It makes every cell identical","It removes the need for organs","It stops the organism from growing further"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forming different tissues leads to division of labour, which increases the efficiency of the body and allows complex life processes."}]},{"id":"ti2","title":"The Growing Edge","icon":"🌱","kind":"lesson","story":"In a lab, two onion bulbs sit in jars of water. One is left alone; the other has its root tips snipped off. Days later, only one jar shows roots still growing. Something at the very tip holds the secret to a plant's growth.","teach":[{"title":"Meristematic tissue","body":"Growth in plants -- increase in length, increase in girth, and regrowth after cutting or grazing -- requires actively dividing cells that together form a tissue called meristematic tissue. These cells are small, have thin walls, a large prominent nucleus, dense cytoplasm, and usually no vacuoles, and they are tightly packed with little intercellular space.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Apical meristem -- growth in length","body":"The onion experiment shows that roots in the jar with intact tips keep growing, while roots with cut tips stop growing. This is because root and shoot tips contain growth zones of actively dividing cells called apical meristems, which make roots and shoots grow in length.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Lateral meristem -- growth in girth","body":"Actively dividing cells arranged in a ring within the stem produce new cells inside and outside in a concentric manner, increasing the stem's diameter. This is called the lateral meristem, and its activity over the years produces the annual growth rings visible in a cut tree trunk.","visual":"🪵"},{"title":"Intercalary meristem -- regrowth after cutting","body":"Located at the base of an internode or just above a node, the intercalary meristem lets plants like grass regenerate and put out new branches after their tips are cut, mowed, or grazed. This is why a mowed lawn or a trimmed hedge grows bushy again.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Differentiation","body":"Due to continuous division, meristematic tissue keeps adding new cells to the plant body. Some new cells stay meristematic, while others lose the ability to divide, change in structure and function, and become specialised permanent tissue. This process is called differentiation.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which meristem is located at root and shoot tips and makes plants grow in length?","options":["Apical meristem","Lateral meristem","Intercalary meristem","Ground tissue"],"answer":0,"explain":"The apical meristem, located at the tips of roots and shoots, is responsible for growth in length."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the onion experiment, what happened to the roots after their tips were cut off?","options":["The roots stopped growing","The roots grew even faster","The roots turned into shoots","There was no change at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Roots grow only from their tips, which contain actively dividing cells; cutting the tip stops further growth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which meristematic tissue is responsible for a tree trunk increasing in girth and forming annual growth rings?","options":["Lateral meristem","Apical meristem","Intercalary meristem","Epidermis"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lateral meristem, arranged in a ring in the stem, divides to increase the stem's diameter, producing annual rings."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A lawn grows bushy again soon after mowing because of which meristematic tissue near its nodes?","options":["Intercalary meristem","Apical meristem","Lateral meristem","Sclerenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"The intercalary meristem, located at the base of the internode near the nodes, allows regrowth after cutting or grazing."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A tree trunk is cut and 25 annual growth rings are counted on its cross-section. According to the annual-ring method, how many years old is the tree?","answer":25,"explain":"Each annual ring formed by the lateral meristem roughly corresponds to one year of growth, so 25 rings means the tree is about 25 years old."}]},{"id":"ti3","title":"Built to Last","icon":"🛡️","kind":"lesson","story":"Under a microscope, a slice of sunflower stem reveals a patchwork of very different-looking cells. Some are tightly packed like a wall, some are loosely stacked like cushions, and some look tough as wood. Each has stopped dividing -- and each now has one job for life.","teach":[{"title":"Permanent tissue","body":"Once meristematic cells differentiate, they become permanent tissue, specialised to perform a specific function such as protection, support, or storage. Permanent tissue can be simple, made of only one type of cell, or complex, made of more than one type of cell.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Epidermis -- the protective tissue","body":"The epidermis forms the outermost layer of the plant body: a single, tightly packed layer of flat, rectangular cells covered with a waxy cuticle that reduces water loss and protects against injury and microbes. In roots it forms root hair for absorbing water and minerals, and in leaves it contains stomata for gas exchange and transpiration.","visual":"🍃"},{"title":"Parenchyma","body":"Parenchyma consists of living cells with thin walls, loosely packed with intercellular spaces. It mainly stores food but also carries out photosynthesis in green parts of the plant; in aquatic plants, specialised parenchyma forms air spaces that help the plant float.","visual":"🟢"},{"title":"Collenchyma","body":"Collenchyma consists of living cells with unevenly thickened corners due to pectin deposition. This gives support along with flexibility, allowing parts like stems and tendrils to bend without breaking.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Sclerenchyma","body":"Sclerenchyma cells have thick, lignified walls that make them hard and strong; most of these cells are dead. This tissue is found in stems, leaf veins, and hard coverings like coconut husk and walnut shell, and forms the woody structure of the plant.","visual":"🥜"},{"title":"Three tissue systems","body":"Plant tissues are organised into three tissue systems: the dermal tissue system (outer covering, protection), the ground tissue system (parenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma forming the main body between dermal and vascular tissues), and the vascular tissue system (the conducting tissues, xylem and phloem).","visual":"🗺️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue forms the outermost, protective layer of the plant body, covered with a waxy cuticle?","options":["Epidermis","Sclerenchyma","Xylem","Lateral meristem"],"answer":0,"explain":"The epidermis is a single tightly packed layer of cells covered by a waxy cuticle, protecting the plant and reducing water loss."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which simple permanent tissue has thin-walled, loosely packed living cells and mainly stores food?","options":["Parenchyma","Collenchyma","Sclerenchyma","Phloem fibres"],"answer":0,"explain":"Parenchyma consists of living, thin-walled cells loosely packed with intercellular spaces, and it mainly stores food."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue lets stems and tendrils bend without breaking, thanks to unevenly thickened corners of pectin?","options":["Collenchyma","Sclerenchyma","Epidermis","Xylem"],"answer":0,"explain":"Collenchyma cells have unevenly thickened corners due to pectin, giving support along with flexibility."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Coconut husk fibres are hard and tough because they are made of which tissue?","options":["Sclerenchyma","Parenchyma","Epidermis","Companion cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sclerenchyma has thick, lignified walls, mostly in dead cells, making tissues like coconut husk hard and strong."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue system lies between the dermal and vascular tissue systems and forms the main body of the plant?","options":["Ground tissue system","Dermal tissue system","Vascular tissue system","Meristematic tissue system"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ground tissue system, made of parenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma, forms the main body between the dermal and vascular systems."}]},{"id":"ti4","title":"Rivers Inside the Stem","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"Somewhere near the roots, water begins an uphill journey -- against gravity, all the way to leaves at the top of a tall tree. Meanwhile, sugar made in those same leaves needs to travel back down. Two invisible rivers run through every plant.","teach":[{"title":"Complex permanent tissues","body":"Xylem and phloem are called complex permanent tissues because, unlike simple tissues, each is made up of more than one type of cell working together. Together they are known as conducting or vascular tissue.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Xylem -- water and mineral transport","body":"Xylem transports water and minerals from the roots to other parts of the plant, and also provides strength. It consists of tracheids, vessels, xylem parenchyma and xylem fibres. Xylem parenchyma is the only living component of xylem, while tracheids, vessels and xylem fibres are mostly sclerenchymatous, tubular and thick-walled.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Phloem -- food transport","body":"Phloem is mostly made up of living cells and transports food from the leaves to other parts of the plant. It consists of sieve tubes, companion cells, phloem parenchyma and phloem fibres.","visual":"🥬"},{"title":"The cells of phloem","body":"Sieve tubes are long tubular cells joined end to end by perforated walls, and they carry food. Companion cells are specialised parenchyma cells that regulate the sieve tubes, mainly controlling the loading and unloading of sugars. Phloem parenchyma stores food, resin, tannins and latex, while phloem fibres, which are sclerenchymatous, provide strength.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue transports water and minerals from the roots to the rest of the plant?","options":["Xylem","Phloem","Epidermis","Collenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Xylem transports water and minerals from roots to other parts of the plant and also gives it strength."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue carries food made in the leaves to other parts of the plant?","options":["Phloem","Xylem","Sclerenchyma","Meristem"],"answer":0,"explain":"Phloem, mostly made of living cells, transports food from the leaves to the rest of the plant body."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the only living component of the xylem tissue?","options":["Xylem parenchyma","Tracheids","Vessels","Xylem fibres"],"answer":0,"explain":"Xylem parenchyma is the only living part of xylem; tracheids, vessels and xylem fibres are mostly sclerenchymatous."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which phloem cells regulate the loading and unloading of sugars into the sieve tubes?","options":["Companion cells","Sieve tubes","Phloem fibres","Phloem parenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Companion cells are specialised parenchyma cells whose main function is to monitor sugar loading and unloading in sieve tubes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are xylem and phloem called complex permanent tissues?","options":["Because each is made up of more than one type of cell working together","Because they are found only in complex organisms","Because they are the newest tissues to form in a plant","Because they never stop dividing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Complex permanent tissues, unlike simple ones, are composed of more than one type of cell working together."}]},{"id":"ti5","title":"Cover and Connect","icon":"🩸","kind":"lesson","story":"Blink your eyes. Touch something cold. Look at a small cut on your skin as it clots. Every one of these moments is animal tissue at work -- covering your body, lining your organs, and quietly holding everything together.","teach":[{"title":"Epithelial tissue","body":"Epithelial tissue forms the outer covering of the body (skin) and lines internal organs such as the mouth, lungs, blood vessels and intestine. It is made of closely packed cells with very little space between them, which prevents entry of germs, reduces water loss, and helps in absorption, secretion and movement of substances.","visual":"🪧"},{"title":"Structure matches function","body":"Different epithelial tissues are shaped for their job: a single layer of thin flat cells lines blood vessels and lungs for rapid exchange of gases and liquids; many layers of tightly packed flat cells protect the skin, mouth and oesophagus; cuboidal or columnar cells in glands specialise in secretion; receptor cells with cilia handle sensory functions like smell and taste; and a single layer of tall columnar cells lines the small intestine for efficient absorption.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Connective tissue","body":"A tissue that connects and supports other tissues is called connective tissue. Blood and bone are both connective tissues, but they differ in composition and consistency because of their matrix -- watery, soft and jelly-like in blood, but hard, solid and rigid in bone.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Blood -- a fluid connective tissue","body":"Blood is made of plasma (about 55% of volume) and formed elements (about 45%): red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs) and platelets. Haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein in RBCs, gives blood its red colour; RBCs live about 4 months. Platelets help blood clot at the site of an injury, while WBCs gather at infected areas, causing pus and inflammation.","visual":"🩸"},{"title":"Bone, cartilage, tendons and ligaments","body":"Bone has a rigid matrix containing calcium and phosphorus compounds, giving it strength and support. Cartilage has a soft, jelly-like matrix that provides flexibility and cushioning, such as in the ear and nose. Tendons connect muscle to bone, transmitting the pull that causes movement, while ligaments connect bone to bone and limit movement to prevent dislocation.","visual":"🦴"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tissue forms the outer covering of the body and lines organs like the lungs and intestine?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Muscular tissue","Nervous tissue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Epithelial tissue covers the body and lines internal organs, made of closely packed cells with little space between them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main reason blood and bone are both classed as connective tissue despite being so different?","options":["Both connect and support other tissues, differing mainly in their matrix","Both are made of exactly the same type of cell","Both are found only in the skeleton","Both are unable to move materials around the body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Connective tissue connects and supports other tissues; blood and bone differ in consistency because their matrix differs (fluid vs rigid)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which blood component helps in clotting at the site of an injury?","options":["Platelets","Red blood cells","Plasma","White blood cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Platelets help in blood clotting at the site of injury."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which connective tissue connects muscle to bone and transmits the pull of a contracting muscle?","options":["Tendon","Ligament","Cartilage","Plasma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tendons connect muscles to bones, so a muscle's contraction is transmitted to the bone to cause movement."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which connective tissue connects bone to bone and helps prevent dislocation at a joint?","options":["Ligament","Tendon","Cartilage","Bone marrow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ligaments connect bone to bone, providing stability and limiting movement to prevent dislocation."}]},{"id":"ti6","title":"Move and Command","icon":"💪","kind":"lesson","story":"Your hand jerks back from a hot pan before you even think about it. Your heart beats on, whether you notice or not. Somewhere between muscle and nerve, your body is making split-second decisions -- and turning them into motion.","teach":[{"title":"Skeletal muscle -- voluntary movement","body":"Voluntary movements like running, writing or lifting are carried out by skeletal muscles, attached to the skeleton. They are made of bundles of long, cylindrical fibres that are unbranched, multinucleate (many nuclei), and striated (show light and dark bands).","visual":"🦵"},{"title":"Smooth and cardiac muscle -- involuntary movement","body":"Involuntary movements, such as food moving through the intestine, are carried out by smooth muscle: spindle-shaped cells with a single nucleus and no striations, found in organs like the stomach and intestines. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart -- its cylindrical, branched fibres have a single nucleus and faint striations, and it contracts rhythmically throughout life without fatigue.","visual":"❤️"},{"title":"Nervous tissue and the neuron","body":"Nervous tissue forms the body's control and coordination network; the brain acts as the control centre for activities, memory and responses. Its cells, called neurons, have a cell body (with the nucleus, controlling cell activities), dendrites (receiving signals from other neurons), and an axon (a long fibre carrying messages to axon terminals, which pass them to other cells).","visual":"🧠"},{"title":"The musculoskeletal system","body":"The musculoskeletal system is made up of bones, muscles, joints, cartilage, tendons and ligaments, and functions under the control of the nervous system. Muscles are attached to bones by tendons; when a muscle contracts, the tendon transmits this force to the bone, producing movement at a joint. On average, the adult human skeleton makes up about 12-15 percent of body weight.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"Types of joints","body":"A joint is a junction between two or more bones. A ball and socket joint (shoulder) allows free movement in many directions. A hinge joint (elbow, knee) bends and straightens in one direction only, like a door. A pivot joint (neck, at the skull-backbone junction) allows side-to-side rotation. Fixed joints (skull bones) do not allow any movement, keeping the brain safe.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"The skeletal system","body":"The skeletal system -- skull, vertebral column and rib cage -- provides strength and protects delicate organs. The vertebral column is a flexible column of vertebrae cushioned by cartilage discs between them. The rib cage has 12 pairs of ribs, joined to the spine and sternum by flexible cartilage, letting the chest expand and contract during breathing.","visual":"🦴"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which muscle type is voluntary, striated and attached to the skeleton?","options":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Epithelial muscle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Skeletal muscles carry out voluntary movements and are made of striated, multinucleate, unbranched fibres attached to the skeleton."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which muscle type is found only in the heart and can contract rhythmically without fatigue?","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Sclerenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cardiac muscle, found only in the heart, has branched fibres and works tirelessly and rhythmically throughout life."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of a neuron is a long fibre that carries messages away from the cell body to other cells?","options":["Axon","Dendrite","Cell body","Nucleus"],"answer":0,"explain":"The axon is a long fibre that carries messages from the cell body to axon terminals, which transmit them to other cells."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of joint allows the shoulder to move forward, backward, sideways and in a circle?","options":["Ball and socket joint","Hinge joint","Pivot joint","Fixed joint"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rounded top of the upper arm bone fits into a shallow hollow of the shoulder bone, forming a ball and socket joint that allows free movement."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of joint connects the bones of the skull and allows no movement at all?","options":["Fixed joint","Hinge joint","Pivot joint","Ball and socket joint"],"answer":0,"explain":"The bones of the skull are connected by fixed joints, keeping the brain safe even when the body moves."}]},{"id":"ti-boss","title":"The Tissue Titan","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the organism looms the Tissue Titan, built from every kind of tissue you have studied -- meristem, xylem, phloem, epithelium, bone and muscle fused into one towering challenge. Only a true understanding of structure and function can bring it down.","teach":[{"title":"Plants: growth and permanence","body":"Plant tissues divide into meristematic tissue (apical, lateral, intercalary -- responsible for growth in length, girth and regrowth) and permanent tissue, which can be simple (parenchyma, collenchyma, sclerenchyma) or complex (xylem, phloem), each specialised for protection, support or conduction.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Animals: four main tissue types","body":"Animal tissues are mainly of four types: epithelial tissue (covering and lining), connective tissue (connecting and supporting, like blood and bone), muscular tissue (voluntary and involuntary movement), and nervous tissue (control and coordination via neurons).","visual":"🩺"},{"title":"Movement needs teamwork","body":"In the body, movement happens through the coordination of muscles and bones -- the musculoskeletal system -- all under the control of the nervous system, with joints determining how freely each body part can move.","visual":"🦴"}],"boss":{"name":"The Tissue Titan","taunt":"Tell your parenchyma from your sclerenchyma, or crumble apart cell by cell!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which meristematic tissue is responsible for a plant's growth in length?","options":["Apical meristem","Lateral meristem","Intercalary meristem","Sclerenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"The apical meristem, at root and shoot tips, drives growth in length."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which complex permanent tissue transports water and minerals from roots to the rest of the plant?","options":["Xylem","Phloem","Epidermis","Collenchyma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Xylem transports water and minerals from the roots and also provides strength to the plant."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which complex permanent tissue carries food from the leaves to the rest of the plant?","options":["Phloem","Xylem","Parenchyma","Meristem"],"answer":0,"explain":"Phloem, made mostly of living cells including sieve tubes, transports food from the leaves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of epithelial tissue lines the small intestine, suited for efficient absorption?","options":["A single layer of tall, pillar-like columnar cells","Many layers of flat, tightly packed cells","A single layer of thin, flat cells","Cells with hair-like cilia"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single layer of tall, columnar cells, often with hair-like structures, lines the small intestine for efficient absorption."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which connective tissue has a hard, rigid matrix containing calcium and phosphorus compounds?","options":["Bone","Blood","Cartilage","Areolar tissue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bone has a rigid matrix rich in calcium and phosphorus compounds, giving it strength and rigidity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which muscle type is involuntary, spindle-shaped, single-nucleated and found in the stomach and intestines?","options":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Epithelial muscle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with a single nucleus and no striations, enabling slow, continuous involuntary movements like digestion."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many pairs of ribs make up the human rib cage, according to the text?","answer":12,"explain":"The text states that humans have 12 pairs of ribs, together forming the rib cage that protects the heart and lungs."}]}]},{"id":"motion9","index":3,"name":"Describing Motion","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 4","emoji":"🏃","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"mo1","title":"Finding Your Position","icon":"📍","kind":"lesson","story":"You wake up in an unmarked forest with only a notebook and a compass. Before you can track anything that moves, you must learn to pin down exactly where things are - starting with a fixed reference point and a straight line marked with distances.","teach":[{"title":"Position needs a reference point","body":"To describe where an object is, we first fix a reference point (the origin O). The position of the object at any instant is described by its distance and direction from this reference point. If the object's position changes with time, it is in motion; if it doesn't, it is at rest.","visual":"📌"},{"title":"Distance travelled vs displacement","body":"Distance travelled is the total length of the path covered, with no regard to direction. Displacement is the net change in position between two instants of time - it needs both a magnitude and a direction, measured from the starting position straight to the ending position.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Worked example: the athlete","body":"An athlete starts at O (0 m), runs to A (100 m), then runs back to B (40 m). Total distance = OA + AB = 100 m + 60 m = 160 m. But her displacement, from O to the final point B, is only 40 m in the positive direction - much smaller than the distance covered.","visual":"🏃"},{"title":"When are distance and displacement equal?","body":"For motion in a straight line without turning back - moving in one direction only - the total distance travelled and the magnitude of displacement are equal. The moment an object reverses direction, they start to differ, with distance always greater than or equal to the magnitude of displacement.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is displacement?","options":["The net change in position between two instants, including direction","The total path length covered by an object","The speed of an object at a single instant","The time taken to complete a journey"],"answer":0,"explain":"Displacement is the straight-line change in position from start to end, together with its direction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An athlete runs from O (0 m) to A (100 m), then back to B (40 m). What is the total distance travelled, in metres?","answer":160,"explain":"Distance = OA + AB = 100 m + 60 m = 160 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same athlete (O to A to B), what is the magnitude of the displacement, in metres?","answer":40,"explain":"Displacement is the net change in position from O (0 m) to the final point B (40 m), so it is 40 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Under what condition are total distance travelled and the magnitude of displacement equal?","options":["When the object moves in one direction only, without turning back","When the object returns exactly to its starting point","When the object moves in a circle","When the time interval is very small"],"answer":0,"explain":"If the object never reverses direction, every bit of path length adds directly to the net displacement."}]},{"id":"mo2","title":"Speeding Up the Story","icon":"⏱️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two rival messengers race to warn the same village from opposite ends of the kingdom. To know who is truly moving 'faster', and whether either is actually getting anywhere, you need two new tools: speed and velocity.","teach":[{"title":"Average speed","body":"Average speed is the total distance travelled divided by the time interval taken: average speed = total distance travelled / time interval. Since distance has no direction, speed has none either - it is a plain number.","visual":"🏎️"},{"title":"Average velocity","body":"Average velocity is the displacement divided by the time interval: v_av = s / t. Because displacement has direction, velocity does too, shown with a + or - sign for straight-line motion. Speed and velocity share the same SI unit, metre per second (m/s).","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Uniform vs non-uniform motion","body":"If an object covers equal distances in equal intervals of time, it is in uniform motion (constant speed). If it covers unequal distances in equal time intervals, its motion is non-uniform - speeding up, slowing down, or both.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Worked example: Sarang's swim","body":"Sarang swims one length of the pool and back in 50 s, covering 50 m of distance but ending up back where he started. His average speed = 50 m / 50 s = 1 m/s, but his average velocity = 0 m / 50 s = 0 m/s, since his displacement is zero.","visual":"🏊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Sarang swims to the far end of a pool and back, covering 50 m of total distance in 50 s. What is his average speed, in m/s?","answer":1,"explain":"Average speed = total distance / time = 50 m / 50 s = 1 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Since Sarang ends up exactly where he started, his displacement is 0 m over the 50 s swim. What is his average velocity, in m/s?","answer":0,"explain":"Average velocity = displacement / time = 0 m / 50 s = 0 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An object covers equal distances in equal intervals of time. What kind of motion is this?","options":["Uniform motion","Non-uniform motion","Circular motion","Accelerated motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal distances in equal time intervals means the speed stays constant - uniform motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of both average speed and average velocity?","options":["metre per second (m/s)","kilometre (km)","metre (m)","second (s)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both are distance-or-displacement divided by time, so their SI unit is m/s (also often expressed as km/h)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two postmen start walking towards each other from a distance of 210 yojanas apart. One covers 9 yojanas per day, the other 5 yojanas per day. In how many days will they meet?","answer":15,"explain":"Together they close 9 + 5 = 14 yojanas per day; 210 / 14 = 15 days."}]},{"id":"mo3","title":"The Jolt of Change","icon":"🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"The kingdom's fastest chariot isn't the one with the highest top speed - it's the one that changes speed the quickest. Time to measure exactly how fast velocity itself is changing.","teach":[{"title":"Average acceleration","body":"Average acceleration is the change in velocity divided by the time interval: average acceleration = (final velocity - initial velocity) / time interval, or a = (v - u) / (t2 - t1). Its SI unit is metre per second squared (m/s^2).","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Direction of acceleration","body":"If the magnitude of velocity is increasing, the acceleration points in the same direction as the velocity. If the magnitude of velocity is decreasing, the acceleration points opposite to the direction of velocity.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Worked example: the bus","body":"A bus speeds up from 36 km/h (10 m/s) to 54 km/h (15 m/s) in 10 s: a = (15-10)/10 = 0.5 m/s^2. Later it brakes from 54 km/h (15 m/s) to a stop in 5 s: a = (0-15)/5 = -3 m/s^2, the minus sign showing the acceleration is opposite to the direction of motion.","visual":"🚌"},{"title":"Free fall: a constant acceleration","body":"An object dropped from height gains 9.8 m/s of speed every second: 0, 9.8, 19.6, 29.4, 39.2 m/s at t = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 s. The average acceleration in every interval is the same, 9.8 m/s^2 - this constant is called the acceleration due to gravity, g.","visual":"🍎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A bus speeds up from 10 m/s to 15 m/s in 10 s as the driver presses the accelerator. What is the average acceleration, in m/s^2?","answer":0.5,"explain":"a = (v-u)/t = (15-10)/10 = 0.5 m/s^2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The same bus later brakes from 15 m/s to a stop (0 m/s) in 5 s. What is the average acceleration, in m/s^2? (Include the sign.)","answer":-3,"explain":"a = (0-15)/5 = -3 m/s^2; the minus sign shows it is opposite to the direction of velocity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A bus moves on a straight highway at a constant 25 m/s. What is its acceleration?","options":["Zero - acceleration depends on how quickly velocity changes, not on how fast the object is moving","25 m/s^2","Very high, since the speed is high","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acceleration depends only on the rate of change of velocity; at constant velocity that change is zero, however fast the object moves."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object falls freely and its velocity increases by the same amount every second. What is the magnitude of this constant acceleration due to gravity, in m/s^2?","answer":9.8,"explain":"Falling objects gain 9.8 m/s of speed every second; this constant is denoted g."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of average acceleration?","options":["m/s^2 (metre per second squared)","m/s (metre per second)","m (metre)","s (second)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity with time, so its unit is (m/s)/s = m/s^2."}]},{"id":"mo4","title":"Reading the Signs","icon":"📉","kind":"lesson","story":"The old cartographer's logbook is full of squiggly lines instead of words. To survive the next leg of the journey, you must learn to read position-time and velocity-time graphs like a map of motion itself.","teach":[{"title":"Position-time graphs","body":"A straight-line position-time graph means the object moves with constant velocity. A curved position-time graph means the velocity is changing, so the object is accelerating. A horizontal line (parallel to the time axis) means the object is at rest.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Slope equals velocity","body":"The slope of a position-time graph equals the average velocity: slope = (s2 - s1) / (t2 - t1). For example, if position is 40 m at t=2 s and 80 m at t=4 s, velocity = (80-40)/(4-2) = 20 m/s.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Slope of a velocity-time graph equals acceleration","body":"On a velocity-time graph, a horizontal line means zero acceleration (constant velocity). A straight sloped line means constant acceleration; its slope equals a = (v-u)/(t2-t1).","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Area under a velocity-time graph equals displacement","body":"The area enclosed between the velocity-time line and the time axis gives the displacement in that time interval. For a constant velocity of 20 m/s over 6 s, displacement = area of rectangle = 20 x 6 = 120 m. For changing velocity, the area splits into a rectangle plus a triangle.","visual":"🔺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On a position-time graph, the position is 40 m at t=2 s and 80 m at t=4 s. What is the velocity, in m/s?","answer":20,"explain":"Slope = (80-40)/(4-2) = 40/2 = 20 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A position-time graph is a straight horizontal line (parallel to the time axis). What does this tell you about the object?","options":["It is at rest - its position isn't changing with time","It is moving with constant acceleration","It is moving with increasing speed","It has zero displacement but is moving"],"answer":0,"explain":"An unchanging position over time means the object is stationary at that position."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car moves at a constant 20 m/s for 6 s. Using the area under its velocity-time graph, what is its displacement, in metres?","answer":120,"explain":"Area of the rectangle = velocity x time = 20 m/s x 6 s = 120 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car's velocity increases steadily from 5 m/s at t=10 s to 10 m/s at t=20 s. Using the area under the velocity-time graph (rectangle + triangle), what is its displacement between 10 s and 20 s, in metres?","answer":75,"explain":"Rectangle: 5 m/s x 10 s = 50 m. Triangle: 0.5 x 10 s x 5 m/s = 25 m. Total = 50 + 25 = 75 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What physical quantity does the slope of a velocity-time graph represent?","options":["Acceleration","Displacement","Distance travelled","Average speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The slope of a velocity-time graph is the rate of change of velocity with time, i.e., acceleration."}]},{"id":"mo5","title":"The Three Equations","icon":"🧮","kind":"lesson","story":"The alchemist's vault only opens for those who can predict exactly where a moving object will be, without watching it the whole way. Three kinematic equations are the key - as long as the acceleration stays constant.","teach":[{"title":"First equation: v = u + at","body":"For constant acceleration, the final velocity v equals the initial velocity u plus the acceleration a times the time t: v = u + at. This lets you find the velocity at any time if you know the starting velocity and acceleration.","visual":"1️⃣"},{"title":"Second equation: s = ut + (1/2)at^2","body":"The displacement s covered in time t is s = ut + (1/2)at^2. It comes from the area under the velocity-time graph, combining a rectangle (ut) and a triangle ((1/2)at^2).","visual":"2️⃣"},{"title":"Third equation: v^2 = u^2 + 2as","body":"Eliminating time from the first two equations gives v^2 = u^2 + 2as. This is useful when you know the velocities and acceleration but not the time - like in braking-distance problems.","visual":"3️⃣"},{"title":"Worked example: braking distance","body":"A car brakes with a = -4 m/s^2 from u = 15 m/s (54 km/h) to a stop (v = 0). Using v^2 = u^2 + 2as: 0 = 15^2 - 8s, so s = 225/8 = 28.1 m before it stops.","visual":"🛑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which equation correctly relates final velocity v to initial velocity u, constant acceleration a, and time t?","options":["v = u + at","v = u - at","v = ut + at","v = u x a x t"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the first kinematic equation for constant acceleration."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car starts from rest (u=0) and reaches a velocity of 24 m/s in 6 s. What is its average acceleration, in m/s^2?","answer":4,"explain":"a = (v-u)/t = (24-0)/6 = 4 m/s^2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using that same acceleration (4 m/s^2) and s = ut + (1/2)at^2, what distance (in metres) does the car cover in those 6 s?","answer":72,"explain":"s = 0x6 + 0.5x4x6^2 = 0 + 0.5x4x36 = 72 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car moving at 15 m/s (54 km/h) brakes with a constant acceleration of -4 m/s^2. Using v^2 = u^2 + 2as, how far (in metres) does it travel before stopping? (Round to 1 decimal place.)","answer":28.1,"explain":"0 = 15^2 + 2x(-4)xs, so s = 225/8 = 28.1 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which kinematic equation is most useful when you know u, v and a but NOT the time t?","options":["v^2 = u^2 + 2as","v = u + at","s = ut + (1/2)at^2","average speed = distance / time"],"answer":0,"explain":"v^2 = u^2 + 2as relates u, v, a and s without needing t at all."}]},{"id":"mo6","title":"Round and Round","icon":"🎡","kind":"lesson","story":"The final trail loops around an old stone well in a perfect circle. Your compass keeps spinning, speed never changing - yet something about your motion still feels off. Time to understand why going in circles counts as accelerating.","teach":[{"title":"Distance and displacement in a circle","body":"For an object moving around a circular path of radius R, the distance travelled in one full revolution equals the circumference, 2*pi*R. But the displacement after one complete revolution is zero, since the object returns to its starting point.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Average speed for one revolution","body":"If an object takes time T to complete one revolution, its average speed is: average speed = 2*pi*R / T. Its average velocity over that same revolution is zero, because the net displacement is zero.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Uniform circular motion","body":"When an object moves along a circular path at constant speed, its motion is called uniform circular motion. The velocity at any point is directed along the tangent to the circle at that point, in the direction of motion.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Why circular motion is accelerated","body":"Even though the speed stays constant in uniform circular motion, the direction of the velocity keeps changing continuously as the object moves around the circle. Since acceleration depends on any change in velocity - magnitude or direction - uniform circular motion is always accelerated motion.","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An object moves once around a circular path of radius R. What distance does it travel?","options":["2*pi*R (the circumference)","pi*R (half the circumference)","R (the radius)","Zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"One full revolution covers exactly the circumference of the circle, 2*pi*R."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the object's displacement after exactly one complete revolution?","options":["Zero, since it returns to its starting point","Equal to the circumference","Equal to the radius","Equal to the diameter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Displacement depends only on start and end position - after one full loop they are the same point, so displacement is zero."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At any point on a circular path, in what direction does the velocity point?","options":["Along the tangent to the circle at that point","Toward the centre of the circle","Away from the centre of the circle","Opposite to the direction of motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"The instantaneous velocity of an object on a circular path is always along the tangent at that point."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In uniform circular motion, the speed never changes. Is the motion accelerated?","options":["Yes - the direction of velocity keeps changing, and that alone counts as acceleration","No - acceleration requires the speed to change","No - acceleration is only possible in straight-line motion","Only if the radius is changing too"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acceleration arises from any change in velocity, including a change in direction even when speed is constant."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object moves around a circular path of radius 7 m and completes one revolution every 44 s. Using average speed = 2*pi*R/T (with pi = 22/7), what is its average speed in m/s?","answer":1,"explain":"average speed = 2 x (22/7) x 7 / 44 = 44/44 = 1 m/s."}]},{"id":"mo-boss","title":"The Motion Master","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Motion Master","taunt":"Speed won't save you here - only precise physics will."},"story":"At the heart of the old observatory stands the Motion Master, a guardian built from gears, graphs and equations, daring you to prove everything you've learned about distance, speed, acceleration, graphs, kinematic equations and circles - all at once.","teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"Distance vs displacement, average speed vs average velocity, average acceleration, position-time and velocity-time graphs, the three kinematic equations, and uniform circular motion - the Motion Master will test all of it.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A man walks from home to a shop 250 m away, realises he forgot something, walks back home, then walks to the shop again and back home once more. What is the total distance he travels, in metres?","answer":1000,"explain":"He covers the 250 m stretch four times in total: 250+250+250+250 = 1000 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Since the man ends up back at home where he started, what is his overall displacement?","options":["Zero, since his final position is the same as his starting position","1000 m","500 m","250 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Displacement depends only on the net change between the start and end position, which are the same point here."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A motorbike moving at 28 m/s with constant acceleration comes to a stop after travelling 98 m. Using v^2 = u^2 + 2as, what is the magnitude of its acceleration, in m/s^2?","answer":4,"explain":"0 = 28^2 + 2xax98, so a = -784/196 = -4 m/s^2; magnitude is 4 m/s^2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same motorbike (u=28 m/s, a=-4 m/s^2), how many seconds does it take to stop?","answer":7,"explain":"Using v=u+at: 0 = 28 + (-4)t, so t = 28/4 = 7 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A truck driver slows from 54 km/h (15 m/s) to 36 km/h (10 m/s) over 36 s with constant acceleration. Using s = ut + (1/2)at^2, what distance (in metres) does the truck cover in that time?","answer":450,"explain":"a = (10-15)/36 = -5/36 m/s^2; s = 15x36 + 0.5x(-5/36)x36^2 = 540-90 = 450 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car starts from rest, accelerates uniformly to 20 m/s in 5 s, cruises at 20 m/s for 10 s, then brakes uniformly to a stop in 6 s. What is the total distance travelled, in metres?","answer":310,"explain":"Phase 1: 0.5x(0+20)x5=50 m. Phase 2: 20x10=200 m. Phase 3: 0.5x(20+0)x6=60 m. Total = 50+200+60 = 310 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A girl rides a scooter with a perfectly constant speedometer reading. Could she still be accelerating?","options":["Yes - if she is turning, her velocity's direction is changing even though her speed is not","No - constant speed always means zero acceleration","No - acceleration only applies to objects that are speeding up","Yes, but only if she is also changing gears"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acceleration depends on any change in velocity, and turning changes the direction of velocity even at constant speed."}]}]},{"id":"mixtures9","index":4,"name":"Exploring Mixtures","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 5","emoji":"🧪","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"mi1","title":"Sorting the Mixture Lab","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Mixture Lab, where sugar dissolves into secrets and sand refuses to disappear. Your first task: tell the invisible from the visible, and sort every jar in the room.","teach":[{"title":"Homogeneous Mixtures (Solutions)","body":"A mixture with the same composition throughout is called a homogeneous mixture or a solution — a well-stirred sugar-water mixture tastes equally sweet in the first sip and the last. Vinegar (acetic acid in water) and soda (carbon dioxide in water) are other examples. A solution always stays homogeneous.","visual":"🥤"},{"title":"Heterogeneous Mixtures","body":"A mixture whose composition is not uniform throughout is called a heterogeneous mixture. In sand and water, the sand particles are clearly visible and settle over time — unlike in a true solution.","visual":"🏖️"},{"title":"Telling Them Apart","body":"Filtering a mixture and shining a laser through it can reveal its type: a solution leaves no residue on filter paper and lets light pass without scattering, while heterogeneous mixtures like chalk powder in water leave a residue and scatter the light beam visibly.","visual":"🔦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of a homogeneous mixture?","options":["Vinegar (acetic acid in water)","Sand and water","Chalk powder and water","Muddy water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vinegar is acetic acid uniformly dissolved in water, so it has the same composition throughout — it is a solution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A well-stirred cup of sugar and water tastes equally sweet from the first sip to the last. What kind of mixture is this?","options":["A homogeneous mixture (solution)","A heterogeneous mixture","A suspension","An alloy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Uniform composition throughout is the defining feature of a homogeneous mixture, or solution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a mixture of sand and water, what happens to the sand particles?","options":["They stay visible and settle with time","They dissolve completely","They form a solution","They become invisible"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sand does not dissolve; its particles remain visible and settle out, showing the mixture is heterogeneous."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How can you tell a solution apart from a heterogeneous mixture using filtration?","options":["A solution leaves no residue on the filter paper","A solution always leaves a residue","Both always leave a residue","Filtration cannot distinguish them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since a solution's solute is fully dissolved, filtering it leaves no residue on the paper, unlike a heterogeneous mixture."}]},{"id":"mi2","title":"The Concentration Chamber","icon":"⚗️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Concentration Chamber will not open until you get every mixture's recipe exactly right — too little pesticide and the crops wither, too much and the soil burns. Precision is your only way through.","teach":[{"title":"Solute, Solvent & Concentration","body":"In a solution, the substance that dissolves is the solute and the substance that does the dissolving is the solvent — in sugar water, sugar is the solute and water is the solvent. The amount of solute dissolved in a given amount of solvent or solution is called the concentration of the solution, and getting the right proportion matters, as in ORS or pesticide sprays.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Expressing Concentration as Percentage","body":"Mass by mass percentage (% m/m) gives grams of solute per 100 g of solution — used for both homogeneous mixtures and things like milk powder. Mass by volume percentage (% m/v) gives grams of solute per 100 mL of solution — used where measuring volume is easier, like medicines (a saline drip is 0.9% m/v salt in water). Volume by volume percentage (% v/v) gives millilitres of solute per 100 mL of solution — used for miscible liquids like vinegar's acetic acid.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Solubility and Saturation","body":"The maximum amount of solute that dissolves in a fixed quantity of solvent (100 g or 100 mL) at a given temperature is its solubility; a solution that cannot dissolve any more solute at that temperature is saturated. The solubility of a solid in a liquid generally increases with temperature, while the solubility of a gas in a liquid generally decreases as temperature rises.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If 10 g of salt is dissolved in 90 g of water, what is the mass by mass percentage (% m/m) of the solution?","answer":10,"explain":"Total mass of solution = 10 g + 90 g = 100 g; (10/100)×100 = 10% m/m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5 g of glucose is dissolved in water to make 100 mL of solution. What is its mass by volume percentage (% m/v)?","answer":5,"explain":"(5 g / 100 mL) × 100 = 5% m/v."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As temperature increases, how does the solubility of a gas in a liquid generally change?","options":["It generally decreases","It generally increases","It stays exactly the same","It becomes zero immediately"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unlike solids, gases dissolved in liquids generally become less soluble as temperature rises."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A saline drip used in hospitals is 0.9% m/v sodium chloride in water. How many grams of salt are in 100 mL of this solution?","options":["0.9 g","9 g","90 g","0.09 g"],"answer":0,"explain":"0.9% m/v means 0.9 g of solute is present in every 100 mL of solution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a saturated solution?","options":["A solution that cannot dissolve any more solute at that temperature","A solution with no solute at all","A solution that has been boiled","A solution that always contains a gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"A saturated solution has reached the maximum amount of solute it can hold at that particular temperature."}]},{"id":"mi3","title":"Refinery of Miscible Liquids","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Refinery Wing, four twin liquids and a rainbow-inked scroll block your path. Only by cooling, boiling, and letting a solvent creep up paper will you split each mixture into its true parts.","teach":[{"title":"Crystallization","body":"When a hot saturated solution is cooled, the solvent can hold less solute, so the excess separates out as pure solid crystals. Crystallization is used to separate two soluble solids when one is present in a small quantity, and to purify solids, based on differences in solubility at different temperatures.","visual":"💎"},{"title":"Distillation","body":"Distillation separates two miscible liquids by heating the mixture so the liquid with the lower boiling point vaporises first; the vapour is then cooled in a condenser back into a pure liquid. It works when the boiling points differ by at least about 25°C, as with acetone (56°C) and water (100°C), and can also recover a liquid from a solution of a dissolved solid.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Fractional Distillation","body":"Fractional distillation separates components of a mixture whose boiling points differ by less than about 25°C. It is used in petroleum refineries to split crude oil into petroleum gas, petrol, kerosene, diesel and other fractions.","visual":"🛢️"},{"title":"Paper Chromatography","body":"Paper chromatography separates the components of a mixture, such as the dyes in ink, using differences in how each component interacts with the solvent and the paper — the solvent carries the substances up the paper, and components that move at different rates separate into different spots.","visual":"🖍️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A saturated solution of compound B at 60°C contains 287 g of B per 100 g of water. At 40°C, the solubility of B drops to 241 g per 100 g water. How many grams of B crystallize out per 100 g of water on cooling from 60°C to 40°C?","answer":46,"explain":"287 g − 241 g = 46 g of compound B separates out as crystals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the minimum difference in boiling points generally needed for simple distillation to separate two miscible liquids?","options":["About 25°C","About 5°C","About 100°C","There is no minimum requirement"],"answer":0,"explain":"Simple distillation works well when boiling points differ by at least about 25°C, as with acetone and water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fractional distillation is used in petroleum refineries because crude oil's components have:","options":["Boiling point differences smaller than about 25°C","Boiling point differences larger than 100°C","Exactly the same boiling point","No boiling points at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fractional distillation separates components whose boiling points differ by less than roughly 25°C, which is the case for petroleum fractions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In paper chromatography, what actually separates the different coloured components of an ink spot?","options":["Differences in how the components interact with the solvent and paper as the solvent moves up","Differences in the colour of the ink alone","The weight of the paper strip","Boiling the ink on the paper"],"answer":0,"explain":"Components move at different rates up the paper depending on their interaction with the solvent and paper, which separates them into distinct spots."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the underlying principle behind purifying a solid by crystallization?","options":["Differences in the solubility of a substance at different temperatures","Differences in boiling points of liquids","Differences in particle size only","Differences in colour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Crystallization purification relies on how a substance's solubility changes with temperature."}]},{"id":"mi4","title":"The Alchemist's Vault","icon":"🪨","kind":"lesson","story":"The Alchemist's Vault holds oil floating on water, ghostly camphor vapours, and shining metal blends that refuse to unmix. Master the separating funnel and the sublimation flame to crack it open.","teach":[{"title":"Immiscible Liquids & the Separating Funnel","body":"Liquids that do not mix, like mustard oil and water, are called immiscible liquids and form separate layers — the less dense oil floats on top of water. A separating funnel lets you drain off the denser lower layer (water) through its stopcock, leaving the oil behind, so the two liquids are collected separately.","visual":"🧴"},{"title":"Sublimation","body":"Some solids, like camphor, change directly from solid to vapour on heating (below their melting point) without passing through the liquid state — this is sublimation. On cooling, the vapour turns directly back into a solid, called deposition. A mixture of camphor and sand can be separated this way, since sand does not sublime; naphthalene and dry ice (solid carbon dioxide) also sublime.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Alloys","body":"When metals are melted together and cooled, they can form a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals (or a metal and non-metal) called an alloy, such as brass (about 80% copper, 20% zinc), bronze (about 80% copper, 20% tin) or stainless steel (iron with carbon, chromium, nickel and molybdenum). Alloys cannot be separated back into their components by physical methods.","visual":"🪙"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When mustard oil and water are mixed in a separating funnel and left to stand, what do you observe?","options":["Two separate layers form, with oil on top and water below","They mix completely into one layer","Water floats on top of the oil","Nothing happens, they stay mixed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oil and water are immiscible; the less dense mustard oil forms the upper layer and water forms the lower layer."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can camphor be separated from sand by heating the mixture?","options":["Camphor sublimes directly into vapour, while sand does not","Sand melts and camphor stays solid","Camphor dissolves in sand","Both camphor and sand sublime equally"],"answer":0,"explain":"Camphor changes directly from solid to vapour on heating, leaving the non-subliming sand behind, so the vapour can be collected and re-deposited elsewhere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is an alloy?","options":["A homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal","A heterogeneous mixture of two liquids","A mixture that can be separated by filtration","A type of colloid found only in blood"],"answer":0,"explain":"An alloy, such as brass or bronze, is a homogeneous mixture formed by melting metals together, and it cannot be separated by physical methods."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Brass is an alloy made of approximately what composition?","options":["80% copper and 20% zinc","80% copper and 20% tin","50% iron and 50% carbon","100% copper"],"answer":0,"explain":"Brass is approximately 80% copper and 20% zinc, while bronze (80% copper, 20% tin) is a different alloy."}]},{"id":"mi5","title":"The Shimmering Colloid Chamber","icon":"🧈","kind":"lesson","story":"One last chamber shimmers strangely — glasses of muddy water, milk, and blood that never quite settle. Spin them, coagulate them, and shine a light through to reveal what is really dispersed inside.","teach":[{"title":"Suspensions","body":"In a suspension, solid particles do not dissolve but remain suspended throughout the liquid — they are larger than solution particles, visible to the naked eye, and eventually settle if left undisturbed. Muddy water, sawdust in water and tea leaves in water are examples of suspensions.","visual":"🥛"},{"title":"Centrifugation & Coagulation","body":"Centrifugation spins a mixture at high speed so centrifugal force pushes the heavier particles outward and down, while the lighter liquid stays on top — it is used to separate blood into red blood cells and plasma. Coagulation uses a substance called a coagulant, such as alum in muddy water or acid in milk (to make cheese), to make fine suspended particles clump together so they settle and can be removed by decantation or filtration.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Colloids","body":"A colloid, like milk, tomato sauce, ice cream or blood, is neither a true solution nor a suspension — its particles (1–1000 nm) are larger than a solution's (less than 1 nm) but smaller than a suspension's (more than 1000 nm), and they stay uniformly dispersed without settling. The dissolved-looking component is the dispersed phase, and the component it is spread through is the dispersion medium; when both are liquids, the colloid is called an emulsion, like milk (oil-in-water) or butter (water-in-oil).","visual":"🧈"},{"title":"The Tyndall Effect","body":"When light passes through a colloid or a suspension, its particles scatter the light and make the beam's path visible — this scattering is called the Tyndall effect, named after scientist John Tyndall. It does not happen in a true solution, because solution particles are too small to scatter light; you can see it in floodlights at a stadium or sunlight through dusty air.","visual":"🔦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to the path of a laser beam passed through a true solution, compared to a suspension?","options":["The path is not visible in a solution, but is visible in a suspension","The path is visible in both equally","The path is visible only in the solution","Neither solution nor suspension shows a visible path"],"answer":0,"explain":"Solution particles are too small to scatter light, so the beam's path is invisible, while suspension particles scatter light and make the path visible (the Tyndall effect)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Roughly what particle size range is found in a colloid?","options":["1 – 1000 nm","Less than 1 nm","More than 1000 nm","Exactly 1 mm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Colloid particles range from 1 to 1000 nm, larger than solution particles (<1 nm) but smaller than suspension particles (>1000 nm)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"During centrifugation, what happens to the heavier particles in a spinning mixture?","options":["They move outward and settle at the bottom of the tube","They rise to the top","They dissolve completely","They stay exactly where they started"],"answer":0,"explain":"Centrifugal force during high-speed spinning pushes heavier particles outward, where they settle at the bottom, while lighter liquid stays on top."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What role does alum play when added to muddy water?","options":["It acts as a coagulant, making fine particles clump together and settle","It dissolves the mud completely","It evaporates the water","It bleaches the colour of the mud"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alum is a coagulant — it causes suspended particles to clump into larger masses that settle by sedimentation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a colloid like milk, what are the dispersed phase and dispersion medium called when both are liquids?","options":["An emulsion","A suspension","A solution","An alloy"],"answer":0,"explain":"When both the dispersed phase and dispersion medium of a colloid are liquids, it is called an emulsion, such as milk (oil-in-water)."}]},{"id":"mi-boss","title":"The Great Separator","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"All five chambers have opened, but the Great Separator himself blocks the exit — a shape-shifting mixture of everything you have learned. Prove your mastery of solutions, separations and colloids to pass.","boss":{"name":"The Great Separator","taunt":"Sort me, dissolve me, spin me down — prove you truly understand mixtures!"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a homogeneous mixture?","options":["Sugar dissolved in water","Sand in water","Chalk powder in water","Oil in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sugar solution has uniform composition throughout, making it homogeneous."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 mL of a liquid pesticide is mixed with water to make 100 mL of spray solution. What is its volume by volume percentage (% v/v)?","answer":1,"explain":"(1 mL / 100 mL) × 100 = 1% v/v."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which separation technique is used to obtain pure salt crystals from a saturated salt solution?","options":["Crystallization","Sublimation","Centrifugation","Chromatography"],"answer":0,"explain":"Crystallization separates a pure solid from a saturated solution based on solubility differences with temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can distillation separate acetone (boiling point 56°C) from water (boiling point 100°C)?","options":["Their boiling points differ by enough (about 44°C) for one to vaporise well before the other","Their boiling points are identical","Acetone does not boil at all","Water evaporates instantly at room temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"A large boiling point difference lets acetone vaporise first, allowing it to be collected separately by condensation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What causes the Tyndall effect?","options":["Scattering of light by particles in a colloid or suspension","Absorption of light by a true solution","Reflection of light off a mirror","Refraction of light through pure water only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Tyndall effect is the scattering of light by dispersed particles in colloids and suspensions, making a light beam's path visible."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How are the components of blood, such as red blood cells and plasma, typically separated?","options":["Centrifugation","Sublimation","Simple distillation","Paper chromatography"],"answer":0,"explain":"Blood components are separated by spinning at high speed in a centrifuge, which pushes heavier cells outward and down."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A mixture of camphor and sand is heated gently. What happens?","options":["Camphor sublimes into vapour, leaving sand behind","Both camphor and sand melt","Sand sublimes, leaving camphor behind","Neither substance changes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Camphor sublimes directly from solid to vapour on heating, while sand does not, allowing the two to be separated."}]}]},{"id":"forces9","index":5,"name":"How Forces Affect Motion","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 6","emoji":"⚡","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"fo1","title":"The Concept of Force","icon":"👊","kind":"lesson","story":"You wake up in the Hall of Motion, where every statue is frozen mid-action — a kicked ball, a struck coin, a squeezed lemon. A plaque reads: nothing here moves without a push or a pull. To unlock the hall's exit, you must master the basic language of force.","teach":[{"title":"Force changes motion and shape","body":"A force can make an object at rest start moving, change the speed or direction of a moving object, or change its shape. Kicking a ball, striking it with a bat, and squeezing a lemon are all everyday examples of applying a force.","visual":"👊"},{"title":"Force needs a direction","body":"Force is a physical quantity that must be described with both magnitude and direction, just like position, displacement, velocity and acceleration. If either the magnitude or the direction of a force changes, the effect of the force also changes.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Measuring force: the newton","body":"The SI unit of force is the newton, symbol N. A spring balance can be used to measure the magnitude of a force, including weight, which is simply the gravitational force with which the Earth pulls an object.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of force?","options":["newton (N)","joule (J)","watt (W)","pascal (Pa)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Force is measured in newtons, symbol N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which instrument can be used to measure the magnitude of a force, including weight?","options":["A spring balance","A thermometer","A stopwatch","A measuring tape"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pulling on a spring balance shows the force with which you pull on the spring inside it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why must force always be described along with a direction?","options":["Because force is a physical quantity that has both magnitude and direction","Because force only exists when moving north","Because the newton is a directional unit by definition","Direction does not actually matter for force"],"answer":0,"explain":"Like velocity and acceleration, force needs both a magnitude and a direction to be fully described."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ball at rest suddenly starts moving. What must have happened?","options":["A force was applied to it","It changed shape on its own","Time passed and it moved by itself","Nothing needs to happen for this"],"answer":0,"explain":"A force is needed to make a stationary object start moving."}]},{"id":"fo2","title":"Balanced Forces and Friction","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"The hall opens into a tug-of-war arena where two ghostly teams strain against a rope that refuses to move. Beyond them, a stone box sits stubbornly on the floor no matter how hard you shove it. Something invisible is fighting back — and you must learn its rules before you can pass.","teach":[{"title":"Balanced forces","body":"When two forces acting on an object are equal in magnitude but opposite in direction, they are called balanced forces. In a tug of war, if both teams pull with equal force, the rope does not move because the forces are balanced.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Unbalanced forces: opposite directions","body":"If the forces applied on an object are not balanced, a non-zero net force acts on it. When two forces are opposite in direction but unequal in magnitude, the net force equals the difference between their magnitudes, acting in the direction of the larger force.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Unbalanced forces: same direction","body":"When two forces act in the same direction on an object, the magnitude of the net force is the sum of the magnitudes of the two forces, and its direction is the same as that of the individual forces.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"The force of friction","body":"Friction is a force that acts on an object in the direction opposite to its motion, arising between two surfaces in contact. A box being pushed will only start moving once the applied force is larger than the force of friction, so that a net force acts in the direction of motion.","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two forces of 10 N and 6 N act on a block in the same direction. What is the net force in N?","answer":16,"explain":"Forces in the same direction add: 10 N + 6 N = 16 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two forces of 10 N and 6 N act on a block in opposite directions. What is the magnitude of the net force in N?","answer":4,"explain":"Forces in opposite directions subtract: 10 N - 6 N = 4 N, acting along the larger force."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a tug of war, both teams pull with exactly equal force and the rope stays still. These two forces are called:","options":["balanced forces","unbalanced forces","net forces","frictional forces"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal and opposite forces that produce no motion are called balanced forces."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A student pushes a stationary box but it refuses to move. What must be true about the force of friction?","options":["It is equal to or greater than the applied force","It is exactly zero","It acts in the same direction as the push","It only exists once the box starts moving"],"answer":0,"explain":"The box stays still because friction balances or exceeds the applied push."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A moving box is no longer pushed, yet it gradually slows down and stops. What causes this?","options":["The force of friction acting opposite to its motion","The force of gravity acting sideways","The normal force pushing it backward","The applied force, which secretly continues by itself"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no applied force left, friction alone acts against the motion and brings the box to rest."}]},{"id":"fo3","title":"Newton's First Law: Inertia","icon":"🛑","kind":"lesson","story":"A spectral figure in 17th-century robes blocks your path, holding a perfectly smooth glass sphere that neither speeds up nor slows down as it rolls forever. 'I am Sir Isaac,' he says, 'and nothing changes its motion here without cause. Prove you understand inertia, and the sphere's rule is yours.'","teach":[{"title":"Newton's first law of motion","body":"Newton's first law of motion states: An object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues to move with a constant velocity, unless a net force acts upon the object.","visual":"🛑"},{"title":"The idea of inertia","body":"Newton used the word 'inertia' to describe the tendency of objects to resist a change in their state of rest or uniform motion. Earlier, Galileo Galilei had argued through thought experiments that a body moving on a horizontal plane, with all impediments to its motion removed, would continue moving indefinitely.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Zero net force means zero acceleration","body":"If the net force acting on an object is zero, the object cannot begin to move or change its velocity — its acceleration is zero. For example, if a person pushes a moving box forward with a force exactly equal to friction, the two forces balance and the box continues moving at constant velocity.","visual":"⚙️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's first law of motion states that an object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues moving with constant velocity, unless:","options":["a net force acts upon it","it changes shape","time stops passing","friction becomes exactly zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the exact statement of Newton's first law of motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What word did Newton use for an object's tendency to resist a change in its state of rest or motion?","options":["Inertia","Momentum","Acceleration","Friction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Newton used the word inertia to frame his first law of motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A person pushes a moving box forward with a force exactly equal to the force of friction acting on it. What happens to the box?","options":["It continues moving with constant velocity","It accelerates forward","It stops immediately","It starts moving backward"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two forces balance, so the net force is zero and the box keeps its constant velocity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the net force acting on an object is zero, its acceleration is:","options":["zero","constant but non-zero","always increasing","equal to its velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"A zero net force means zero acceleration, as stated by Newton's first law."}]},{"id":"fo4","title":"Newton's Second Law: F = ma","icon":"🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"Sir Isaac hands you a cart, a pulley and a set of weights. 'Pull harder,' he says, 'and watch how quickly it speeds up. Load it heavier, and watch it resist.' The relationship between force, mass and acceleration is the key that will launch you toward the chapter's final trial.","teach":[{"title":"Newton's second law of motion","body":"Newton's second law states: when a net force acts on an object, the object accelerates in the direction of the net force. The magnitude of the acceleration is proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to the mass of the object. Mathematically, a = F/m, or F = ma.","visual":"🚀"},{"title":"Defining the newton","body":"Using F = ma with m = 1 kg and a = 1 m/s², we get F = 1 kg × 1 m/s² = 1 N. So one newton is defined as the force that produces an acceleration of 1 m/s² on an object of mass 1 kg.","visual":"1️⃣"},{"title":"Weight: F = mg","body":"Under the influence of gravitational force, an object falls toward the Earth with acceleration g. Using F = ma, the gravitational force (weight) acting on an object of mass m is F = mg, where g = 9.8 m/s² near the Earth's surface (often taken as 10 m/s² for quick estimates).","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Momentum and the fuller form","body":"Momentum is defined as the product of an object's mass and velocity, and points in the same direction as the velocity. The more complete form of Newton's second law states that the rate of change of momentum of an object is proportional to the net force and takes place in the direction in which the net force acts.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A weightlifter holds a barbell of total mass 30 kg steady overhead. Using g = 9.8 m/s², what force in N must she apply upward?","answer":294,"explain":"F = mg = 30 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 294 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 25 kg block is pushed with a net force of 5 N (after friction is subtracted). What is its acceleration in m/s²?","answer":0.2,"explain":"a = F/m = 5 N / 25 kg = 0.2 m/s²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sports car of mass 1500 kg accelerates at 2 m/s². What net force in N acts on it?","answer":3000,"explain":"F = ma = 1500 kg × 2 m/s² = 3000 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One newton is defined as the force that produces an acceleration of:","options":["1 m/s² on an object of mass 1 kg","1 m/s² on an object of mass 10 kg","10 m/s² on an object of mass 1 kg","1 m/s on an object of mass 1 kg"],"answer":0,"explain":"F = ma gives 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m/s²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for the gravitational force (weight) acting on an object of mass m?","options":["F = mg","F = ma alone, without gravity","F = m/g","F = g/m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weight is the gravitational force, given by F = mg."}]},{"id":"fo5","title":"Newton's Third Law and Systems","icon":"🤜","kind":"lesson","story":"A canoeist glides past on a mirrored river, paddling backward against the water yet somehow moving forward. Beside her, two boxes tied by a string are dragged together across frictionless ice. Every action here has an equal and opposite reaction — learn to read the pairs, and the river's secret path opens.","teach":[{"title":"Newton's third law of motion","body":"Newton's third law states: whenever one object is exerting a force on a second object, the second object is simultaneously exerting an equal and opposite force on the first object. These forces always occur in pairs but act on two different objects.","visual":"🤜🤛"},{"title":"Why action-reaction pairs don't cancel","body":"Because the two forces of a third-law pair act on different objects, they do not balance each other, even though they are equal and opposite. This is why a canoeist pushing water backwards with a paddle makes the canoe move forward — the water pushes the paddle forward with an equal force.","visual":"🛶"},{"title":"Equal forces, unequal accelerations","body":"Even though the two forces in a third-law pair are always equal in magnitude, they do not generally produce equal accelerations, because a = F/m and the masses of the two interacting objects may be very different.","visual":"🔫"},{"title":"Treating connected objects as a system","body":"When two objects are connected, like two boxes joined by a string and pulled by an external force F, they can be treated as a single system of combined mass (m1 + m2). The internal force (tension) can be ignored, and the acceleration of the system is a = F / (m1 + m2).","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's third law of motion states that whenever one object exerts a force on a second object:","options":["the second object exerts an equal and opposite force on the first object","the second object exerts a smaller force back","no force acts on the first object at all","both objects immediately come to rest"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forces always occur in equal and opposite pairs acting on two different objects."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A 0.1 kg bullet is fired from a 5 kg gun with a force of 2 N. What is the recoil force on the gun?","options":["2 N","0.1 N","5 N","10 N"],"answer":0,"explain":"By Newton's third law, the recoil force on the gun equals the 2 N force on the bullet."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the gun scenario above (2 N recoil force, gun mass 5 kg), what is the magnitude of the gun's initial acceleration in m/s²?","answer":0.4,"explain":"a = F/m = 2 N / 5 kg = 0.4 m/s²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same 2 N force acting on the 0.1 kg bullet, what is the bullet's initial acceleration in m/s²?","answer":20,"explain":"a = F/m = 2 N / 0.1 kg = 20 m/s²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two boxes of mass m1 and m2 are connected by a string and pulled by an external force F on a frictionless surface. Treated as a single system, what is its acceleration?","options":["F / (m1 + m2)","F × (m1 + m2)","(m1 + m2) / F","F/m1 - F/m2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The system of combined mass (m1 + m2) accelerates as a = F / (m1 + m2), since internal tension forces cancel out."}]},{"id":"fo-boss","title":"The Final Trial: Sir Isaac's Colossus","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The three laws converge into a towering Colossus of stone and gears, guarding the exit of the Hall of Motion. It will not fall to a single push — only a mind that has mastered force, balance, inertia, and action-reaction together can bring it down.","boss":{"name":"The Inertia Colossus","taunt":"Apply your force wisely, mortal — I do not accelerate for the unprepared!"},"teach":[{"title":"At a glance: friction","body":"The force of friction acts on an object in the direction opposite to its direction of motion.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"At a glance: the three laws","body":"First law: an object at rest remains at rest, and an object in motion continues with constant velocity, unless a net force acts upon it. Second law: a net force accelerates an object in its own direction, with acceleration proportional to force and inversely proportional to mass (F = ma). Third law: whenever one object exerts a force on a second object, the second exerts an equal and opposite force back on the first.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"At a glance: systems","body":"Connected objects, like two boxes joined by a string, can be treated as one system of combined mass. Internal forces cancel out, and only external forces determine the system's acceleration.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A toy car of mass 100 g moves with a constant velocity of 0.5 m/s on a frictionless surface. What is the net force acting on it, in N?","answer":0,"explain":"Constant velocity means zero acceleration, so by F = ma, the net force is 0 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a tug of war, two teams pull with equal and opposite forces and the rope does not move. This demonstrates that:","options":["balanced forces produce no acceleration","unbalanced forces are acting","Newton's third law forbids the rope from moving","friction between the teams and ground is zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal and opposite forces are balanced forces, so there is no net force and no acceleration."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 50 g (0.05 kg) bullet moving at 100 m/s enters a heavy stationary block and stops after penetrating 0.5 m, with constant deceleration. What is the magnitude of the stopping force on the bullet, in N?","answer":500,"explain":"Using v^2 = u^2 - 2as: 0 = 100^2 - 2a(0.5), so a = 10000 m/s^2. Then F = ma = 0.05 kg x 10000 m/s^2 = 500 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a cricket fielder pull their hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball?","options":["It increases the time to stop the ball, reducing the force needed","It increases the force needed to stop the ball faster","It decreases the mass of the ball","It has no physical effect on the catch"],"answer":0,"explain":"A longer stopping time means a smaller acceleration, and therefore a smaller force, on both the ball and the fielder's hands."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a canoeist paddles, pushing water backward, what pushes the canoe forward?","options":["The water pushing the paddle forward with an equal and opposite force","The paddle pushing itself forward","Friction between the canoeist and the seat","The buoyant force alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"By Newton's third law, the water pushes back on the paddle with an equal and opposite force, moving the canoe forward."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A weightlifter holds a barbell of total mass 20 kg steady overhead. Using g = 9.8 m/s², what upward force in N must be applied?","answer":196,"explain":"F = mg = 20 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 196 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two boxes connected by a string are pulled by an external force F. Which force should be ignored when finding the acceleration of the whole system?","options":["Internal forces, like the string's tension","The external force F itself","Gravity acting on the boxes","The normal force from the ground"],"answer":0,"explain":"Internal forces like tension act within the system and cancel out; only external forces determine the system's acceleration."}]}]},{"id":"workenergy9","index":6,"name":"Work, Energy and Simple Machines","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 7","emoji":"⚙️","palette":{"from":"#2dd4bf","to":"#134e4a"},"lessons":[{"id":"we1","title":"The Meaning of Work","icon":"🏋️","kind":"lesson","story":"You creep into the Ruined Foundry, where an ancient door is carved with a warning: 'Only true WORK shall open me.' Shoving, shouting, and straining against solid stone teaches you fast — the Foundry only counts force that actually moves something.","teach":[{"title":"Work Done by a Force","body":"The work done on an object by a constant force is defined as: work done = force applied × displacement in the direction of the force, or W = F × s. The SI unit of work is the joule (J), where 1 J = 1 N × 1 m.","visual":"🏋️"},{"title":"When Work is Zero","body":"No work is done if the force is zero, or if there is no displacement (such as pushing against a rigid wall that doesn't move). Work is also zero if the force acts perpendicular to the displacement, such as the upward force a girl applies while carrying a box that moves horizontally.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Positive and Negative Work","body":"When the displacement is in the same direction as the applied force, the work done is positive — like pushing a wheelchair forward. When the displacement is opposite to the force, the work done is negative — like a goalkeeper's hand pushing back against a ball to stop it.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for work done by a constant force?","options":["W = F × s","W = F + s","W = F / s","W = F × s²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Work done = force applied × displacement in the direction of the force."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A boy pushes hard against a rigid wall, but it does not move at all. How much work does he do on the wall?","options":["Zero, because there is no displacement","Positive, because he applied force","Negative work","It depends on how hard he pushes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the displacement s = 0, the work done W = F × s is zero, even though the boy feels tired."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A girl carries a box horizontally, applying an upward force to support its weight while it moves sideways. What is the work done by her upward force on the box?","options":["Zero, since the force is perpendicular to the displacement","Positive, equal to the box's weight times distance","Negative","Equal to mgh"],"answer":0,"explain":"When force and displacement are perpendicular, there is no displacement in the direction of the force, so the work done is zero."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"While stopping a ball, a goalkeeper's hand moves back by 0.15 m in a direction opposite to a force of 200 N that she applies. Using W = F × s with the displacement taken as negative, what is the work done (in J) by the goalkeeper on the ball?","answer":-30,"explain":"W = 200 N × (–0.15 m) = –30 J; the work is negative because the force and displacement are in opposite directions."}]},{"id":"we2","title":"Waking the Spark-Golem","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Foundry, a dormant Spark-Golem stands drained of all energy. Legend says only work poured into it — through motion — can wake it, and reveal just how dangerous something moving fast can become.","teach":[{"title":"The Work-Energy Theorem","body":"An object having the capacity to do work is said to possess energy. The work-energy theorem states: work done on an object = change in its energy. When positive work is done on an object, it gains energy; when negative work is done, it loses energy.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Kinetic Energy","body":"The energy possessed by an object due to its motion is called kinetic energy. For an object of mass m starting from rest and reaching velocity v, the kinetic energy is K = ½mv². The SI unit of kinetic energy is the joule (J).","visual":"🏃"},{"title":"Kinetic Energy and Speed","body":"Because K = ½mv² depends on the square of the velocity, doubling an object's speed makes its kinetic energy four times larger, not just double.","visual":"🚀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of kinetic energy?","options":["joule (J)","watt (W)","newton (N)","kilogram (kg)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kinetic energy has the same SI unit as work and energy: the joule (J)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the velocity of a moving vehicle doubles in magnitude, what happens to its kinetic energy?","options":["It becomes 4 times the original value","It becomes 2 times the original value","It stays the same","It becomes half the original value"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since K = ½mv², doubling v means K increases by a factor of 2² = 4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A jet aircraft lands and a wire applies a constant backward force over a distance to stop it. What can we say about the work done by the wire on the jet?","options":["It is negative, since the force opposes the jet's displacement","It is positive","It is zero","It cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"The wire's force is opposite to the jet's motion, so it does negative work, reducing the jet's kinetic energy to zero."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ball of mass 2 kg moves with a velocity of 10 m/s. Using K = ½mv², calculate its kinetic energy in joules.","answer":100,"explain":"K = ½ × 2 kg × (10 m/s)² = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 J."}]},{"id":"we3","title":"The Pendulum Chasm","icon":"🎢","kind":"lesson","story":"A chasm splits your path, spanned only by a great swinging pendulum and a boulder poised on a high ledge above a sand pit. To cross safely, you must trust one rule: energy never vanishes, it only changes form.","teach":[{"title":"Potential Energy","body":"Energy stored by an object due to its deformation, or in a system of objects due to their relative positions, is called potential energy. For an object of mass m raised to a height h above the ground, the gravitational potential energy is U = mgh.","visual":"⛰️"},{"title":"Conservation of Mechanical Energy","body":"The sum of an object's kinetic energy and potential energy is called its mechanical energy. As an object falls freely under gravity with no other forces acting, its potential energy decreases while its kinetic energy increases by an equal amount, so its total mechanical energy stays constant — this is the conservation of mechanical energy.","visual":"🎢"},{"title":"Speed at the Bottom of a Slide","body":"When an object slides down a frictionless slope, all its potential energy mgh converts to kinetic energy ½mv² at the bottom. Equating these gives v = √(2gh) — the speed depends only on the height h, not on the object's mass or the shape of the slide.","visual":"🛝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for the gravitational potential energy of an object of mass m at height h?","options":["U = mgh","U = ½mv²","U = mg/h","U = mgh²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Potential energy near Earth's surface is U = mgh, the work done in raising the object to height h."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A pendulum bob released from point P swings down through the lowest point Q. At point Q, the bob has:","options":["Maximum kinetic energy and (taking Q as reference) zero potential energy","Zero kinetic energy","Maximum potential energy","Zero mechanical energy"],"answer":0,"explain":"At the lowest point, all the potential energy the bob had at P has converted into kinetic energy."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ball of mass 0.2 kg is held 10 m above the ground. Using U = mgh with g = 10 m/s², calculate its potential energy in joules.","answer":20,"explain":"U = mgh = 0.2 kg × 10 m/s² × 10 m = 20 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A child slides down a frictionless slide of height 5 m. Using v = √(2gh) with g = 10 m/s², what is the child's speed in m/s at the bottom?","answer":10,"explain":"v = √(2 × 10 × 5) = √100 = 10 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why doesn't the speed of a child at the bottom of a frictionless slide depend on the child's mass or the shape of the slide?","options":["Because equating mgh = ½mv² cancels out the mass, leaving v = √(2gh)","Because friction removes all mass-dependence","Because power is independent of mass","Because kinetic energy is always constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mass m appears on both sides of mgh = ½mv² and cancels, so only h (and g) determine v."}]},{"id":"we4","title":"Racing the Closing Gate","icon":"⏱️","kind":"lesson","story":"Your torch is guttering and the exit gate above the Foundry has begun to grind shut. You have the strength to do the work needed to escape — the only question left is whether you can do it fast enough.","teach":[{"title":"Power","body":"Power is defined as the rate at which work is done. The average power P is the work done W divided by the time taken t, i.e., P = W / t. The SI unit of power is the watt (W), where 1 W = 1 J per second (1 W = 1 J s⁻¹).","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Comparing Power","body":"Doing more work in the same time interval requires more power, and doing the same amount of work in a shorter time also requires more power. Another unit, horsepower (hp), is used for engines, where 1 hp = 746 W.","visual":"🐎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for power?","options":["P = W / t","P = W × t","P = F × t","P = W / F"],"answer":0,"explain":"Power is the rate of doing work: P = work done ÷ time taken."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One watt is equal to:","options":["1 joule per second","1 newton per second","1 joule per metre","1 newton per metre"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 W = 1 J s⁻¹, the rate of doing one joule of work every second."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A weightlifter lifts a 75 kg mass by 2 m in 5 seconds (g = 10 m/s²). First find the work done (W = mgh), then the power required in watts (P = W/t).","answer":300,"explain":"Work = 75 kg × 10 m/s² × 2 m = 1500 J; Power = 1500 J / 5 s = 300 W."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A motor does 20000 J of work in 10 seconds. What is its power output in watts?","answer":2000,"explain":"P = W / t = 20000 J / 10 s = 2000 W."}]},{"id":"we5","title":"Machines that Multiply Muscle","icon":"🔧","kind":"lesson","story":"The final gate is sealed by a boulder far too heavy to lift by hand. Scattered around the chamber lie a dusty pulley, a long plank, and an old iron bar — the Foundry's builders clearly expected visitors to get clever.","teach":[{"title":"Simple Machines and Mechanical Advantage","body":"Simple machines help us do tasks by changing the magnitude or direction of the force we need to apply, though the total work required for a task cannot be reduced. The force we apply is called the effort, and the force to be overcome is called the load. Mechanical advantage = load / effort.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Pulley","body":"A pulley is a wheel with a groove that guides a rope. A fixed pulley changes only the direction of the effort, not its magnitude, so its mechanical advantage is 1. A movable pulley or system of pulleys can have a mechanical advantage greater than 1, lifting heavier loads with smaller effort.","visual":"🪢"},{"title":"Inclined Plane","body":"For an inclined plane of length L used to raise a load mg to height h, the effort F′ needed satisfies F′ × L = mgh, so mechanical advantage = load/effort = mg/F′ = L/h. Since L is larger than h, the effort required is less than mg.","visual":"🛤️"},{"title":"Lever","body":"A lever is a rigid bar that rotates about a fixed point called the fulcrum. It balances when effort × effort arm = load × load arm, i.e., F1 × d1 = F2 × d2. Its mechanical advantage = load/effort = effort arm/load arm.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the mechanical advantage of a fixed pulley?","options":["1","Greater than 1","Less than 1","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fixed pulley only changes the direction of the effort, not its magnitude, so load and effort are equal and mechanical advantage = 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an inclined plane of length L raising a load to height h, the mechanical advantage (load/effort) equals:","options":["L / h","h / L","L × h","L − h"],"answer":0,"explain":"From F′ × L = mgh, mechanical advantage = mg/F′ = L/h."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An inclined plane has a length of 4 m and raises a load to a height of 2 m. Using mechanical advantage = L/h, what is its mechanical advantage?","answer":2,"explain":"Mechanical advantage = L/h = 4 m / 2 m = 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On a lever, an effort of 20 N is applied at an effort arm of 3 m to balance a load at a load arm of 1 m. Using effort × effort arm = load × load arm, what is the load in newtons?","answer":60,"explain":"20 N × 3 m = load × 1 m, so load = 60 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A seesaw balances with a 15 kg child sitting 2 m from the fulcrum. Using n1 × L1 = n2 × L2, at what distance from the fulcrum must a 30 kg child sit on the other side to balance it?","options":["1 m","2 m","3 m","0.5 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"15 kg × 2 m = 30 kg × L, so L = 1 m."}]},{"id":"we-boss","title":"The Perpetual Motion Golem","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Foundry looms the Perpetual Motion Golem, built by an inventor who swore it would run forever without fuel. It hums with borrowed energy, daring you to prove you understand where that energy truly comes from — and where it must eventually go.","boss":{"name":"The Perpetual Motion Golem","taunt":"I need no fuel and I never tire — prove your physics before my gears wind down on their own!"},"teach":[{"title":"Chapter Recap","body":"Work done = force × displacement in the direction of force (W = F×s). The work-energy theorem: work done on an object equals the change in its energy. Kinetic energy K = ½mv², potential energy U = mgh, and mechanical energy (KE + PE) is conserved when only gravity acts. Power is the rate of doing work, P = W/t. Simple machines like pulleys, inclined planes, and levers change the effort needed but never reduce the total work done — no machine, however clever, can run forever without an energy source.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly expresses the work-energy theorem?","options":["Work done on an object equals the change in its energy","Work done equals mass times acceleration","Energy equals force times time","Power equals work times time"],"answer":0,"explain":"The work-energy theorem states: work done on an object = change in its energy."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A force of 50 N moves a box 4 m in the direction of the force. Calculate the work done in joules.","answer":200,"explain":"W = F × s = 50 N × 4 m = 200 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 4 kg object moves at 5 m/s. Using K = ½mv², calculate its kinetic energy in joules.","answer":50,"explain":"K = ½ × 4 kg × (5 m/s)² = ½ × 4 × 25 = 50 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object of mass 3 kg is at a height of 4 m above the ground (g = 10 m/s²). Using U = mgh, calculate its potential energy in joules.","answer":120,"explain":"U = mgh = 3 kg × 10 m/s² × 4 m = 120 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As a ball falls freely under gravity, ignoring air resistance, what happens to its total mechanical energy?","options":["It remains constant, as potential energy converts to kinetic energy","It keeps increasing","It keeps decreasing","It becomes zero the instant it starts falling"],"answer":0,"explain":"With gravity as the only force acting, mechanical energy (KE + PE) is conserved during free fall."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A crane does 6000 J of work in 3 seconds. Using P = W/t, what is its power in watts?","answer":2000,"explain":"P = W/t = 6000 J / 3 s = 2000 W."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is true of all simple machines, such as pulleys, inclined planes, and levers?","options":["They change the magnitude or direction of the effort needed, but never reduce the total work done","They reduce the total work required for a task","They create extra energy to make tasks easier","Their mechanical advantage is always less than 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Simple machines make tasks feel easier by changing effort, but the work done (force × distance) stays the same; machines do not create energy."}]}]},{"id":"atom9","index":7,"name":"Journey Inside the Atom","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 8","emoji":"⚛️","palette":{"from":"#818cf8","to":"#312e81"},"lessons":[{"id":"at1","title":"Whispers of the Ancients","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"Your expedition drills through two thousand years of dust — from Acharya Kanada's ancient Indian sutras to Democritus's Greek philosophy — searching for the first thinker bold enough to say matter has a smallest piece. But philosophy alone won't cut it; you need proof, and in 1808 a chemist named John Dalton is about to deliver it.","teach":[{"title":"Kanada's Parmanus","body":"More than 2,000 years ago, Acharya Kanada in India proposed that if matter (dravya) is divided again and again, you eventually reach the smallest particles that cannot be divided further, which he called parmanus. His ideas are recorded in the Sanskrit text Vaisesika Sutras, and he described how parmanus combine into dyads and triads to build the material world.","visual":"📿"},{"title":"Greek Atomos","body":"Around the same era, the Greek philosophers Leucippus and Democritus proposed a similar idea, calling these indivisible particles atomos, meaning 'indivisible' in Greek. Importantly, both the Indian and Greek ideas of the atom were imaginative philosophical ideas, not conclusions from experiments.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Dalton's Atomic Theory (1808)","body":"In 1808, John Dalton proposed the first scientific atomic theory, based on real experiments of his time. He suggested that all matter is made of indivisible atoms, the fundamental building blocks of matter that cannot be broken into smaller parts. This became the true starting point of modern atomic science.","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who proposed the idea of 'parmanus', the smallest indivisible particles of matter, in ancient India?","options":["Acharya Kanada","Democritus","John Dalton","J.J. Thomson"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acharya Kanada described parmanus in the Sanskrit text Vaisesika Sutras."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Greek word 'atomos' mean?","options":["Indivisible","Invisible","Tiny sphere","Empty space"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leucippus and Democritus used 'atomos' to mean a particle that cannot be cut or divided further."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did John Dalton propose his atomic theory?","answer":1808,"explain":"Dalton's atomic theory, based on scientific experiments, was proposed in 1808."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What made Dalton's atomic theory different from the earlier Indian and Greek ideas of the atom?","options":["It was based on scientific experiments rather than philosophy alone","It said atoms could be divided into smaller parts","It was written in Sanskrit","It came before the Greek ideas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dalton's theory was the first atomic idea grounded in actual scientific experimentation."}]},{"id":"at2","title":"Thomson's Discovery","icon":"🍉","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep inside a glowing vacuum tube, mysterious rays race from a dark cathode toward a glowing anode. J.J. Thomson is watching closely in 1897 — and what he finds inside that tube will crack open the 'indivisible' atom for the very first time.","teach":[{"title":"The Cathode Ray Tube","body":"In 1897, J.J. Thomson studied how electric current passed through gases at very low pressure inside a glass tube fitted with two electrodes. He observed rays travelling from the cathode (negative electrode) to the anode (positive electrode), called cathode rays, and found by studying their behaviour in electric and magnetic fields that they were streams of negatively charged particles with much less mass than atoms.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Electrons: A Universal Ingredient","body":"Thomson found that the nature of cathode rays did not depend on the cathode material or the gas used in the tube. This showed that these particles, later named electrons, are a fundamental component present in every atom of every element.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"The Plum Pudding Model","body":"Since atoms are electrically neutral, Thomson faced a puzzle: if atoms contain negative electrons, where is the balancing positive charge? He proposed that the atom is a sphere of positive charge with electrons distributed throughout it, like plums in a pudding, or seeds spread through the red pulp of a watermelon.","visual":"🍉"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What subatomic particle did J.J. Thomson discover using the cathode ray tube?","options":["Electron","Proton","Neutron","Nucleus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thomson identified electrons as negatively charged particles far lighter than atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Thomson's model of the atom is popularly compared to which two everyday objects?","options":["A plum pudding and a watermelon","A solar system and its orbits","A cricket ground and a pepper grain","A cloud and a raindrop"],"answer":0,"explain":"The positive sphere with embedded electrons resembled plums in a pudding or seeds in a watermelon."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why did Thomson propose that positive charge fills the whole atom?","options":["Because atoms are electrically neutral overall, so negative electrons had to be balanced by positive charge somewhere in the atom","Because he could directly see the nucleus","Because alpha particles bounced back off gold foil","Because protons repel each other"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since atoms carry no net charge, Thomson reasoned the negative electrons must sit inside a matching positive charge."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did Thomson conduct his cathode ray experiments that led to the discovery of the electron?","answer":1897,"explain":"Thomson's cathode ray experiments were carried out in 1897."}]},{"id":"at3","title":"The Gold Foil Shock","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"A beam of alpha particles is about to slam into a sheet of gold foil thinner than a hair. Under Rutherford's direction, Geiger and Marsden expect the particles to sail straight through — but when a few come bouncing straight back in 1911, the plum pudding model shatters in an instant.","teach":[{"title":"The Gold Foil Experiment","body":"In 1911, Geiger and Marsden, working under Ernest Rutherford, aimed a narrow beam of alpha particles — tiny, positively charged particles emitted by radioactive elements — at an extremely thin sheet of gold foil. According to Thomson's model, the spread-out positive charge should have let the alpha particles pass through with little or no deflection.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"A Surprising Scattering","body":"Most alpha particles did pass straight through, but to everyone's surprise, some were deflected through large angles, and a few even bounced straight back. This deflection is called scattering, which is why the gold foil experiment is also known as the alpha-ray scattering experiment — and it could not be explained by Thomson's model.","visual":"💥"},{"title":"The Nuclear (Planetary) Model","body":"Rutherford concluded that an atom's positive charge and most of its mass are packed into an extremely small, dense region called the nucleus, while most of the atom is empty space. He proposed that electrons revolve around this nucleus somewhat like planets orbiting the Sun, so this is called the planetary model of the atom.","visual":"🪐"},{"title":"The Proton, and a Problem","body":"Rutherford also showed that the nucleus's positive charge comes from particles he discovered and named protons, much heavier than electrons and carrying an equal but opposite charge. However, his model could not explain atomic stability: a constantly accelerating orbiting electron should lose energy and spiral into the nucleus, which does not actually happen.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who conducted the gold foil experiment in 1911, working under Ernest Rutherford?","options":["Geiger and Marsden","Chadwick and Bohr","Dalton and Berzelius","Thomson and Barkla"],"answer":0,"explain":"Geiger and Marsden performed the alpha-particle scattering experiment under Rutherford's supervision."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What surprising observation from the gold foil experiment disproved Thomson's model?","options":["A few alpha particles bounced straight back","All alpha particles passed straight through with no deflection","The gold foil melted instantly","Electrons were emitted from the foil"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sharp deflections and particles bouncing back were impossible if positive charge were spread evenly, as Thomson's model claimed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Rutherford's nuclear model, where is most of an atom's mass concentrated?","options":["In a tiny, dense nucleus at the centre","Spread evenly throughout the atom","In the electron shells","Outside the atom entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rutherford concluded the nucleus holds nearly all the mass and all the positive charge of the atom."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What key limitation did Rutherford's model fail to explain?","options":["Why electrons don't lose energy, spiral inward, and collapse into the nucleus","Why atoms are electrically neutral","Why the nucleus is positively charged","Why alpha particles are positively charged"],"answer":0,"explain":"A constantly accelerating orbiting electron should radiate energy and fall in, yet real atoms are stable — Rutherford's model couldn't explain why."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What positively charged subatomic particle, found in the nucleus, did Rutherford discover and name?","options":["Proton","Neutron","Electron","Photon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rutherford identified and named the proton as the source of the nucleus's positive charge."}]},{"id":"at4","title":"Bohr's Energy Shells","icon":"🪐","kind":"lesson","story":"Rutherford's atom should have collapsed the instant it formed — accelerating electrons ought to spiral into the nucleus and vanish. In 1913, Niels Bohr steps into the wreckage with a radical fix: electrons locked onto invisible tracks that simply refuse to lose energy.","teach":[{"title":"Fixed Shells, Not Random Paths","body":"In 1913, Niels Bohr proposed that electrons do not move randomly around the nucleus but follow fixed circular paths called stationary states, orbits, or shells, each holding a definite amount of energy. These shells are labelled K, L, M, N, ... or by numbers n = 1, 2, 3, 4, ...","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Stability Through Stationary States","body":"While an electron stays in one of these allowed shells, it does not lose energy — this idea of a stationary state is exactly what explains why atoms are stable, solving the problem Rutherford's model could not. An electron can only jump shells by absorbing or releasing a fixed amount of energy equal to the difference between the two shells' energies.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Energy Rises With Distance","body":"The K-shell (n = 1), closest to the nucleus, has the least energy, and energy increases as shells get farther from the nucleus — so an electron in the L-shell (n = 2) has more energy than one in the K-shell.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Filling the Shells (Bohr-Bury Rule)","body":"Each shell can hold only a certain maximum number of electrons, given by the formula 2n^2: the K-shell holds up to 2, the L-shell up to 8, and the M-shell up to 18. Electrons fill the shells step by step starting from the one closest to the nucleus, and the outermost shell can never hold more than 8 electrons.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did Niels Bohr propose his atomic model of fixed energy shells?","answer":1913,"explain":"Bohr proposed his model of stationary energy shells in 1913."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Bohr, what happens to an electron's energy while it stays in one fixed shell?","options":["It remains constant — the electron does not lose energy","It steadily decreases until the electron falls into the nucleus","It increases continuously","It becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bohr's stationary states have constant energy, which is why atoms don't collapse."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the formula 2n^2, what is the maximum number of electrons the M-shell (n = 3) can hold?","answer":18,"explain":"2 x 3^2 = 2 x 9 = 18 electrons."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which shell is closest to the nucleus and has the least energy?","options":["K-shell (n = 1)","L-shell (n = 2)","M-shell (n = 3)","N-shell (n = 4)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The K-shell, with n = 1, is nearest the nucleus and has the lowest energy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How can an electron move from one shell to another, according to Bohr's model?","options":["By absorbing or releasing a fixed amount of energy equal to the difference between the two shells' energies","By colliding with a proton in the nucleus","By passing straight through the nucleus","Electrons can never move between shells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jumps between shells happen only in fixed energy amounts matching the gap between the two energy levels."}]},{"id":"at5","title":"Neutrons and Numbers","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Something doesn't add up: a helium atom weighs about four times as much as a hydrogen atom, yet has only twice the protons. In 1932, James Chadwick hunts down the missing mass hiding inside the nucleus — and you're right behind him, ready to count every particle in the atom.","teach":[{"title":"Discovery of the Neutron","body":"In 1932, James Chadwick, a student of Rutherford, discovered a new subatomic particle with a mass nearly equal to that of a proton but carrying no electrical charge, which he named the neutron (symbol n). This explained the extra mass in atoms like helium, since neutrons are found in the nucleus of all atoms except hydrogen.","visual":"⚪"},{"title":"Three Subatomic Particles","body":"Atoms are made of three subatomic particles: the electron (symbol e-, relative charge -1), the proton (symbol p+, relative charge +1), and the neutron (symbol n0, relative charge 0). Protons and neutrons, packed tightly in the nucleus, are together called nucleons.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Atomic Number (Z)","body":"The number of protons in the nucleus of an atom is called its atomic number, denoted Z. Since an atom is electrically neutral, its number of protons always equals its number of electrons, and the atomic number determines the identity and chemical behaviour of an element.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Mass Number (A)","body":"The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom is called its mass number, denoted A, so Mass number = Number of protons + Number of neutrons. Electrons have almost negligible mass and can be ignored in this calculation.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who discovered the neutron, and in what year?","options":["James Chadwick, in 1932","J.J. Thomson, in 1897","Ernest Rutherford, in 1911","Niels Bohr, in 1913"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chadwick, a student of Rutherford, discovered the neutron in 1932."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the relative charge of a neutron?","options":["0","+1","-1","+2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Neutrons are electrically neutral, carrying a relative charge of 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An atom has 20 protons and a mass number of 41. How many neutrons does it have?","answer":21,"explain":"Neutrons = Mass number - protons = 41 - 20 = 21."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What determines the atomic number of an element?","options":["The number of protons in its nucleus","The number of neutrons in its nucleus","The total mass of the atom","The number of electron shells"],"answer":0,"explain":"Atomic number (Z) is defined as the number of protons in the nucleus."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A nitrogen atom has atomic number 7 and mass number 14. How many neutrons does it have?","options":["7","14","21","6"],"answer":0,"explain":"Neutrons = Mass number - protons = 14 - 7 = 7."}]},{"id":"at6","title":"Isotope Twins and Isobars","icon":"♊","kind":"lesson","story":"No two atoms of hydrogen weigh exactly the same, yet each one is still stubbornly hydrogen. Dive into the strange world of isotopic twins and isobar look-alikes, where an atom's true identity depends on its protons, not its mass.","teach":[{"title":"Isotopes: Twin Atoms","body":"Atoms of the same element can have the same number of protons (same atomic number) but different numbers of neutrons, giving them different mass numbers. These 'twin atoms' are called isotopes — for example, hydrogen has three isotopes: protium, deuterium (one neutron), and tritium (two neutrons).","visual":"👯"},{"title":"Same Chemistry, Different Physics","body":"Isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties because they have the same number of electrons and the same electronic configuration, though their physical properties, like boiling and melting points, differ. Useful isotopes include uranium-235 (nuclear reactor fuel), cobalt-60 (cancer radiation treatment), iodine-131 (treating thyroid disease), and carbon-14 (dating ancient fossils).","visual":"☢️"},{"title":"Weighted Average Atomic Mass","body":"Because isotopes don't occur in equal amounts in nature, an element's average atomic mass is a weighted average based on each isotope's abundance. For chlorine, with 75% chlorine-35 and 25% chlorine-37, the weighted average works out to (35 x 75 + 37 x 25) / 100 = 35.5 u.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Isobars","body":"When atoms of different elements have the same mass number but different atomic numbers, they are called isobars. For example, calcium (atomic number 20), potassium (atomic number 19), and argon (atomic number 18) are all different elements, yet each has a mass number of 40.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are isotopes?","options":["Atoms of the same element with the same atomic number but different mass numbers","Atoms of different elements with the same mass number","Atoms with no neutrons at all","Atoms with an equal number of protons and neutrons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isotopes share the same atomic number (protons) but differ in neutron count and mass number."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do isotopes of an element share the same chemical properties?","options":["They have the same number of electrons and the same electronic configuration","They have the same number of neutrons","They have the same mass number","They belong to different elements"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemical behaviour depends mainly on electron arrangement, which is identical across isotopes of an element."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Chlorine has two isotopes: mass 35 u (75% abundance) and mass 37 u (25% abundance). What is the weighted average atomic mass, in u?","answer":35.5,"explain":"(35 x 75 + 37 x 25) / 100 = (2625 + 925) / 100 = 35.5 u."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Calcium (Z=20), potassium (Z=19), and argon (Z=18) are different elements that all share a mass number of 40. What are such atoms called?","options":["Isobars","Isotopes","Isomers","Ions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isobars are atoms of different elements with the same mass number but different atomic numbers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which isotope is commonly used in archaeology to determine the age of ancient fossils?","options":["Carbon-14","Carbon-12","Uranium-235","Iodine-131"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon-14 is used for radiocarbon dating of fossils and artefacts."}]},{"id":"at-boss","title":"The Nucleus Sentinel","icon":"☢️","kind":"boss","story":"You've traced the atom from ancient guesswork to Chadwick's neutron, through Thomson's pudding, Rutherford's nucleus, and Bohr's shells. Now the deepest chamber awaits, guarded by a being who has watched every atomic model rise and fall — and it won't let you pass without proving you've truly mastered the atom.","boss":{"name":"The Nucleus Sentinel","taunt":"Peel back every shell you like, traveller — the heart of matter isn't given, it's earned!"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which atomic model came first in the historical development of atomic theory?","options":["Dalton's indivisible atom","Thomson's plum pudding model","Rutherford's nuclear model","Bohr's energy shell model"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dalton's 1808 theory of indivisible atoms came before Thomson, Rutherford, and Bohr's models."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which model first proposed a small, dense, positively charged nucleus at the centre of the atom?","options":["Rutherford's model","Thomson's model","Dalton's model","Bohr's model"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rutherford proposed the nuclear (planetary) model after the gold foil experiment in 1911."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which model finally explained why electrons don't spiral into the nucleus?","options":["Bohr's model, using fixed energy shells","Rutherford's model, using the nucleus alone","Thomson's model, using spread-out positive charge","Dalton's model, using indivisible atoms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bohr's stationary states, where electrons don't lose energy, explained atomic stability."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An atom has atomic number 17 and mass number 35. How many neutrons does it have?","answer":18,"explain":"Neutrons = Mass number - Atomic number = 35 - 17 = 18."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the name for atoms of the same element that have different numbers of neutrons?","options":["Isotopes","Isobars","Ions","Nucleons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isotopes share the same atomic number but differ in neutron count and mass number."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which subatomic particle was discovered last, in 1932?","options":["Neutron","Electron","Proton","Nucleus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932, after the electron (1897) and the proton (via Rutherford, early 1900s-1911)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 7. What is its valency?","options":["1, because it needs to gain just one electron to complete its octet","7, matching its valence electrons exactly","8, to form a full octet","2, matching its innermost shell"],"answer":0,"explain":"With 7 valence electrons (more than four), the atom gains 1 electron to complete the octet, giving it a valency of 1."}]}]},{"id":"atomicfound9","index":8,"name":"Atomic Foundations of Matter","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 9","emoji":"🧬","palette":{"from":"#f87171","to":"#7f1d1d"},"lessons":[{"id":"af1","title":"Mass Never Lies","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A sealed flask of vinegar and baking soda fizzes wildly in your hands, yet the weighing balance refuses to budge. Something ancient and unbreakable is at work here, and you're about to name it.","teach":[{"title":"Law of Conservation of Mass","body":"In 1789, Antoine Lavoisier showed that matter can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. The total mass of reactants always equals the total mass of products, as long as no gas escapes and nothing is added or lost.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Why the balloon experiment worked","body":"When baking soda was dropped into vinegar in a closed, tied balloon, carbon dioxide gas was trapped instead of escaping, so the final reading matched the initial reading. When the flask was left open, the gas escaped and the mass appeared to drop.","visual":"🎈"},{"title":"Law of Constant Proportions","body":"Joseph Proust showed that a compound always contains the same elements combined in a fixed ratio by mass, no matter its source. Purified water from a river, a borewell, or the ocean always contains hydrogen and oxygen in a mass ratio of 1:8.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Using the fixed ratio","body":"Since sodium chloride (NaCl) always has sodium and chlorine in the mass ratio 23:35.5, you can scale this ratio up or down to find how much of one element is needed to react completely with a known mass of the other.","visual":"🧂"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who proposed the Law of Conservation of Mass, in 1789?","options":["Antoine Lavoisier","John Dalton","Joseph Proust","Ernest Rutherford"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lavoisier, the Father of Modern Chemistry, proposed this law in 1789."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Law of Constant Proportions is also known as:","options":["Proust's Law","Avogadro's Law","Gay-Lussac's Law","Law of Multiple Proportions"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is also called the Law of Definite Proportions or Proust's Law, after Joseph Proust."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"12 g of carbon combines with 32 g of oxygen to form 44 g of carbon dioxide. How many grams of carbon dioxide will 2.4 g of carbon produce on complete reaction?","answer":8.8,"explain":"1 g of carbon gives 44/12 g of CO2, so 2.4 g gives (44/12) x 2.4 = 8.8 g of CO2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Water always contains hydrogen and oxygen in a mass ratio of 1:8. If 9 g of pure water is fully decomposed, how many grams of oxygen are obtained?","answer":8,"explain":"Out of 9 total parts (1+8), 8 parts are oxygen, so 9 g of water gives 8 g of oxygen."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Experimental set-up 1, baking soda was poured directly into an open flask of vinegar and the final weighing balance reading was lower than the initial reading. Why?","options":["Carbon dioxide gas escaped into the air before it could be weighed","Mass was destroyed during the reaction","The Law of Conservation of Mass was violated","Vinegar evaporated completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mass is always conserved; the apparent loss occurred only because the CO2 gas escaped the open system instead of being weighed."}]},{"id":"af2","title":"Dalton's Blueprint","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"Two great laws stand before you like locked gates, and only one explanation unlocks both. An English schoolteacher named John Dalton hands you the key: the atom.","teach":[{"title":"Atoms are the building blocks","body":"John Dalton proposed that all matter is made of very tiny, indivisible particles called atoms, which take part in chemical reactions but are never created or destroyed during them.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Atoms of one element are identical","body":"Dalton postulated that atoms of a given element are identical in mass and chemical properties, while atoms of different elements have different masses and different chemical properties.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Whole-number combinations","body":"Atoms combine in the ratio of simple whole numbers to form compounds, and the relative number and kinds of atoms are constant in a given compound. This is exactly why elements always combine in fixed, predictable ratios.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Explaining the two laws","body":"Because atoms are indivisible and simply rearrange during a reaction rather than being created or destroyed, Dalton's theory logically explains both the Law of Conservation of Mass and the Law of Constant Proportions.","visual":"💡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Dalton's Atomic Theory, atoms of a given element are:","options":["Identical in mass and chemical properties","Always different from one another","Divisible into smaller reactive parts","Created fresh during every reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dalton postulated that all atoms of one element share the same mass and chemical properties."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dalton proposed that atoms combine to form compounds in the ratio of:","options":["Simple whole numbers","Any random fraction","Exactly equal numbers only","Prime numbers only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Atoms combine in simple whole-number ratios, which is why chemical formulae have whole-number subscripts."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement is NOT one of Dalton's postulates?","options":["Atoms can be created or destroyed during a chemical reaction","Atoms are indivisible particles","Atoms of different elements have different masses","All matter is made of tiny particles called atoms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dalton held the opposite view: atoms cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Assertion (A): 2 g of hydrogen combines with 16 g of oxygen to form 18 g of water. Reason (R): According to Dalton's Atomic Theory, atoms combine in a simple whole-number ratio by mass to form compounds. Choose the correct option.","options":["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"answer":0,"explain":"A (2+16=18) reflects the Law of Conservation of Mass, while R describes whole-number combining ratios -- both true, but R does not explain why mass is conserved in A."}]},{"id":"af3","title":"Sharing Is Caring: Covalent Bonds","icon":"🤝","kind":"lesson","story":"Lonely atoms drift in your simulation chamber, each one short of a full outer shell. Watch closely as hydrogen, chlorine, and oxygen atoms learn to share electrons and pair up into stable molecules.","teach":[{"title":"What is a molecule?","body":"A molecule is an electrically neutral entity made of more than one atom that can exist independently and shows all the properties of that substance. Atoms of the same element form molecules of an element (like H2); atoms of different elements form molecules of a compound (like HCl).","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Covalent bond by sharing electrons","body":"A hydrogen atom (atomic number 1) has one electron in its K-shell and needs one more to complete its duplet, so two hydrogen atoms share one electron each to form H2, joined by a single covalent bond (H-H).","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Single and double covalent bonds","body":"A chlorine atom has 7 valence electrons and shares one electron with another chlorine atom to form Cl2 (single bond, Cl-Cl). An oxygen atom has 6 valence electrons and shares two electrons with another oxygen atom to form O2, held together by a double bond (O=O).","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Naming covalent compounds","body":"Covalent compounds are named using prefixes (mono-, di-, tri-, tetra-, penta-, hexa-) to show the number of atoms of each element, with the second element ending in -ide; mono- is dropped for the first element. So CO is carbon monoxide, CO2 is carbon dioxide, and N2O4 is dinitrogen tetroxide.","visual":"🏷️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an oxygen molecule (O2), the two oxygen atoms are held together by:","options":["A double bond, formed by sharing two electrons each","A single bond, formed by sharing one electron each","A triple bond, formed by sharing three electrons each","No bond, oxygen atoms just stay near each other"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each oxygen atom needs two more electrons to complete its octet, so two pairs of electrons are shared, forming a double bond (O=O)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A hydrogen atom has one electron in its K-shell. How many more electrons does it need to become stable?","options":["1","2","7","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"The K-shell holds a maximum of 2 electrons, so a hydrogen atom with 1 electron needs just 1 more to complete its duplet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"N2O4 is correctly named as:","options":["Dinitrogen tetroxide","Nitrogen tetroxide","Dinitrogen tetraoxide","Tetranitrogen dioxide"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prefixes di- and tetra- show two nitrogen and four oxygen atoms; the final 'a' of tetra- is dropped before 'oxide', giving tetroxide."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is H2S named 'hydrogen sulfide' and not 'dihydrogen sulfide'?","options":["No prefix is used before hydrogen, regardless of how many atoms are present","Sulfur always takes priority over hydrogen in naming","H2S actually contains only one hydrogen atom","Prefixes are never used for compounds containing hydrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"By convention, when hydrogen is the first element in the formula, no prefix is placed before it."}]},{"id":"af4","title":"Give and Take: Ionic Bonds","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"A sodium atom stands nervously clutching a single spare electron, while a chlorine atom eyes it hungrily, needing just one more to complete its octet. This is a trade that will forge an entire crystal empire: table salt.","teach":[{"title":"Forming a cation","body":"Sodium (atomic number 11) has one electron in its valence shell. When it loses this electron to become stable, it is left with 11 protons but only 10 electrons, giving it a single positive charge: the sodium cation, Na+.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Forming an anion","body":"Chlorine (atomic number 17) has seven valence electrons and gains one electron from another atom to complete its octet, becoming the negatively charged chloride anion, Cl-. A sulfur atom, with six valence electrons, gains two electrons to become S2-.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"The ionic bond","body":"Cations and anions are together called ions. The electrostatic force of attraction between oppositely charged ions, such as Na+ and Cl-, that holds them together is called an ionic bond.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Ionic crystals","body":"Ionic compounds do not exist as single molecules; instead they form 3-D crystal lattices of repeating ions. In sodium chloride, each Na+ ion is surrounded by six Cl- ions, and each Cl- ion is surrounded by six Na+ ions.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sodium atom (atomic number 11) becomes a Na+ cation by:","options":["Losing one electron","Gaining one electron","Losing one proton","Gaining one proton"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sodium has one valence electron; losing it leaves 11 protons and 10 electrons, giving a +1 charge."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the valency (charge number) of the ferric ion, Fe3+?","answer":3,"explain":"The ferric ion carries a charge of 3+, so its valency is 3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What holds the Na+ and Cl- ions together in sodium chloride?","options":["Electrostatic force of attraction (an ionic bond)","A shared pair of electrons (a covalent bond)","Gravitational attraction","Nuclear force"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oppositely charged ions attract each other electrostatically; this attraction is called an ionic bond."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sulfur atom has six electrons in its outer shell. After gaining two electrons to complete its octet, it is represented as:","options":["S2-","S2+","S-","S+"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gaining two electrons gives the sulfur atom two extra negative charges, so it becomes the sulfide ion, S2-."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the sodium chloride crystal lattice, how many chloride ions surround each sodium ion?","answer":6,"explain":"Each Na+ ion is surrounded by six Cl- ions, and each Cl- ion is surrounded by six Na+ ions, in a repeating 3-D pattern."}]},{"id":"af5","title":"Formula Forge","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The Formula Forge only accepts one input: valencies. Criss-cross them correctly and it stamps out a perfect chemical formula. Get it wrong, and the forge spits your compound right back out.","teach":[{"title":"Criss-cross for covalent compounds","body":"To write a covalent formula, write the symbols and valencies of the elements, then criss-cross the valencies as subscripts. For carbon tetrachloride: C has valency 4, Cl has valency 1, so criss-crossing gives CCl4. A valency of 1 is never written.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"Criss-cross for ionic compounds","body":"For ionic compounds, write the cation symbol first, then the anion, with their charges underneath. Criss-cross the charge numbers as subscripts, then simplify by dividing by any common factor. For magnesium oxide, Mg2+ and O2- criss-cross to Mg2O2, which simplifies to MgO.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Brackets for polyatomic ions","body":"When two or more of the same polyatomic ion appear in a formula, brackets are used, as in Al(OH)3 (three hydroxide ions) and Al2(SO4)3 (three sulfate ions for every two aluminium ions). Brackets are skipped when only one polyatomic ion is present, as in CaCO3.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Charges are not shown in the final formula","body":"Although ionic formulae are built from charged ions, the final compound is always electrically neutral overall, so the individual ion charges are never written in the finished chemical formula.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Al3+ and O2-, the correct formula for aluminium oxide is:","options":["Al2O3","AlO3","Al3O2","Al2O2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Criss-crossing the charges 3 and 2 gives subscripts 2 and 3: Al2O3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Ca2+ and Cl-, the correct formula for calcium chloride is:","options":["CaCl2","Ca2Cl","CaCl","Ca2Cl2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Criss-crossing charges 2 and 1 gives CaCl2, since a subscript of 1 is not written."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Mg2+ and OH-, the correct formula for magnesium hydroxide is:","options":["Mg(OH)2","MgOH2","Mg2OH","MgOH"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two hydroxide ions are needed per magnesium ion, and brackets are used around the polyatomic OH- ion: Mg(OH)2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Al3+ and SO4^2-, the correct formula for aluminium sulfate is:","options":["Al2(SO4)3","AlSO4","Al3(SO4)2","Al2SO4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Criss-crossing charges 3 and 2 gives Al2(SO4)3, with brackets since there is more than one sulfate ion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using H (valency 1) and S (valency 2), the criss-cross method gives the formula of hydrogen sulfide as:","options":["H2S","HS2","H2S2","HS"],"answer":0,"explain":"Criss-crossing valencies 1 and 2 gives H2S, matching the formula of hydrogen sulfide."}]},{"id":"af6","title":"Weigh the Molecule","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two mystery powders sit on your lab bench, one dissolving instantly in water and lighting a bulb, the other refusing to dissolve at all. Identify them, then weigh their molecules atom by atom.","teach":[{"title":"Solubility and conductivity","body":"Ionic compounds like sodium chloride and copper sulfate are generally soluble in water but insoluble in kerosene and petrol. Most covalent compounds, like camphor and naphthalene, show the opposite: insoluble in water but soluble in kerosene and petrol.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Why ionic compounds conduct electricity","body":"Ionic compounds do not conduct electricity in the solid state because their ions are locked in fixed positions, but they do conduct when dissolved in water or melted, since the ions become free to move. Covalent compounds like sugar dissolve in water but do not form ions, so they do not conduct electricity.","visual":"💡"},{"title":"Melting and boiling points","body":"Ionic compounds generally have high melting and boiling points because of strong attractions between ions, whereas covalent compounds usually have comparatively low melting and boiling points.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Molecular mass","body":"The molecular mass of a covalent compound is found by adding the atomic masses of all atoms in its molecule. For water (H2O), with H = 1 u and O = 16 u: molecular mass = (1x2) + (16x1) = 18 u. For carbon dioxide (CO2), with C = 12 u: molecular mass = (12x1) + (16x2) = 44 u.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Formula unit mass","body":"Ionic compounds form crystal lattices, not molecules, so their mass is called formula unit mass, calculated the same way. For sodium oxide (Na2O), with Na = 23 u, O = 16 u: formula unit mass = (23x2) + (16x1) = 62 u.","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compared to covalent compounds, ionic compounds generally have:","options":["High melting and boiling points","Low melting and boiling points","No definite melting point","Exactly the same melting point as covalent compounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strong electrostatic attractions between ions in the crystal lattice give ionic compounds high melting and boiling points."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass of carbon dioxide, CO2? (C = 12 u, O = 16 u)","answer":44,"explain":"(12 x 1) + (16 x 2) = 12 + 32 = 44 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass of methane, CH4? (C = 12 u, H = 1 u)","answer":16,"explain":"(12 x 1) + (1 x 4) = 12 + 4 = 16 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the formula unit mass of sodium oxide, Na2O? (Na = 23 u, O = 16 u)","answer":62,"explain":"(23 x 2) + (16 x 1) = 46 + 16 = 62 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the formula unit mass of potassium chloride, KCl? (K = 39 u, Cl = 35.5 u)","answer":74.5,"explain":"39 + 35.5 = 74.5 u."}]},{"id":"af-boss","title":"The Valency Vault","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"Every law, bond, and formula you've mastered now guards the final vault. Only a chemist who has truly understood atomic foundations can crack it open.","boss":{"name":"The Valency Vault","taunt":"Mass, charge, and formula -- get even one wrong and I stay locked forever!"},"teach":[{"title":"Chapter recap","body":"Mass is conserved in chemical reactions (Lavoisier), compounds always contain elements in a fixed mass ratio (Proust), and Dalton's atomic theory explains both. Atoms combine by sharing electrons (covalent bonds) or transferring electrons (ionic bonds) to become stable, and their formulae can be found by criss-crossing valencies or charges.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass of nitric acid, HNO3? (H = 1 u, N = 14 u, O = 16 u)","answer":63,"explain":"1 + 14 + (16 x 3) = 1 + 14 + 48 = 63 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the formula unit mass of magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)2? (Mg = 24 u, O = 16 u, H = 1 u)","answer":58,"explain":"24 + 2 x (16 + 1) = 24 + 34 = 58 u."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Law of Conservation of Mass states that during a chemical reaction, matter:","options":["Can neither be created nor destroyed","Is always created in small amounts","Is always partly destroyed as heat","Doubles in total mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lavoisier's law: the total mass of reactants always equals the total mass of products."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Ca2+ and CO3^2-, the correct formula for calcium carbonate is:","options":["CaCO3","Ca2CO3","CaCO6","Ca2(CO3)2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Criss-crossing charges 2 and 2 gives Ca2(CO3)2, which simplifies by the common factor 2 to CaCO3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An ionic bond typically forms between:","options":["A metal and a non-metal, by transfer of electrons","Two non-metals, by sharing electrons","Two metals, by transfer of protons","Any two atoms of the same element"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metals lose electrons to form cations, and non-metals gain those electrons to form anions, creating an ionic bond."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Sodium chloride contains sodium and chlorine in the mass ratio 23:35.5. If 46 g of sodium reacts completely, how many grams of chlorine are needed to form NaCl?","answer":71,"explain":"Mass of chlorine = (35.5 / 23) x 46 = 71 g."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A solid compound is insoluble in water, dissolves in kerosene, and does not conduct electricity in any state. This compound is most likely:","options":["Covalent","Ionic","Metallic","A mixture of ions only"],"answer":0,"explain":"This solubility and conductivity pattern (insoluble in water, soluble in kerosene, non-conducting) is typical of covalent compounds like camphor or naphthalene."}]}]},{"id":"sound9","index":9,"name":"Sound Waves","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 10","emoji":"🔊","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"so1","title":"The Vibrating Source","icon":"🪘","kind":"lesson","story":"A festival drum falls silent the moment your hand presses its skin flat, then roars back to life the instant you let go. Something invisible is happening inside every sound in the world -- and you are about to catch it in the act.","teach":[{"title":"Sound comes from vibration","body":"Sound is produced by vibrating objects. Vibration means the periodic to-and-fro motion (oscillation) of an object. Pluck a stretched rubber band and it vibrates and produces sound; once the vibration stops, the sound stops too.","visual":"🫀"},{"title":"The source of sound","body":"The object that produces sound is called the source. Sound can be produced by vibrating strings, membranes, air columns, and many other vibrating objects -- for example, blowing across a bansuri vibrates the air column inside it.","visual":"🎵"},{"title":"How humans make sound","body":"In humans and some other animals, sound is produced by the vibration of vocal cords -- tightly stretched muscular flaps located inside the voice box, or larynx, in the throat. The tongue, lips, mouth and nasal cavity then shape this into speech or music.","visual":"🗣"},{"title":"The tuning fork","body":"A tuning fork is a U-shaped metal bar with a stem, usually made of steel or aluminium. Its two arms are called prongs or tines. Striking a prong on a rubber pad makes it vibrate; touching the vibrating prong to a water surface creates ripples, proving it is truly vibrating.","visual":"🎶"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the ultimate cause of every sound?","options":["Vibration of an object","Light striking an object","Heat inside an object","Colour of an object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound is produced whenever an object vibrates; once the vibration stops, the sound stops."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In humans, sound is produced mainly by the vibration of the","options":["vocal cords in the larynx","eardrum","lungs","cochlea"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vocal cords are tightly stretched muscular flaps inside the larynx (voice box) that vibrate to produce sound."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A tuning fork is best described as","options":["a U-shaped metal bar with a stem, whose prongs vibrate when struck","a hollow tube that only makes sound when blown","a flat metal disc with no moving parts","a liquid-filled container that hums on its own"],"answer":0,"explain":"A tuning fork is a U-shaped metal bar (steel or aluminium) with a stem; its prongs vibrate when struck on a rubber pad."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Grasshoppers and crickets produce their characteristic sound by","options":["rubbing their wings or legs together","vibrating vocal cords like humans","blowing air through a hollow shell","striking their antennae on leaves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Some animals produce sound by striking or rubbing certain body parts; grasshoppers and crickets rub their wings or legs."}]},{"id":"so2","title":"Riding the Slinky Wave","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"You seal an electric bell inside a glass jar and start sucking the air out. The bell still rattles behind the glass -- but the sound grows fainter and fainter, as if the world itself were swallowing it. What is sound actually travelling through?","teach":[{"title":"Sound needs a medium","body":"Sound can travel through solids, liquids and gases -- the material through which it propagates is called a medium. In the vacuum bell jar experiment, as air is pumped out of a jar containing a ringing electric bell, the sound grows fainter until almost nothing is heard, even though the bell can still be seen ringing. Sound cannot propagate in a vacuum (a space with no matter).","visual":"🛎"},{"title":"Compressions and rarefactions","body":"As a vibrating source pushes into the medium, it creates a compression -- a region of higher-than-average density. As it pulls back, it creates a rarefaction -- a region of lower-than-average density. A sound wave is this disturbance of alternating compressions and rarefactions travelling through a medium, without the particles of the medium actually flowing along with it.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Longitudinal waves","body":"In a sound wave, particles of the medium vibrate back and forth parallel to the direction the wave travels. Waves of this kind are called longitudinal waves. In a transverse wave, by contrast, particles vibrate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Sound is a mechanical wave","body":"Waves that require a material medium to propagate are called mechanical waves, and sound is one such wave. This is why astronauts on a spacewalk, surrounded by the near-vacuum of space, cannot directly hear each other speak or hear metal clanking -- they must use radio devices instead. Light, by contrast, is a transverse wave that can travel through vacuum.","visual":"🚀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound cannot travel through","options":["a vacuum","a solid","a liquid","a gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vacuum bell jar experiment shows that sound fades to nothing as air is removed -- sound needs a medium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a sound wave, particles of the medium vibrate","options":["parallel to the direction of wave propagation","perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation","in random, uncoordinated directions","not at all -- only energy moves"],"answer":0,"explain":"This parallel vibration is exactly what makes sound a longitudinal wave."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As a sound wave passes through a medium, what actually travels from the source to the listener?","options":["Energy, carried by the disturbance of compressions and rarefactions","The individual particles of the medium themselves","A steady stream of compressed air","Nothing physical -- only vibration of the ear"],"answer":0,"explain":"The particles of the medium only oscillate about their mean positions; it is energy that is transferred through the wave."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Waves that require a material medium to propagate are called","options":["mechanical waves","electromagnetic waves","standing waves","transverse waves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound is a mechanical, longitudinal wave -- it cannot exist without a medium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A region of a sound wave where the density of the medium is higher than average is called a","options":["compression","rarefaction","crest of light","vacuum pocket"],"answer":0,"explain":"A compression is a region of higher density; a rarefaction is a region of lower density."}]},{"id":"so3","title":"Reading the Wave","icon":"📊","kind":"lesson","story":"A graph of dots and dashes lies before you -- crests, troughs, a wobbling density curve. Your guide insists this squiggle secretly holds the pitch, loudness and identity of every sound ever made. Time to decode it.","teach":[{"title":"Wavelength","body":"The distance between two consecutive crests (or two consecutive troughs) of a wave is called its wavelength, represented by the Greek letter λ (lambda). Its SI unit is the metre (m).","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Frequency and time period","body":"The number of density oscillations at a fixed point per unit time is the frequency of the sound wave, represented by ν (nu); its SI unit is per second, called hertz (Hz). The time period (T) is the time taken for one complete oscillation. Frequency and time period are inversely related: ν = 1/T.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Amplitude","body":"The amplitude of a sound wave is the maximum change in the density of air in a compression (or rarefaction), compared to the average density. A larger change in density means a larger amplitude, and a wave with larger amplitude carries more energy than one with smaller amplitude.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Intensity","body":"The amount of sound energy passing through a unit area perpendicular to the direction of propagation, in a unit time, is called the intensity of sound. As a sound wave spreads out from its source over a larger area, the same energy is spread thinner, so intensity decreases with distance from the source.","visual":"🔋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The distance between two consecutive crests of a sound wave is called its","options":["wavelength","amplitude","frequency","time period"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wavelength (λ) is the distance between two consecutive crests or troughs, measured in metres."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At a fixed point, 10 density oscillations occur in 2 seconds. What is the frequency of the sound wave, in Hz?","answer":5,"explain":"Frequency = number of oscillations / time taken = 10 / 2 s = 5 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sound wave has a frequency of 100 Hz. Using ν = 1/T, what is its time period, in seconds?","answer":0.01,"explain":"T = 1/ν = 1/100 Hz = 0.01 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sound wave with a larger amplitude, compared to one with smaller amplitude, carries","options":["more energy","less energy","exactly the same energy","no energy at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"A larger density change (larger amplitude) corresponds to more energy carried by the wave."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The intensity of sound is defined as","options":["sound energy passing through a unit area, perpendicular to the direction of propagation, per unit time","the total energy of the source at the moment of production","the number of compressions produced per second","the loudness felt by a particular listener's ear"],"answer":0,"explain":"Intensity is a measurable physical quantity, unlike loudness which depends on the listener."}]},{"id":"so4","title":"Racing the Speed of Sound","icon":"🏃","kind":"lesson","story":"Lightning flashes across the sky -- and you count the seconds until the thunder rumbles in. That gap is not silence; it is sound running a race against light, and losing badly. Time to clock its speed.","teach":[{"title":"Speed = wavelength x frequency","body":"The speed of sound (v) is the distance a point on the wave, such as a crest, travels in unit time. Since one wavelength is covered in one time period, v = λ/T, and since ν = 1/T, this gives the key relation: v = λ × ν, or speed = wavelength × frequency.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Speed depends on the medium","body":"The speed of sound depends on the medium it travels through: it is fastest in solids, slower in liquids, and slowest in gases. At 15°C, sound travels at about 5000 m/s in steel, 1500 m/s in water, and 340 m/s in air -- sound is typically 4-5 times faster in water and 15-20 times faster in solids than in air.","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"Temperature and speed in air","body":"The speed of sound in air depends on temperature and humidity, increasing as either increases. The speed of sound in dry air is about 331 m/s at 0°C and nearly 344 m/s at 22°C. In a given medium, speed does not depend on the source or frequency -- only wavelength changes if frequency changes.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Thunder and lightning","body":"Since light travels enormously faster than sound, a lightning flash is seen almost instantly, while its thunder arrives later. The time delay, multiplied by the speed of sound, gives the distance to the strike: distance = speed × time.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The lowest frequency humans can hear is 20 Hz. If the speed of sound in air is 344 m/s, what is the wavelength of this sound, in metres?","answer":17.2,"explain":"λ = v/ν = 344 m/s / 20 Hz = 17.2 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"During a storm, thunder is heard 5 s after the lightning flash is seen. Taking the speed of sound in air as 340 m/s, estimate the distance to the lightning strike, in metres.","answer":1700,"explain":"distance = speed x time = 340 m/s x 5 s = 1700 m (1.7 km)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound generally travels fastest through","options":["solids","liquids","gases","vacuum"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound travels fastest in solids, slower in liquids, and slowest in gases."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sound wave travelling through steel (speed = 5000 m/s) has a wavelength of 50 m. What is its frequency, in Hz?","answer":100,"explain":"ν = v/λ = 5000 m/s / 50 m = 100 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As the temperature of air increases, the speed of sound in it","options":["increases","decreases","stays exactly the same","becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"The speed of sound in dry air rises from about 331 m/s at 0°C to about 344 m/s at 22°C."}]},{"id":"so5","title":"The Limits of Hearing","icon":"👂","kind":"lesson","story":"A dog perks up its ears at a whistle you cannot hear at all, and an elephant seems to sense a storm long before it arrives. Your own ears, it turns out, only open a narrow window onto a much bigger world of sound.","teach":[{"title":"The human hearing range","body":"Humans can only hear a limited range of frequencies, called the audible range or human hearing range: 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (20 kHz). This range varies from person to person and decreases with age.","visual":"👂"},{"title":"Infrasonic and ultrasonic waves","body":"Sound waves with frequency below 20 Hz are called infrasonic waves, while sound waves with frequency above 20 kHz are called ultrasonic waves. Humans cannot hear either, but dogs, cats, bats and dolphins can detect ultrasound, while elephants can detect infrasound.","visual":"🐘"},{"title":"Pitch","body":"How frequency is perceived by humans is called pitch. Shrill sounds like a whistle or siren have high pitch (higher frequency), while deep sounds like thunder or an aircraft rumble have low pitch (lower frequency).","visual":"🎷"},{"title":"Loudness","body":"Humans perceive the amplitude of a sound wave as loudness; larger amplitude sounds louder. Loudness is commonly measured in decibels (dB) -- rustling leaves are a few dB, normal conversation is about 60 dB, and loud sounds like firecrackers can exceed 100 dB. Loudness decreases as the listener moves farther from the source.","visual":"🔊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The human audible (hearing) range of frequencies is approximately","options":["20 Hz to 20,000 Hz","2 Hz to 200 Hz","200 Hz to 2,000,000 Hz","20,000 Hz to 200,000 Hz"],"answer":0,"explain":"Humans can hear sound frequencies roughly from 20 Hz to 20 kHz (20,000 Hz)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound waves with frequency below 20 Hz are called","options":["infrasonic waves","ultrasonic waves","supersonic waves","microwaves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Frequencies below 20 Hz are infrasonic; elephants can detect these."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound waves with frequency above 20 kHz are called","options":["ultrasonic waves","infrasonic waves","radio waves","audible waves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Frequencies above 20 kHz are ultrasonic; dogs, cats, bats and dolphins can detect these."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A shrill, high-pitched sound like a whistle corresponds to","options":["a higher frequency","a lower frequency","a larger amplitude only","a longer wavelength only, with no change in frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pitch is how frequency is perceived -- high pitch sounds have higher frequency."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An octave is the interval between two notes where one has double the frequency of the other. A note has frequency 200 Hz. What is the frequency of the note exactly one octave higher, in Hz?","answer":400,"explain":"An octave means double the frequency, so 200 Hz x 2 = 400 Hz."}]},{"id":"so6","title":"Echoes, Sonar and the Bat's Secret","icon":"🦇","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in a cave, a bat swoops through pitch darkness without ever touching the walls. It is not seeing the cave -- it is listening to its own voice bounce back. You are about to learn the trick that lets sound draw a picture of the unseen.","teach":[{"title":"Echo","body":"Sound waves bounce off hard obstacles just as light reflects, following the same laws of reflection. This reflection of sound is called an echo. If the time gap between the original sound and its reflection is at least 0.1 s, the brain hears them as two separate sounds; if less, it cannot distinguish them. Using speed of sound = 340 m/s, the minimum distance to a reflecting surface for an echo to be heard is (340 m/s x 0.1 s) / 2 = 17 m.","visual":"🏔️"},{"title":"Reverberation","body":"In a large hall, sound can reflect off walls multiple times, making it persist after the source stops -- this is called reverberation, and it occurs when reflections arrive with a time difference of less than 0.05 s. Auditoriums use sound-absorbing panels, upholstered chairs and curtains to control unwanted reverberation.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Echolocation and sonar","body":"Bats emit short bursts of ultrasonic waves and sense the reflected echoes to locate obstacles and prey in the dark -- an ability called echolocation, also used by dolphins, whales and some birds. Humans use the same principle in sonar (sound navigation and ranging): ultrasonic waves are sent into water, and their reflections reveal the distance, direction and speed of underwater objects like submarines or shipwrecks.","visual":"🦭"},{"title":"Applications of ultrasonic and infrasonic waves","body":"Ultrasonic waves are used in ultrasonography (imaging internal organs without surgery), breaking kidney stones, ultrasonic welding and cleaning delicate machine parts, and detecting flaws inside metal blocks. Infrasonic waves, which travel long distances through air and the Earth, are used to detect earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and severe storms.","visual":"🩺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An echo is heard clearly when the reflected sound reaches the ear","options":["at least 0.1 s after the original sound","at least 1 minute after the original sound","before the original sound is even produced","at exactly the same instant as the original sound"],"answer":0,"explain":"A time gap of at least 0.1 s lets the brain distinguish the echo from the original sound."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using a minimum time gap of 0.1 s and speed of sound = 340 m/s, what is the minimum distance (in metres) a reflecting surface must be for an echo to be heard?","answer":17,"explain":"Total distance travelled = 340 m/s x 0.1 s = 34 m (to the surface and back), so the minimum distance to the surface is 34/2 = 17 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Reverberation in a large hall occurs when reflected sound arrives","options":["less than 0.05 s after the original sound","more than 5 seconds after the original sound","only when the hall is completely empty of air","only for infrasonic waves"],"answer":0,"explain":"A time difference of less than 0.05 s between reflections causes sound to persist as reverberation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A naval sonar signal sent into seawater (speed = 1530 m/s) returns after 0.90 s. How far away is the object, in metres?","answer":688.5,"explain":"The signal travels to the object and back in 0.90 s, so time to reach it is 0.45 s; distance = 1530 m/s x 0.45 s = 688.5 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ultrasonography, used to image internal organs without surgery, relies on","options":["ultrasonic waves","infrasonic waves","visible light waves","radio waves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ultrasonic waves (above 20 kHz) are used for ultrasonography, among other medical and industrial applications."}]},{"id":"so-boss","title":"Boss: The Vacuum Wraith","icon":"👻","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Vacuum Wraith","taunt":"In my domain there is no medium and no mercy -- not even a scream survives!"},"story":"The Vacuum Wraith has sealed the final chamber in silence, sucking every trace of air from the room. Only a warrior who truly understands vibration, propagation, waves, speed, hearing and reflection can force sound back into existence and break its hold.","teach":[{"title":"The chapter, in one breath","body":"Sound is produced by vibrating objects and travels as a longitudinal mechanical wave -- alternating compressions and rarefactions -- through solids, liquids or gases, but never through a vacuum. Its speed equals wavelength x frequency (v = λν), it reflects to give echoes and reverberation, and frequencies beyond 20 Hz-20 kHz (infrasonic and ultrasonic) power everything from earthquake detection to ultrasonography and sonar.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which observation best supports the idea that sound is a mechanical wave?","options":["Sound needs a medium to propagate","Sound shows reflection","Sound has a frequency","Sound carries energy"],"answer":0,"explain":"A defining feature of mechanical waves is that they cannot travel without a material medium, unlike electromagnetic waves such as light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound is best classified as a","options":["longitudinal mechanical wave","transverse mechanical wave","longitudinal electromagnetic wave","transverse electromagnetic wave"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound particles vibrate parallel to the direction of wave travel (longitudinal), and it needs a medium (mechanical)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If 20 compressions pass a fixed point in 4 seconds, what is the frequency of the sound wave, in Hz?","answer":5,"explain":"Frequency = number of oscillations / time = 20 / 4 s = 5 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sound wave has wavelength 3.44 m and travels at 344 m/s. What is its time period, in seconds?","answer":0.01,"explain":"T = λ/v = 3.44 m / 344 m/s = 0.01 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sonar signal sent to find the depth of the ocean takes 4 s to return. If the speed of sound in seawater is 1500 m/s, what is the depth of the ocean, in metres?","answer":3000,"explain":"Time to reach the seabed is half the round trip: 4 s / 2 = 2 s. Depth = 1500 m/s x 2 s = 3000 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Loudness is the human perception of a sound wave's","options":["amplitude","frequency","wavelength","time period"],"answer":0,"explain":"Loudness corresponds to amplitude, just as pitch corresponds to frequency."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An oscillating piston produces a sound wave of frequency 20 Hz. How many oscillations does the piston complete in one minute?","answer":1200,"explain":"20 oscillations per second x 60 seconds = 1200 oscillations per minute."}]}]},{"id":"reproduction9","index":10,"name":"Reproduction","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 11","emoji":"🌱","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"re1","title":"Cloning Without a Mate","icon":"🌿","kind":"lesson","story":"A scout mission finds a strange garden where every plant looks exactly like its neighbour. Your job: figure out how a potato eye, a Bryophyllum leaf, and a lonely yeast cell can all grow into whole new organisms without any partner at all.","teach":[{"title":"One Parent, Identical Offspring","body":"Asexual reproduction needs only a single parent and produces genetically identical individuals. It is seen in unicellular organisms like bacteria, amoeba and yeast, in simple multicellular organisms like hydra and sponge, and in many plants.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Vegetative Propagation","body":"Many plants sprout new shoots and roots from their existing vegetative parts. Potato and ginger have fleshy underground stems, money plant and sugarcane grow from stem cuttings, and Bryophyllum leaves sprout tiny plantlets that grow into new plants.","visual":"🥔"},{"title":"Cutting, Grafting, Layering, Tissue Culture","body":"Scientists and horticulturists adapted vegetative propagation into methods such as cutting, grafting (joining a stem piece of one plant onto a rooted plant of another variety), layering (burying part of a twig until it roots), and tissue culture (mass-producing plantlets from a shoot tip). These help farmers cultivate crops on a large scale.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"Budding","body":"In yeast, small round outgrowths called buds form on the parent cell. In hydra, repeated cell division at a specific site produces a bud that enlarges and separates from the parent to live independently. This process is called budding.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Spore Formation and Mitosis","body":"Fungi like Rhizopus and Aspergillus reproduce by releasing huge numbers of lightweight spores that float through air and germinate wherever conditions are moist. Behind all these methods lies mitosis, a cell division producing daughter cells identical to the parent, so the offspring are genetically identical clones.","visual":"🍞"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of vegetative propagation?","options":["A potato sprouting a new plant from its eye","A yeast cell budding once","A frog laying eggs in water","A bee carrying pollen between flowers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Potato tubers are fleshy underground stems that sprout new plants without seeds -- a classic case of vegetative propagation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key genetic outcome of asexual reproduction?","options":["Offspring are genetically identical to the parent","Offspring get half their genes from each parent","Offspring have more chromosomes than the parent","Offspring are always sterile"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because only one parent is involved and mitosis is used, offspring are clones of the parent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In hydra, a small outgrowth that enlarges and separates to live independently is called a...?","options":["bud","spore","zygote","graft"],"answer":0,"explain":"This process, seen in hydra and yeast, is called budding."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Tissue culture in banana farming uses cells from which part of the plant?","options":["The shoot tip (apical meristem)","The ripe fruit pulp","The flower petals","The root hair"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tissue culture uses cells from the shoot tip to mass-produce healthy, virus-free plantlets."}]},{"id":"re2","title":"The Shuffle of Meiosis","icon":"🎲","kind":"lesson","story":"Before two parents can combine their traits, their cells must solve a strange puzzle: halve the chromosome number without losing any genetic information. Step into a gamete factory and watch meiosis shuffle the genetic deck.","teach":[{"title":"Two Parents, Mixed Traits","body":"Sexual reproduction involves two parents contributing genetic material to the offspring. Because offspring inherit a mix of characteristics from both parents, small differences arise between parents and their young ones.","visual":"👨‍👩‍👧"},{"title":"Why Meiosis Is Needed","body":"If every generation received a full set of chromosomes from both parents, the chromosome number would double each generation. Meiosis solves this by producing gametes with half the parent's chromosome number, so fusing two gametes restores the normal number.","visual":"➗"},{"title":"Gametes in Animals and Plants","body":"In animals, male gametes are called sperm and female gametes are called eggs. In plants, pollen grains carry male gametes to the ovule, which holds the female gamete. Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes (46 total); each gamete carries just 23.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Meiosis Creates Variation","body":"During meiosis, chromosomes of each pair separate so each gamete gets a random combination. With just 3 pairs of contrasting characters, 8 combinations are possible; with 23 human chromosome pairs, the possible combinations are enormous, making every child genetically unique.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Why Variation Matters","body":"Variation helps some individuals adapt better to changing environments -- for example, tolerating low oxygen at high altitude or digesting milk as adults. Accumulated over generations, this variation contributes to evolution and helps species survive.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is meiosis necessary in sexual reproduction?","options":["It halves the chromosome number so it doesn't double every generation","It doubles the chromosome number for stronger offspring","It removes the need for a second parent","It converts eggs into sperm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Meiosis produces haploid gametes so that fusion of two gametes restores the normal chromosome number."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If a species' body cells have 46 chromosomes, how many chromosomes does each gamete produced by meiosis contain?","answer":23,"explain":"Meiosis halves the diploid number (46) to the haploid number (23) found in each gamete."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"With 3 pairs of contrasting characters, how many different gamete combinations are possible?","options":["8","3","6","16"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each pair can contribute one of 2 versions, so 2x2x2 = 8 combinations, as shown in the bead activity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main advantage of the variation created by sexual reproduction?","options":["It helps species adapt to changing environments over generations","It guarantees offspring look identical to parents","It prevents any mutations from occurring","It speeds up population growth more than asexual reproduction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Variation lets some individuals cope better with changing conditions, which over time contributes to evolution."}]},{"id":"re3","title":"Flowers: Nature's Matchmakers","icon":"🌸","kind":"lesson","story":"A flower isn't just decoration -- it's a finely tuned reproductive machine. Explore its parts, trace pollen's journey from anther to ovule, and discover why some flowers bribe insects with nectar while others simply ride the wind.","teach":[{"title":"Parts of a Flower","body":"A complete flower has four parts: sepals (the outer green covering that protects the bud), petals (the coloured, often fragrant projections), the stamen (the male part, made of a filament and an anther that produces pollen), and the pistil (the female part).","visual":"🌺"},{"title":"The Pistil","body":"The pistil has three subparts: the stigma at the tip (often flat or sticky), the style (a long tube connecting stigma to ovary), and the ovary, which contains ovules. Each ovule has an egg cell, the female gamete.","visual":"🌷"},{"title":"Pollination: Self and Cross","body":"Pollination is the transfer of pollen from the anther to a stigma. When pollen moves to the stigma of the same flower or another flower on the same plant, it is self-pollination. When pollen moves to a flower on a different plant of the same species, it is cross-pollination.","visual":"🐝"},{"title":"Pollination Strategies","body":"Wind-pollinated plants like wheat, maize and rice produce light, small pollen in huge numbers, caught by long feathery stigmas. Water-pollinated aquatic plants like Vallisneria rely on currents. Insect-pollinated flowers like sunflower and hibiscus are bright, fragrant and nectar-rich with sticky or spiny pollen, while bird-pollinated flowers like the coral tree attract birds such as sunbirds.","visual":"🦋"},{"title":"Fertilisation, Fruit and Seed","body":"After pollination, the pollen grows a tube down through the style to the ovule, where the male gamete fuses with the egg cell -- this fusion is fertilisation, forming a zygote that develops into an embryo. The ovary enlarges into a fruit, while the ovules become seeds inside it.","visual":"🍎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the flower produces pollen grains?","options":["Anther","Stigma","Ovary","Sepal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The anther, part of the stamen, produces pollen grains containing the male gametes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pollen transferred from the anther of one plant to the stigma of a flower on a different plant of the same species is called...?","options":["Cross-pollination","Self-pollination","Fertilisation","Germination"],"answer":0,"explain":"Transfer between flowers of different plants of the same species is cross-pollination."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pollination strategy typically involves light, small pollen produced in very large numbers and a long feathery stigma?","options":["Wind pollination","Insect pollination","Water pollination","Bird pollination"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wind-pollinated plants like wheat and maize rely on producing huge quantities of light pollen since wind transfer is inefficient."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"After fertilisation in a flowering plant, what does the ovary develop into?","options":["A fruit","A seed","A petal","A new stigma"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ovary enlarges into a fruit while the ovules inside it become seeds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the pea plant experiment, fruit failed to form only when stamens were removed from...?","options":["a flower bud before it could pollinate","an already-blossomed flower","the plant's leaves","the ovary directly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing stamens from the unopened bud prevented any pollen transfer, so no fertilisation or fruit formation occurred."}]},{"id":"re4","title":"Survival Strategies in the Animal Kingdom","icon":"🐸","kind":"lesson","story":"From a fish releasing thousands of eggs into open water to a bird carefully guarding just a few, animals have evolved wildly different bets for passing on their genes. Compare the strategies and see which pays off in survival.","teach":[{"title":"External vs Internal Fertilisation","body":"In many aquatic animals like frogs and most fish, fertilisation happens outside the body -- the female releases eggs into water and the male releases sperm over them. In reptiles, birds and mammals, fertilisation happens inside the female's body, where the embryo is better protected and generally has a higher chance of survival.","visual":"🐟"},{"title":"Eggs, Numbers and Survival","body":"Fish and frogs release hundreds to tens of thousands of eggs at a time, but survival of the young is low. Lizards and birds produce far fewer eggs with internal fertilisation, and survival is moderate to high. Producing huge numbers of eggs compensates for the low chance each one survives.","visual":"🥚"},{"title":"The Larva Stage","body":"Fish, amphibians and insects lay eggs with just enough yolk to produce a larva, an intermediate feeding stage that hatches early and gets nutrition by eating on its own. Once enough nutrition is gathered, the larva transforms into the adult body, as seen in butterflies and frogs.","visual":"🦋"},{"title":"Eggs and Live Birth","body":"Reptiles and birds lay eggs with enough yolk to nourish the embryo fully until it hatches, so there is no larval stage. In mammals, the zygote grows and develops directly inside the female's body, and after birth the young feed on the mother's milk.","visual":"🐦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of fertilisation generally gives embryos a higher chance of survival?","options":["Internal fertilisation","External fertilisation","Both are equally protective","Neither offers protection"],"answer":0,"explain":"Internally fertilised embryos are protected inside the female's body, giving them better survival chances."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do fish and frogs produce far more eggs than birds or lizards?","options":["Their eggs and young are more exposed to loss, so many eggs compensate for low individual survival","Because external fertilisation always damages most eggs before they develop","Because they lack ovaries","Because external fertilisation doubles the chromosome number"],"answer":0,"explain":"External fertilisation leaves eggs vulnerable to currents and predators, so species produce huge numbers to ensure some survive."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a larva?","options":["An early, independently-feeding stage that hatches from an egg with limited yolk and later transforms into the adult","A fertilised egg still inside the mother's body","An unfertilised egg cell","A type of pollen grain"],"answer":0,"explain":"Butterflies and frogs are examples where a larva hatches early and feeds itself before transforming into an adult."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which animal group lays internally fertilised eggs, produces the fewest eggs at a time, and shows moderate to high survival of young?","options":["Bird","Fish","Frog","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Birds lay only 1-15 eggs at a time with internal fertilisation, and survival of young is moderate to high."}]},{"id":"re5","title":"Building the Human Reproductive System","icon":"🧑‍⚕️","kind":"lesson","story":"Every human being starts as a single fertilised cell. Before that can happen, two very different reproductive systems must each do their job. Tour the organs that make sperm and eggs, and see why the two gametes look nothing alike.","teach":[{"title":"The Male Reproductive System","body":"Sperm are produced in two testes housed in the scrotum, which keeps them slightly cooler than body temperature -- necessary for sperm formation. Testes also produce a hormone that controls sperm production and causes puberty changes. Sperm travel through the vas deferens into the urethra, helped along by fluids from the seminal vesicles and prostate gland.","visual":"👨"},{"title":"The Female Reproductive System","body":"The female system has a pair of ovaries (which produce eggs and hormones), oviducts (fallopian tubes) connecting each ovary to the uterus, the uterus itself (a bag-like structure where a foetus develops), and the vagina, reached through a narrow passage called the cervix.","visual":"👩"},{"title":"Gametogenesis","body":"The formation of gametes is called gametogenesis, and it happens by meiosis in the testes and ovaries. Human body cells have 46 chromosomes, but sperm and eggs have only 23 each, so that a zygote formed by their fusion has the normal 46.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Sperm vs Egg","body":"Male gametogenesis produces millions of tiny, actively motile sperm with no stored nutrients. Female gametogenesis produces just a few large, non-motile eggs that do carry stored nutrients. This size and number asymmetry between male and female gametes is seen across most animals.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the scrotum keep the testes slightly cooler than the rest of the body?","options":["Because this lower temperature is necessary for sperm formation","Because it protects the testes from injury","Because it helps produce more urine","Because it speeds up puberty"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sperm formation requires a temperature slightly cooler than normal body temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which structure in the female reproductive system is the site where a foetus develops?","options":["Uterus","Ovary","Oviduct","Cervix"],"answer":0,"explain":"The uterus is the bag-like structure where a foetus develops."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chromosomes does a human egg or sperm cell contain after meiosis?","answer":23,"explain":"Gametogenesis by meiosis halves the normal 46 chromosomes to 23 in each gamete."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares sperm and eggs?","options":["Sperm are small, numerous, and motile; eggs are large, few, and non-motile","Sperm are large and few; eggs are small and numerous","Both sperm and eggs are equally motile and equal in size","Eggs are motile while sperm are not"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 11.5 shows sperm are very small, produced in millions, and motile, while eggs are large, few, and non-motile."}]},{"id":"re6","title":"From Cycle to Childbirth","icon":"🤰","kind":"lesson","story":"Follow one month in the human reproductive cycle -- from an egg's release to either a new pregnancy or the start of menstruation -- then fast-forward nine months to see how a single zygote grows into a newborn.","teach":[{"title":"Ovulation and the Menstrual Cycle","body":"From puberty onward, usually one mature egg is released from an ovary every month -- ovulation, typically around day 14 of a cycle. Before ovulation, the uterine lining thickens to prepare for a possible pregnancy. The full cycle repeats roughly every 21-35 days (often about 28), starting at puberty (ages 10-14) and continuing until menopause around age 50.","visual":"🩸"},{"title":"Fertilisation and Menstruation","body":"During intercourse, millions of sperm may reach the egg in the oviduct; if one fuses with it, a zygote forms and implants in the thickened uterine lining, beginning pregnancy. If the egg is not fertilised, it degenerates within about a day, the thickened lining is no longer needed, and it sheds through the vagina along with some blood -- menstruation, usually lasting 3 to 7 days.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Pregnancy and Birth","body":"Human pregnancy lasts about nine months, divided into three trimesters. In the first trimester the embryo's major organs form, and from about the ninth week it is called a foetus. It grows through the second and third trimesters until birth, when uterine contractions push it out through the birth canal.","visual":"👶"},{"title":"What Determines Biological Sex","body":"Every person has two sex chromosomes: females have XX and males have XY. The mother always contributes an X chromosome; the father contributes either an X (making a girl) or a Y (making a boy) -- so the father's chromosome determines the baby's biological sex.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Sexual Maturity and Responsible Choices","body":"Sexual maturity (like sperm production in boys and menstrual cycles in girls) develops gradually during adolescence, but emotional maturity takes longer. Responsible choices help prevent unplanned pregnancies and Sexually Transmitted Infections such as gonorrhoea, herpes, syphilis, genital warts and HIV. Barrier methods like condoms, hormonal pills, and IUDs like copper-T can prevent unwanted pregnancy, and condoms also help prevent STI transmission.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Around which day of a typical 28-day menstrual cycle does ovulation usually occur?","options":["Day 14","Day 1","Day 28","Day 21"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ovulation typically occurs around day 14 of a 28-day cycle, as shown in Fig. 11.21."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What triggers menstruation?","options":["The unfertilised egg degenerates, so the thickened uterine lining is no longer needed and sheds","The fertilised egg implants in the uterus","The ovaries stop producing eggs permanently","The testes stop producing sperm"],"answer":0,"explain":"When fertilisation does not occur, the egg degenerates and the prepared uterine lining sheds as menstruation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which parent's chromosome contribution determines a baby's biological sex?","options":["The father's, since he contributes either an X or a Y chromosome","The mother's, since she contributes either an X or a Y chromosome","Both parents equally determine it every time","Neither parent's chromosomes are involved"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mother always contributes an X; the father contributes an X (girl, XX) or a Y (boy, XY), so his contribution decides the outcome."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is described as a barrier method of contraception that also helps prevent STIs?","options":["Condom","Oral contraceptive pill","IUD (copper-T)","Menstrual cup"],"answer":0,"explain":"Condoms act as a physical barrier that stops sperm from reaching the egg and also reduce STI transmission."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"About how long does human pregnancy typically last, and how many trimesters is it divided into?","options":["About nine months, divided into three trimesters","About six months, divided into two trimesters","About twelve months, divided into four trimesters","About three months, divided into one trimester"],"answer":0,"explain":"Human pregnancy lasts about nine months, split into three trimesters of roughly three months each."}]},{"id":"re-boss","title":"Chapter Guardian: The Origin Engine","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Origin Engine","taunt":"Two ways to make life, a thousand ways to survive -- prove you understand them all!"},"story":"Deep beneath the garden lies the Origin Engine, a machine built from every idea in this chapter -- clones and gametes, flowers and fertilisation, larvae and lifelines. Only a full understanding of how life continues can power it down.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which cell division process is behind asexual reproduction, producing genetically identical offspring?","options":["Mitosis","Meiosis","Fertilisation","Gametogenesis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mitosis produces daughter cells identical to the parent cell, giving genetically identical clones."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which cell division process halves the chromosome number to form gametes?","options":["Meiosis","Mitosis","Budding","Pollination"],"answer":0,"explain":"Meiosis reduces the diploid chromosome number to the haploid number found in gametes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A gardener buries part of a lemon twig in soil until it grows its own roots, then cuts it free from the parent plant. What method is this?","options":["Layering","Grafting","Budding","Cross-pollination"],"answer":0,"explain":"Burying part of a flexible twig until roots develop, then separating it, is the method of layering."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which structure in a flower must receive pollen for fertilisation to eventually occur?","options":["Stigma","Sepal","Filament","Vas deferens"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pollination is the transfer of pollen onto the stigma, which is necessary before fertilisation can happen."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do animals with external fertilisation, like frogs and fish, typically lay far more eggs than animals with internal fertilisation?","options":["Their eggs and young are more exposed to predators and environmental loss, so more eggs are needed to ensure some survive","External fertilisation always damages most eggs before they can develop","They lack ovaries","External fertilisation doubles the chromosome number"],"answer":0,"explain":"Eggs released into water are vulnerable to currents and predators, so producing many eggs improves the odds that some survive."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chromosomes are present in a normal human zygote right after fertilisation?","answer":46,"explain":"The 23 chromosomes from the sperm and 23 from the egg combine to restore the full number of 46."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of these events in flowering-plant reproduction: pollination, pollen tube growth down the style, fertilisation, fruit and seed formation?","options":["Pollination, pollen tube growth, fertilisation, fruit and seed formation","Fertilisation, pollination, fruit and seed formation, pollen tube growth","Fruit and seed formation, pollination, fertilisation, pollen tube growth","Pollen tube growth, fertilisation, pollination, fruit and seed formation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pollen first reaches the stigma (pollination), then grows a tube down the style, fuses with the egg (fertilisation), and finally the ovary and ovules develop into fruit and seeds."}]}]},{"id":"biodiversity9","index":11,"name":"Patterns in Life","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 12 — Diversity and Classification","emoji":"🦋","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"bd1","title":"India's Living Treasure","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"Your expedition drone crash-lands somewhere between the snow-clad Himalayas and the coral reefs of the Andaman Sea, and its sensors go wild over creatures found nowhere else on the planet. Before you can continue the quest, you must map why India's forests and mountains guard such rare treasures.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Biodiversity?","body":"Biodiversity is the immense variety of living organisms found on Earth, from microscopic algae to giant trees, existing in countless forms and habitats — from the Himalayas to the coral reefs of the Andaman Sea. It is essential for keeping nature stable and functioning.","visual":"🌎"},{"title":"Every Organism Has a Job","body":"Microscopic algae in the oceans release most of the oxygen we breathe. Fungi and bacteria decompose fallen leaves and make soil fertile, while birds, bees and bats pollinate flowers, and plants capture sunlight to make food that supports nearly all life.","visual":"🐝"},{"title":"Endemic Species","body":"Species that are restricted to a particular region and are not found naturally anywhere else are called endemic species. The Nilgiri tahr, Lion-tailed macaque, the pitcher plant Nepenthes khasiana, and Neelakurinji are all found only in India.","visual":"🐐"},{"title":"Biodiversity Hotspots","body":"Regions that support a large number of endemic species and have undergone significant habitat loss are known as biodiversity hotspots. India's hotspots include the Western Ghats, Indo-Burma (including North East India), the Himalayas, and Sundaland (including the Nicobar Islands).","visual":"🗺️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A species found naturally only in one particular region and nowhere else on Earth is called a/an ___ species.","options":["endemic","invasive","hybrid","migratory"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter defines endemic species as those restricted to particular regions and not found naturally anywhere else."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of India's biodiversity hotspots mentioned in the chapter?","options":["Amazon Basin","Western Ghats","Himalayas","Sundaland"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter names the Western Ghats, Indo-Burma, the Himalayas, and Sundaland as India's biodiversity hotspots — the Amazon Basin is not mentioned."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Microscopic algae in the oceans play which vital ecological role?","options":["They release most of the oxygen we breathe","They decompose fallen leaves","They pollinate flowers","They provide fur for mammals"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states that microscopic algae in the oceans release most of the oxygen we breathe."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many named biodiversity hotspots of India does the chapter list (Western Ghats, Indo-Burma, the Himalayas, Sundaland)?","answer":4,"explain":"The chapter names exactly four regions as global biodiversity hotspots found in/around India."}]},{"id":"bd2","title":"Why Classify?","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"A tiger, an eagle, and an earthworm are tangled up in your field notes, and you can't decide which pile any of them belongs in. Before you go further into the Atlas, the Keeper of Records challenges you to prove that sorting living things is survival knowledge, not busywork.","teach":[{"title":"Same Animal, Different Groups","body":"The same organism can fit into different groups depending on the criterion chosen — for example, an eagle, a tiger and a leopard can all be grouped together as carnivores based on their eating habits, even though they look very different.","visual":"🦅"},{"title":"Criteria for Classification","body":"Scientists use several characteristics to group organisms: external features, mode of nutrition (autotrophic/heterotrophic), internal structures, cell structure (unicellular/multicellular, prokaryote/eukaryote), ecological role, reproduction, and genetic similarity studied through DNA.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"The Library Analogy","body":"Finding one book in a library where thousands of books are scattered on the floor is nearly impossible without arranging them into subjects and sections. In the same way, classifying Earth's millions of organisms systematically helps us understand them better.","visual":"📚"},{"title":"Why Classification Matters","body":"Biological classification makes the study of organisms organised, reveals similarities and relationships between them, helps identify and name newly discovered organisms, supports biodiversity conservation, and gives scientists worldwide a common system to discuss life.","visual":"🌐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT one of the criteria scientists use to classify organisms, according to the chapter?","options":["Favourite colour of the observer","Mode of nutrition","Cell structure","Ecological role"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists external features, nutrition, internal structures, cell structure, ecological role, reproduction and genetic similarity — not personal preference."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Out of India's roughly 1,300 bird species, how many were recorded in the Pakke Tiger Reserve alone?","answer":300,"explain":"The chapter states scientists have recorded nearly 300 bird species in Pakke, striking given India has about 1,300 in total."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a benefit of biological classification listed in the chapter?","options":["It helps identify and name newly discovered organisms","It eliminates the need for scientific names","It proves all organisms are identical","It replaces the need for conservation efforts"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists identifying and naming newly discovered organisms as one of the benefits of classification."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Mode of nutrition as a classification criterion divides organisms into which two categories?","options":["Autotrophic and heterotrophic","Vertebrate and invertebrate","Prokaryote and eukaryote","Sessile and motile"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists mode of nutrition as autotrophic or heterotrophic."}]},{"id":"bd3","title":"Kingdoms Through Time","icon":"🕰️","kind":"lesson","story":"Step into the Time-Scope and watch scientists across the centuries argue over how to sort life — from Aristotle's simple land-water-air guesses to Whittaker's five-kingdom masterpiece. Each wrong guess left some strange creature, like Amoeba or bacteria, with nowhere to belong.","teach":[{"title":"Aristotle's Artificial System","body":"Around the 4th century BCE, Aristotle grouped animals mainly based on their habitat — land, water and air — and their external appearance. This system had limitations because it relied on easily observable features alone.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Two Kingdom System","body":"In the 18th century, scientists (Linnaeus, 1758) divided all living organisms into Plantae (do not move, make their own food) and Animalia (move, depend on others for food). But this created confusion over where to place Amoeba, Paramecium and bacteria.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Three & Four Kingdom Systems","body":"In 1866, a third kingdom, Protista, was added for unicellular microscopic organisms. Later, in 1938, scientists noticed bacteria lack a true nucleus unlike Amoeba, so bacteria were placed in a separate kingdom, Monera, giving a four kingdom system.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Five Kingdom System","body":"In 1969, Robert H. Whittaker separated Fungi into its own kingdom, since fungi do not move like plants but are heterotrophic, absorbing nutrients rather than making their own food. This gave the five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who proposed the five kingdom classification system?","options":["Robert H. Whittaker","Carolus Linnaeus","Ernst Haeckel","Aristotle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Robert H. Whittaker proposed the five kingdom classification in 1969."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In which year did Whittaker propose the five kingdom classification?","answer":1969,"explain":"The chapter's timeline places Whittaker's five kingdom system in 1969."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Aristotle classified animals mainly on the basis of:","options":["Habitat (land, water, air) and external appearance","DNA similarity","Mode of nutrition","Presence of a notochord"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aristotle grouped animals by their habitat and external appearance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why was Kingdom Monera separated from Protista?","options":["Monera organisms lack a true, membrane-bound nucleus while Protista have one","Monera organisms are multicellular","Monera organisms can photosynthesise while Protista cannot","Monera organisms have a backbone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scientists noticed Amoeba has a true (membrane-bound) nucleus but bacteria do not, so bacteria were placed in the separate kingdom Monera."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The two kingdom system, introduced in the 18th century, divided all living things into:","options":["Plantae and Animalia","Monera and Protista","Vertebrata and Invertebrata","Prokaryotes and Eukaryotes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two kingdom system divided all organisms into Plantae and Animalia."}]},{"id":"bd4","title":"Monera, Protista & Fungi","icon":"🦠","kind":"lesson","story":"You shrink down to the size of a water droplet and land inside a hay infusion teeming with invisible life. Bacteria, amoebas and thread-like fungi drift past — each one built completely differently, and each one demanding its own kingdom.","teach":[{"title":"Kingdom Monera","body":"Bacteria and cyanobacteria are unicellular prokaryotes (no true nucleus) grouped under Monera. They are found everywhere, including soil, water, hot springs and inside human and animal bodies. Some are harmful pathogens, but many are useful, like Lactobacillus and Rhizobium, and cyanobacteria are autotrophic decomposers.","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"Kingdom Protista","body":"All single-celled eukaryotes, with or without a cellulose cell wall, are grouped under Protista — microscopic and highly diverse organisms such as Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena and Chlamydomonas. Some are autotrophic and others heterotrophic, and they are an important link in aquatic food chains.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Kingdom Fungi","body":"Fungi are mostly multicellular eukaryotes with cell walls made of chitin. They do not make their own food, instead absorbing nutrients from dead or decaying matter through thread-like filaments called mycelium. Most fungi are saprophytic decomposers, though some are symbiotic or parasitic; examples include yeast, mushroom, Aspergillus and Penicillium.","visual":"🍄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bacteria and cyanobacteria are grouped under Kingdom Monera mainly because they are:","options":["Unicellular prokaryotes","Multicellular eukaryotes","Unicellular eukaryotes with cell walls","Heterotrophic vertebrates"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bacteria and cyanobacteria are single-celled prokaryotes, which is why they are grouped under Monera."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these organisms belongs to Kingdom Protista?","options":["Amoeba","Mushroom","Rhizobium","Fern"],"answer":0,"explain":"Amoeba is a unicellular eukaryote and is listed as a member of Kingdom Protista."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Fungal cell walls are mainly made up of:","options":["Chitin","Cellulose","Calcium carbonate","Keratin"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states fungal cell walls are made of chitin, unlike plant cell walls which are made of cellulose."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is yeast, though unicellular, classified under Kingdom Fungi rather than Protista?","options":["Because its cell wall is made of chitin","Because it can photosynthesise","Because it has no nucleus","Because it lives only in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes yeast is unicellular but is placed under Fungi because its cell wall is made up of chitin."}]},{"id":"bd5","title":"Kingdom Plantae — Five Classes","icon":"🌿","kind":"lesson","story":"Climbing out of a pond choked with green slime, you trace the plant kingdom's slow march onto dry land — from simple algae, to moss that still can't leave the water, to towering pines and flowering trees that finally conquered it.","teach":[{"title":"Thallophyta — Body Without Divisions","body":"Thallophytes are among the simplest plants, mostly found in water or moist places. Their body is an undifferentiated thallus that allows direct exchange of gases, nutrients and water with the surroundings. Spirogyra is an example.","visual":"🟢"},{"title":"Bryophyta — First Steps on Land","body":"Bryophytes, such as Marchantia and moss, show an important shift from water to land. They have root-like rhizoids and simple stem-like and leaf-like structures, but still need water for reproduction, so they are called the 'amphibians' of the plant kingdom.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"Pteridophyta — True Roots, Stems & Leaves","body":"Pteridophytes, such as ferns, possess true roots, stems and leaves along with vascular tissue — xylem and phloem — that transport water and food. They still require water for reproduction and do not produce seeds.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Gymnosperms — Naked Seeds","body":"Gymnosperms, such as pines and cycads, are well-adapted to cold and dry regions, with needle-like or scale-like leaves that reduce water loss. They produce seeds that are not enclosed in fruits and are often exposed on cones, and do not need water for fertilisation.","visual":"🌲"},{"title":"Angiosperms — Flowers & Fruits","body":"Angiosperms, or flowering plants, have the most complex body organisation. They produce flowers, which attract pollinators, and fruits, which help disperse seeds — making them the most diverse plant group on Earth.","visual":"🌸"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which class of Plantae has a simple, undifferentiated body called a thallus, as seen in Spirogyra?","options":["Thallophyta","Bryophyta","Pteridophyta","Angiosperm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thallophytes have a simple thallus body and Spirogyra is given as an example."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bryophytes are called the 'amphibians of the plant kingdom' because:","options":["They need water for reproduction even though they live on land","They can live entirely underwater","They have true roots and vascular tissue","They produce seeds enclosed in fruits"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bryophytes represent the shift to land but still require water for reproduction, like amphibians."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ferns (Pteridophyta) differ from bryophytes mainly because ferns have:","options":["True roots, stems, leaves and vascular tissue (xylem and phloem)","Flowers and fruits","A single-celled thallus body","Seeds enclosed in fruit"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pteridophytes possess true roots, stems, leaves and vascular tissue, unlike bryophytes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Gymnosperm seeds are described as 'naked' because:","options":["They are not enclosed in fruits and are exposed on cones","They lack a seed coat entirely","They float freely in water","They have no embryo"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gymnosperm seeds are not enclosed in fruit and are often exposed on cones."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which plant group is described as the most diverse on Earth, producing both flowers and fruits?","options":["Angiosperms","Gymnosperms","Pteridophyta","Thallophyta"],"answer":0,"explain":"Angiosperms produce flowers and fruits and are the most diverse plant group on Earth."}]},{"id":"bd6","title":"Animal Kingdom & the Hierarchy","icon":"🐅","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the forest, you must sort a chaotic parade of animals — sponges, worms, insects, and a tiger — using nothing but one hidden rod called the notochord. Then you learn the secret address system that lets any scientist on Earth find any species instantly.","teach":[{"title":"Chordates vs Non-Chordates","body":"Animals are classified based on the presence or absence of a notochord, a flexible rod-shaped support structure. Non-chordata (invertebrates) lack it, while chordata possess it at some point in life. Chordata is further divided into protochordata and vertebrata.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"Invertebrate Phyla — Rising Complexity","body":"From simple to complex: Porifera (sponges, no true tissues), Cnidaria (Hydra, jellyfish — true tissues), Platyhelminthes (flatworms — bilateral symmetry), Nematoda (roundworms — two body openings), Annelida (earthworms — segmented with a body cavity), Arthropoda (insects, crabs, spiders — jointed legs and exoskeleton), Mollusca (snails, octopuses — soft bodies), and Echinodermata (starfish — spiny internal skeleton).","visual":"🦀"},{"title":"Vertebrates — Animals with a Backbone","body":"Vertebrates possess a vertebral column that supports the body and protects organs like the brain and spinal cord. They are grouped into five classes — fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals — based on habitat use, body covering, and reproduction.","visual":"🐟"},{"title":"The Hierarchy of Classification","body":"Classification works like an address, moving from broad to specific: Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species. At each lower level, organisms share more common features, and every lower group is part of the group above it.","visual":"🏠"},{"title":"Binomial Nomenclature","body":"Carolus Linnaeus introduced binomial nomenclature, a universal two-part scientific naming system written in Latin: genus (capitalised) followed by species (lowercase), always italicised or underlined. For example, the tiger is Panthera tigris and mango is Mangifera indica.","visual":"🏷️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Animals are divided into chordata and non-chordata based on the presence or absence of a:","options":["Notochord","Exoskeleton","Vertebral column only","Cell wall"],"answer":0,"explain":"The presence or absence of a notochord is the major criterion separating chordates from non-chordates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which invertebrate phylum is known for jointed appendages and a hard external skeleton, such as insects and crabs?","options":["Arthropoda","Porifera","Annelida","Echinodermata"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arthropoda (arthro = limbs, poda = appendages) includes insects, crabs and spiders with a hard exoskeleton."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the taxonomic hierarchy, which sequence correctly goes from broadest to most specific?","options":["Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species","Species, Genus, Family, Order, Class, Phylum, Kingdom","Phylum, Kingdom, Order, Class, Genus, Family, Species","Kingdom, Class, Phylum, Family, Order, Species, Genus"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists the hierarchy as Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the scientific name Panthera tigris, 'Panthera' represents the:","options":["Genus","Species","Family","Kingdom"],"answer":0,"explain":"Panthera is the genus name; tigris is the species name."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Vertebrates are divided into fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals based on:","options":["Habitat use, body covering and reproduction","Presence or absence of a notochord","Mode of nutrition only","Cell type"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states vertebrates are classified into these five groups based on broad patterns of habitat use, body covering and reproduction."}]},{"id":"bd-boss","title":"The Final Sorting","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The kingdoms have gathered their creatures for one last test. Prove you can sort life itself — from a single bacterium to a striped tiger — or the Atlas stays locked.","boss":{"name":"The Great Unsorted","taunt":"Name me, group me, or lose to the chaos of unclassified life!"},"teach":[{"title":"Chapter Recap","body":"Biodiversity is Earth's vast variety of life, concentrated in hotspots like the Western Ghats and the Himalayas. Scientists classify organisms using criteria such as cell structure and nutrition, arriving at Whittaker's five kingdoms — Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia — organised in a Kingdom-to-Species hierarchy and named with binomial nomenclature.","visual":"🗝️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A biodiversity hotspot is a region that has:","options":["A large number of endemic species and significant habitat loss","No endemic species at all","Only aquatic organisms","The coldest climate on Earth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hotspots support a large number of endemic species and have undergone significant habitat loss."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many kingdoms are there in Whittaker's classification system?","answer":5,"explain":"Whittaker's system has five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which criterion best separates Kingdom Monera from Kingdom Protista?","options":["Presence or absence of a true, membrane-bound nucleus","Presence or absence of chlorophyll","Body size","Number of legs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Monera organisms lack a true nucleus while Protista have a membrane-bound nucleus."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which plant class was the first to develop true vascular tissue (xylem and phloem)?","options":["Pteridophyta","Thallophyta","Bryophyta","Gymnosperm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pteridophytes were the first group described with true vascular tissue for transporting water and food."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which structure determines whether an animal is classified as chordata or non-chordata?","options":["Notochord","Wings","Fur","Shell"],"answer":0,"explain":"The presence or absence of a notochord is the defining criterion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In binomial nomenclature, the correct way to write a scientific name is:","options":["Genus capitalised + species lowercase, both italicised, e.g. Panthera tigris","Both words in all capitals","Species name first, then genus, with no italics","Only common names written in Latin"],"answer":0,"explain":"The genus name is capitalised, the species name is lowercase, and both are italicised (or underlined when handwritten)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main purpose of biological classification, according to the chapter?","options":["To systematically organise, study and understand the relationships among Earth's vast diversity of organisms","To rename every organism after its discoverer","To prove that all organisms evolved identically","To eliminate the need for conservation efforts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Classification is a scientific system for organising the study of living diversity and understanding relationships between organisms."}]}]},{"id":"earthsystem9","index":12,"name":"Earth as a System","subtitle":"Exploration Chapter 13 — Energy, Matter, and Life","emoji":"🌍","palette":{"from":"#2dd4bf","to":"#134e4a"},"lessons":[{"id":"es1","title":"Earth's Five Spheres","icon":"🌐","kind":"lesson","story":"Your expedition ship drops anchor at the edge of five overlapping worlds — rock, water, ice, air, and life — all woven into one planet. Before you can chart Earth as a single system, you must name each sphere and see how a nudge in one ripples through all the rest.","teach":[{"title":"Geosphere","body":"The geosphere is made of solid rocks, soil, landforms — like the Deccan plateau and the Thar desert — and Earth's interior. It forms the solid foundation that the other spheres rest on and interact with.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Hydrosphere","body":"The hydrosphere is liquid water in the form of surface water — oceans, rivers (like the Ganga–Brahmaputra river system), lakes — and groundwater. It stores and moves water and dissolved nutrients across the planet.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Cryosphere","body":"The cryosphere is the solid form of water: ice and snow, such as the Himalayan glaciers, snow in Ladakh, and the polar ice caps. It locks away huge amounts of frozen fresh water.","visual":"❄️"},{"title":"Atmosphere","body":"The atmosphere is the air surrounding the Earth that we breathe, including the cleaner air found in mountains and forests. It is held in place by Earth's gravity and shapes weather and climate.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Biosphere","body":"The biosphere includes all living organisms and their habitats — mangroves, forests, farms, ocean plankton and coral reefs. Living things constantly exchange matter and energy with the other spheres.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"One Interconnected System","body":"A disturbance in one sphere changes the others. Less snowfall (cryosphere) means less water in a lake (hydrosphere) and less grass for grazing animals (biosphere); a warmer Arabian Sea disrupts the southwest monsoon, melts glaciers, raises sea levels, and threatens habitats — all spheres acting as one connected system.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which sphere includes the Himalayan glaciers, snow in Ladakh, and the polar ice caps?","options":["Cryosphere","Geosphere","Hydrosphere","Atmosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cryosphere is the solid form of water — ice and snow — found in glaciers and polar ice caps."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Ganga–Brahmaputra river system is an example of which sphere?","options":["Hydrosphere","Cryosphere","Geosphere","Biosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rivers, lakes, oceans and groundwater together make up the hydrosphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Coral reefs and ocean plankton are part of which sphere?","options":["Biosphere","Hydrosphere","Cryosphere","Atmosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The biosphere is made up of all living organisms and their habitats, including coral reefs and plankton."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What did Activity 13.1 in the chapter show about Earth's spheres?","options":["A disturbance in one sphere can lead to changes in the others","Each sphere works completely independently of the others","Only the atmosphere is capable of affecting other spheres","Spheres only interact with each other during natural disasters"],"answer":0,"explain":"Less snowfall (cryosphere) can reduce a lake's water level (hydrosphere) and the grass available for grazing (biosphere), showing the spheres are interconnected."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Deccan plateau and the Thar desert are examples of features of which sphere?","options":["Geosphere","Hydrosphere","Cryosphere","Biosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The geosphere consists of solid rocks, soil, landforms and Earth's interior."}]},{"id":"es2","title":"Sunlight, Insolation and Albedo","icon":"☀️","kind":"lesson","story":"In the solar observatory deck, your instruments show that not every patch of Earth catches the same sunlight. To understand why deserts scorch while poles freeze, you must measure how radiation travels, arrives, and gets reflected differently across the globe.","teach":[{"title":"The Electromagnetic Spectrum","body":"Solar radiation reaches Earth as electromagnetic waves, which travel through a vacuum at the speed of light (3 × 10⁸ m/s), unlike sound waves which need a medium. The EM spectrum ranges from high-frequency, short-wavelength gamma rays and X-rays to low-frequency, long-wavelength infrared and radio waves.","visual":"🌈"},{"title":"Radiation That Reaches Earth","body":"About 99% of the Sun's energy reaching Earth falls within the ultraviolet (UV), visible, and infrared (IR) ranges. Gamma rays and X-rays are mostly filtered out by the upper atmosphere, UV is largely absorbed by the ozone layer, visible light powers photosynthesis, and infrared warms the surface, which then re-radiates heat that greenhouse gases partly trap.","visual":"🔆"},{"title":"Solar Constant and Insolation","body":"The solar constant is the average solar energy received per unit area at the top of Earth's atmosphere, about 1.4 kW/m² (1400 J/s/m²), before any atmospheric absorption or scattering. Insolation is the amount that actually reaches Earth's surface — lower than the solar constant, about 1 kW/m² under clear sky conditions.","visual":"📡"},{"title":"Albedo","body":"Albedo is the fraction of solar radiation reflected by a surface. High-albedo surfaces like snow (0.80–0.90) and ice (0.50–0.70) stay cool because they reflect most incoming light, while low-albedo surfaces like black soil and ocean water absorb more radiation and warm up faster.","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"Latitude and Earth's Spherical Shape","body":"Because Earth is spherical, sunlight striking the equator is concentrated over a small area, while the same amount of sunlight spreads over a much larger area near the poles. This is the root cause of uneven heating — equatorial regions stay warm year-round while polar regions stay much colder.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Example 13.1 from the chapter: if insolation on Earth's surface is 1 kW/m², how many joules of energy fall on a 1 m² area in one hour?","answer":3600000,"explain":"E = intensity × area × time = 1000 J/s/m² × 1 m² × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is albedo?","options":["The fraction of solar radiation reflected by a surface","The total energy absorbed by the atmosphere each day","The angle at which sunlight strikes a surface","The temperature difference between land and water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Albedo comes from the Latin word for whiteness and measures how much sunlight a surface reflects."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which surface has the highest albedo, according to Table 13.1 in the chapter?","options":["Snow","Black soil","Ocean water","Crushed rock"],"answer":0,"explain":"Snow has an albedo of about 0.80–0.90, the highest of the materials listed, which keeps polar regions very cold."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do equatorial regions of Earth stay warmer than polar regions?","options":["Because Earth is spherical, sunlight is concentrated over a smaller area at the equator than at the poles","Because the equator is physically closer to the Sun than the poles","Because the poles receive no sunlight at any time of year","Because the atmosphere is thinner at the equator than at the poles"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Sun's radiation falling on the equatorial region is concentrated over a smaller area, while it spreads over a larger area near the poles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Approximately what percentage of the Sun's energy reaching Earth falls within the UV, visible, and infrared range?","options":["About 99%","About 50%","About 10%","About 75%"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states that about 99% of the Sun's energy reaching Earth is concentrated in the UV, visible, and infrared wavelengths."}]},{"id":"es3","title":"Atmosphere, Winds and Ocean Currents","icon":"🌬️","kind":"lesson","story":"You climb aboard a high-altitude glider to ride the currents of air and water that girdle the planet. From the storm-tossed troposphere to the calm ozone shield above, and out across swirling ocean gyres, you'll trace how uneven heating sets the whole planet in motion.","teach":[{"title":"Composition and Layered Structure","body":"The atmosphere is held in place by Earth's gravity and consists mainly of nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%), along with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapour and other gases. It has a layered structure that helps explain key weather patterns.","visual":"🌫️"},{"title":"The Troposphere","body":"Nearly all weather phenomena occur in the troposphere (0–12 km, heated from Earth's surface), where temperature decreases with height at about 6.5 °C per km. As warm air rises, it drives winds and storms; the troposphere is tallest above the equator and shortest above the poles.","visual":"⛈️"},{"title":"The Stratosphere and Ozone Layer","body":"In the stratosphere (12–50 km), ozone absorbs UV rays and heats the layer, so temperature rises with height. This calms the layer by limiting vertical mixing of air, which keeps weather confined to the troposphere below.","visual":"🛡️"},{"title":"The Greenhouse Effect","body":"Earth's surface absorbs sunlight and re-radiates it as infrared heat; greenhouse gases like CO2, CH4 and water vapour trap part of this outgoing heat, keeping Earth warm enough for life. Without an atmosphere Earth would be far too cold, but excess CO2 from human activity strengthens this effect and drives global warming — it's why Venus is hotter than Mercury despite being farther from the Sun.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Local Winds: Valley and Mountain Breezes","body":"By day, sun-facing mountain slopes heat faster than the valley floor, so warm air rises there and cooler valley air flows up to replace it — a valley breeze. After sunset, slopes cool faster than the valley floor, so cool dense air sinks back down into the valley — a mountain breeze, common in places like Shimla and Dehradun.","visual":"⛰️"},{"title":"Planetary Winds","body":"Uneven heating between equator and poles creates pressure belts: an equatorial low, sub-tropical highs near 30°N/S, sub-polar lows near 60°N/S, and polar highs near 90°N/S. Earth's rotation deflects these winds to the right in the Northern Hemisphere and to the left in the Southern Hemisphere, so they follow curved rather than straight paths.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Ocean Currents","body":"Planetary winds drag ocean surface water through friction, while temperature, salinity differences and Earth's rotation shape the flow into large circular gyres — clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere, counter-clockwise in the Southern. The North Atlantic Drift, an extension of the Gulf Stream, carries warm water to north-western Europe and keeps its ports ice-free even in winter.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which atmospheric layer does nearly all weather — like winds, storms and rainfall — occur?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The troposphere, heated from Earth's surface, is where nearly all weather phenomena take place."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does temperature increase with height in the stratosphere?","options":["Ozone in this layer absorbs UV radiation and heats it","It is physically closer to the Sun than the troposphere","Greenhouse gases are most concentrated in the stratosphere","Water vapour condenses heavily in this layer"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ozone layer in the stratosphere absorbs UV rays, warming the layer so temperature rises with altitude."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What causes a valley breeze during the day?","options":["Sun-heated mountain slopes create rising warm air, drawing cooler air up from the valley","Cold air from the mountain peak sinks straight down into the valley","The valley floor heats up faster than the surrounding slopes","Clouds block sunlight from reaching the mountain slopes"],"answer":0,"explain":"During the day, sun-facing slopes heat faster than the valley floor; the rising warm air over the slopes pulls cooler valley air upward."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Northern Hemisphere, planetary winds are deflected in which direction due to Earth's rotation?","options":["To the right","To the left","Straight from high to low pressure with no deflection","Only vertically, never horizontally"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states winds are deflected to the right in the Northern Hemisphere and to the left in the Southern Hemisphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What keeps many north-western European ports ice-free even in winter?","options":["The North Atlantic Drift, an extension of the Gulf Stream, carrying warm water there","Strong winds blowing continuously from the Thar desert","A permanently high-albedo layer of Arctic snow near Europe","The equatorial low pressure belt extending up to Europe"],"answer":0,"explain":"The North Atlantic Drift transports warm equatorial water toward north-western Europe, moderating the climate of its ports."}]},{"id":"es4","title":"Biogeochemical Cycles","icon":"🔄","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Earth-system control room, glowing pipelines show water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen looping endlessly between air, ocean, soil and living things. Follow one atom's journey and you'll understand how the whole planet keeps its nutrients in circulation.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Biogeochemical Cycle?","body":"A biogeochemical cycle is the cyclic movement of matter and energy between the non-living (abiotic) components — air, water, soil, rocks — and the living (biotic) components of Earth. This recycling keeps essential nutrients like carbon, nitrogen and oxygen available to support life.","visual":"♻️"},{"title":"The Water Cycle","body":"Water evaporates from oceans, rivers and lakes, condenses into clouds, and returns as precipitation (rain, hail or snow). Some water runs off to the ocean, some infiltrates soil and rock as groundwater, and this cycle links the cryosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere, geosphere and biosphere together.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"The Carbon Cycle: Fast Loop","body":"Over days to years, plants use photosynthesis to convert atmospheric CO2 into glucose using sunlight. CO2 returns to the atmosphere through respiration by plants and animals, and through decomposition when organisms die.","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"The Carbon Cycle: Slow Loop","body":"Over millions of years, buried dead plants and animals become fossil fuels like coal, oil and gas; burning these releases stored carbon back as CO2 on a much shorter timescale. The ocean also exchanges CO2 with the atmosphere, forming carbonate and bicarbonate ions used by phytoplankton and shell-forming organisms, some of which get stored on the ocean floor.","visual":"🛢️"},{"title":"The Nitrogen Cycle","body":"Nitrogen-fixing bacteria like Rhizobium and Azotobacter convert atmospheric N2 into ammonia (nitrogen fixation); Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter convert this into nitrite and then nitrate (nitrification); plants assimilate these compounds; decomposers return nitrogen to soil as ammonia when organisms die (ammonification); and denitrifying bacteria like Pseudomonas convert nitrates back into N2 gas (denitrification).","visual":"🦠"},{"title":"The Oxygen Cycle","body":"Respiration and combustion consume atmospheric oxygen and release CO2, while photosynthesis restores oxygen using sunlight, water and CO2 to make glucose. This balance between consumption and production circulates oxygen between the atmosphere, land, oceans and living organisms.","visual":"🫁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a biogeochemical cycle?","options":["The cyclic movement of matter and energy between living and non-living components of Earth","A one-way flow of energy from the Sun straight to living organisms","The process by which rocks slowly turn into soil over time","The movement of water alone through the atmosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biogeochemical cycles move matter and energy between abiotic (air, water, soil, rock) and biotic (living) components."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which bacteria, found in the root nodules of legumes, convert atmospheric nitrogen gas into ammonia?","options":["Rhizobium","Nitrosomonas","Nitrobacter","Pseudomonas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rhizobium in legume root nodules (and Azotobacter in soil) carry out nitrogen fixation, converting N2 into ammonia."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the fast carbon cycle, how do plants remove CO2 from the atmosphere?","options":["Photosynthesis","Respiration","Combustion of fossil fuels","Denitrification"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plants use sunlight to convert atmospheric CO2 into glucose through photosynthesis."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Over millions of years, buried dead plants and animals can be converted into what, as part of the slow carbon cycle?","options":["Fossil fuels like coal, oil and gas","Nitrogen gas","Ozone in the stratosphere","Groundwater"],"answer":0,"explain":"The slow carbon cycle, operating over millions of years, buries organic matter and converts it into fossil fuels."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Atmospheric oxygen levels are mainly restored by which process?","options":["Photosynthesis","Respiration","Combustion of fuels","Decomposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plants restore oxygen through photosynthesis, using sunlight, water and CO2 to form glucose and release O2."}]},{"id":"es5","title":"Human Impact and Restoring Balance","icon":"🌆","kind":"lesson","story":"Warning lights flash across the Earth-system control room — the delicate balance you've mapped is being pushed off course. To complete your expedition, you must diagnose what's disrupting the system and find out how it can still be restored.","teach":[{"title":"Rising CO2 and the Keeling Curve","body":"Human activities like burning fossil fuels and deforestation have raised atmospheric CO2 by about 35% since 1960 — from 315 ppm to 420 ppm, as shown by the Keeling curve — an unprecedented rise in human history. This intensifies the greenhouse effect, driving global warming, glacier melt and sea level rise.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Ocean Acidification","body":"Excess atmospheric CO2 increases how much CO2 the ocean absorbs, making seawater more acidic and threatening tiny plankton and coral reefs. Ironically, warmer ocean water reduces the ocean's ability to absorb CO2, weakening it as a carbon sink.","visual":"🪸"},{"title":"Eutrophication","body":"Overuse of nitrogen fertilisers in agriculture washes excess nitrates into rivers and lakes, triggering widespread algal blooms called eutrophication. These blooms deplete oxygen in the water and kill fish, threatening water bodies and coastal fisheries.","visual":"🟢"},{"title":"Deforestation's Ripple Effects","body":"Clearing forests reduces photosynthesis and transpiration, which can lower local rainfall, and alters surface albedo. Without tree roots to hold soil together, erosion increases, and habitat loss over time reduces biodiversity.","visual":"🪓"},{"title":"The Ozone Hole and the Montreal Protocol","body":"Human-made chemicals called CFCs, used in refrigerators and aerosols, destroyed stratospheric ozone over Antarctica in the late 20th century, forming the ozone hole. The global Montreal Protocol reduced CFC use, and the ozone layer is now slowly recovering — a success story of international cooperation.","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Restoring the Balance","body":"Conserving energy, switching to renewable sources like solar and wind, planting trees, saving water, and practising sustainable farming can help restore Earth's natural balance. India's Mission LiFE (Lifestyle for Environment) promotes mindful, eco-friendly individual and community habits toward this goal.","visual":"🌱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By roughly how much has atmospheric CO2 risen since 1960, according to the Keeling curve in the chapter?","options":["About 35% (315 ppm to 420 ppm)","About 5% (400 ppm to 420 ppm)","About 90% (100 ppm to 190 ppm)","It has stayed roughly the same"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states CO2 rose from about 315 ppm in 1960 to about 420 ppm today, a roughly 35% increase."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What mainly causes eutrophication in rivers and lakes?","options":["Excess nitrogen fertiliser runoff triggering algal blooms that deplete oxygen","Volcanic ash settling onto the surface of lakes","Overfishing that removes algae-eating fish species","Excess ozone building up in the stratosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Overuse of fertilisers adds excess nitrates to water bodies, causing algal blooms that deplete oxygen and kill fish."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which international agreement successfully reduced CFCs and is helping the ozone layer recover?","options":["The Montreal Protocol","The Kyoto Protocol","The Paris Agreement","Mission LiFE"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Montreal Protocol reduced the use of ozone-destroying CFCs, and the ozone layer is now slowly recovering."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a consequence of deforestation described in the chapter?","options":["Reduced local rainfall due to less transpiration","Increased thickness of the stratospheric ozone layer","Higher albedo everywhere, cooling the local climate","Increased groundwater recharge from more infiltration"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clearing forests reduces photosynthesis and transpiration, which can lead to a decline in local rainfall."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What effect does excess atmospheric CO2 have on ocean water?","options":["It makes seawater more acidic by increasing CO2 absorption","It makes seawater more alkaline by removing dissolved CO2","It has no measurable effect on ocean chemistry","It only changes ocean temperature, never its chemistry"],"answer":0,"explain":"Excess atmospheric CO2 increases ocean absorption of CO2, which makes seawater more acidic and threatens plankton and coral reefs."}]},{"id":"es-boss","title":"Final Expedition: The System Keeper","icon":"🌏","kind":"boss","story":"You've traced energy from the Sun's core to Earth's ice caps, and matter through the carbon, nitrogen, water and oxygen cycles. At the edge of your journey stands the System Keeper — guardian of Earth's delicate balance — who will test whether you truly understand Earth as one connected system. This is the final trial of your entire Grade 9 Science expedition.","teach":[{"title":"The Earth System, At a Glance","body":"Solar radiation is Earth's primary energy source; uneven heating across latitudes drives winds, ocean currents, and the weather confined to the troposphere. Matter cycles continuously between the biotic and abiotic world through the water, carbon, nitrogen and oxygen cycles, sustaining life and regulating climate — and human activity is now disturbing that balance.","visual":"🌍"}],"boss":{"name":"The System Keeper","taunt":"You've studied every sphere, every cycle, every current — now prove you can see the whole Earth system at once, or your expedition ends here!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the primary role of biogeochemical cycles in an ecosystem?","options":["To recycle essential nutrients between biotic and abiotic components","To provide food directly to all organisms","To create new elements for use by living things","To remove pollutants and toxins from organisms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biogeochemical cycles recycle essential nutrients like carbon, nitrogen and oxygen between living and non-living components, keeping them available for life."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is primarily responsible for the warming of Earth's surface?","options":["The Earth's surface absorbs solar radiation, which is then re-radiated and trapped by greenhouse gases","Solar radiation is immediately absorbed by carbon dioxide, which then releases it as heat","The atmosphere's tiny particles absorb incoming solar radiation, which directly heats the Earth","The Earth's environment is heated only by solar radiation reflected by clouds"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Earth's surface absorbs sunlight and re-radiates it as infrared heat, part of which greenhouse gases trap, warming the planet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer of the atmosphere is responsible for most weather phenomena like winds, storms and rainfall?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Exosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The troposphere, heated from Earth's surface, is where nearly all weather phenomena occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does the fast carbon cycle differ from the slow carbon cycle?","options":["The fast cycle moves carbon through photosynthesis and respiration over days to years, while the slow cycle forms fossil fuels over millions of years","The fast cycle only happens in the ocean, while the slow cycle only happens in forests","Both the fast and slow cycles take millions of years to complete","There is no real difference between the fast and slow carbon cycles"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fast cycle (photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition) operates over days to years, while the slow cycle (fossil fuel formation) takes millions of years."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the chapter, what is the approximate solar constant at the top of Earth's atmosphere, in kW per square metre?","answer":1.4,"explain":"The solar constant is approximately 1.4 kW/m² (about 1400 J/s/m²), measured before any absorption or scattering in the atmosphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does climate change's effect on the water cycle show that Earth's spheres are interconnected?","options":["Melting glaciers, changing rainfall, soil erosion, and effects on crops and fisheries link the cryosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere, geosphere and biosphere together","Only the atmosphere is affected when the water cycle changes","The spheres operate completely independently of the water cycle","Only the biosphere is affected by rising atmospheric CO2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter shows climate change disturbing the water cycle ripples through the cryosphere (glaciers), hydrosphere (rivers, oceans), atmosphere (moisture), geosphere (soil erosion) and biosphere (crops, fisheries) together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Keeling curve in the chapter, what happened to atmospheric CO2 concentration between 1960 and 2025?","options":["It rose from about 315 ppm to about 420 ppm","It fell from about 420 ppm to about 315 ppm","It stayed roughly constant at 400 ppm","It doubled every single decade"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Keeling curve shows atmospheric CO2 rising from about 315 ppm in 1960 to about 420 ppm by 2025, a roughly 35% increase."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these correctly matches a nitrogen cycle process with what it does?","options":["Nitrification: bacteria convert ammonia into nitrite and then into nitrate","Denitrification: bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen gas into ammonia","Nitrogen fixation: decomposers break down organic matter into ammonia","Ammonification: bacteria convert nitrate back into nitrogen gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nitrification is carried out by bacteria like Nitrosomonas (ammonia to nitrite) and Nitrobacter (nitrite to nitrate)."}]}]}];